        <p>REGULAR READER.</p>
        <p>"dE UNIONISTS &amp; SHOP ASSISTANTS. Wttric's-erl:,o,ur correspondent, "One of, the Oreri 6&gt; is nearer the mark than any of the At 4°lia writers on the question of shop hours. tits.: ranee, down the advertising columns of :-"abour,Gazette," and a tour of the town tlig4-,Saturday night at 11.30, will give the 4iiiteg„uilli man sufficient food for reflection to ',llbarl for many days. lety a truly remarkable thing that the 'll traPle who are demanding "recognition" a,ir several works, refuse absolutely to ilveZige the real and vital claims of the 4,The,.,,11011 assistants in' Warrington. who mass at the strike meetings to !tie',7laarktible war-cry of " Buttons up!" are late :TY Men Who could solve this problem of 7ing, if they would just reflect and see u,ialisra is not the good of " the indivi- stab: hut "the greatest good of the greatest l'lo th myself being one of the number." hour ese , wearers of buttons carry their eight- they :4111Paign into the work of others? Do 04tijaileavour to give the shop assistant a t(,) lock the door at a reasonable hour? -11`ler how many of them-honestly refuse after ten on a Saturday night;: or how insist that the good wife's shopping is °II Saturday afternoon? It is as morally to shop late as it is for the employer oPen late. • But it is the self-evident t are so difficult to eradicate. Discover ' injustice and shout it abroad at Bridge ;alai a strike immediately results. But 4i'3 shop assistant may go on serving his trade unionist until the small hours of 'ing, and all the sympathy he gets is 411 Y, which is as hollow, as it is aggra-</p>
        <p>gl'eatest tyrants are often those who pro- their rights the loudest. ' NOT A SIIOP ASSISTANT.</p>
        <p>4. read with interest the letter of j`n , "One e Workers" in last week's "E • • "Ita "liter appeared to think that, shop 1 ass_iastt: ;1' tl,ll4ll:fter working frosin 11 to 18 hours ‘on S , ~t thas many do, have so much energy left 411? a-"Y d rn !, omuchot know how to .the- theirlway 'home. My exPerience is that i,.!nese long hours the assistant is only tool • jag 'p'awl home and get to bed, without sta ' 4:ltilet oy. Prove the moral tone of the streets r 1 great barrier to the closing of shas on Saturday is the Market, being allowed. , illeir—li °Pen until after-midnight and work 1 grier assistants, mostly; young girls, to this late °ll`,a, • It' is quite time that religious bodies, , tit tlllsed he labour, B:c., brought preesure to beari ' u`tflair .I)owers thilt be to remedy this state o 1, 0.41:' and close the Market not later than ' bers n°4 Wednesday and 10 p.m. on Saturday. ~ ro Shop this - 14-1 after tl • hour do so- from th 11ROtih t to essuess not necessity. I have ye t) ••• the . . . , t ~, ,tair-tninded man who will say that 10 il3•••Bl:2aturday is not plenty late enough for "1) or market to be open. • EARLIER. ,</p>
        <p>Before Mr. R. T. Fairelough (in the chair), Alderman Tinnion,, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, J. Upson and T. Pemberton. Turning Over a New Leaf.—Benjamin Woolley was charged with assaulting his wife, Maud, of 61; Cartwright-street. —The evidence showed that on several occasions - the prisoner had deserted his wife, and that he went to her house and ,caused trouble.—Prisoner said he was sorry and promised to turn over a,new leaf: The case was adjourned for a month. MONDAY. •</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor. (in the chair), Aldermen Tinnion, James Evans and Bolton, Pr. Joseph, Councillor Wilkinson,. Messrs. S. Wright, W.</p>
        <p>Roberts, T. Locker and G. Pilling. Amateur' Jeweller.--Patrick Richard Moran, 15, Ship-yard, described as a loiterer, was charged with being a convicted thief, loitering in a passage off Brown-street, on Saturday, with intent to commit a felony. He pleaded guilty.— The Chief Constable said the case was a repeti- tion of what, the prisoner did every time he was out of prison. Detective Green observed him stop a man named James Sankey, to whom he offered a ring for sale. When the officer came on the scene prisoner exclaimed "It's a fair cop, this time." Moran told Mr. Sankey that he had found the ring in Manchester, that it was worth 905., and Would let him have it for half-a-crown. "The real history of the ring," said Mr. Nicholls, "is that it was bought in the Market for 40." Prisoner had a large number of pre- vious convictions, Mostly of three months each. —He was again sent to prison for three months; the maximum penalty. - TUESDAY. Before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair), Air. C. Jackson Holmes and Mr. J. Rymer</p>
        <p>Young. "How Do!"--The magistrates were greeted r • with several "How do's!" from George Postle- thwaite, 8, Morris's-court, who, attired in blankets, cut a curions figure when he was brought forward to answer a charge of drunk and disorderly. Prisoner persisted in saying "How Do!" but the Chief said this was to sham madnesi in order to deceive the police. He also stated that near the place where prisoner was arrested two houses had been broken into, and they had every reason to believe that prisoner was responsible for them. Sergeant Holland saw the- man in the cell that morning, and pri- soner, who seemed quite rationaVsaid : "I will tell you all about it if you give Me a cigarette." The officer -went out, and there being no cigar- ette forthcoming prisoner stripped himself of his clothes, and shammed the madness. He openly- boasted thatele "did" the police -in a similar Way some time ago, and he thought he would do, it again. -"I think," added the Chief, "he is as sane as I am, and that is saying a good deal."—Prisoner was remanded, and on Thursday Was sent to prison for a month on the charge of being drunk and disorderly.—The Chief Constable said he was satisfied the man knew nothing about the house broken into. " Minding the Scales."—Mabel Alice Kelley, a stranger to the town, was charged with stealing a pair of scales, value .11 15.,. from the shop of Mr. Tickle, 9, Scotland-road.—The Chief Con- stable said the scales were missed at 10.5 the previous night. At 11.15 Mr. Tickle saw the woman carrying something under her shawl in Winwick-road. He followed her, and in the Market-place he met P.C. Gibbs. As they were talking the prisoner said: "If you don't stop following me about I shall give you into cus- tody," Gibbs searched her and found the scales under her shawl. The woman had said she was sent in a shop to buy cigarettes, and that the man for whom she was 'buying the cigarettes gave her the scales to mind. "She minded them fori n some asking time, for commented, ‘a" rernat t pr isle orn,st a she Chief said e-f Con had had a drop of drink, was remanded for a week.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY. Before Messrs. J. W. Hayward, (in the chair), C. Jackson Holmes and W. T. Williams. Drink's Evil Power.—Annie Humphries, wife of Henry Humphries, puddler, of 58, Hallam- lane, was charged with neglecting her fout youngest children,, and sent to goal for three months. The evidence showed that the children were in a shocking condition and that the mother was constantly under the influence of drink. To satisfy her craving she sold the bed steads for 2s. 6d., and also sold the bed clothing and food. As the result the husband slept on a sofa downstairs and the wife and children on some straw upstairs and found a covering in a piece of sacking.</p>
        <p>Cruelty to a Dog.—William lioslyn, 15, Car- lisle-street, was fined 10s. for cruelty to a dog by tying its four legs together, fastening the string to its collar, and then throwing it into the river. Acting-Sergeant Whitley discovered the dog on the bank of the river in a very exhausted state.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY. Before Mr. A. J. Jolley (in the chair), Dr. 7 - Naden and Mr. J. Rymer, Young. Market Betting.—llarris Myers, 7, Clarendon- .street,- Chorlton-on-Mecllock, Manchester, and a 'boot and store dealer in the Warrington Market, was fined £lO for loitering in the Market for the purpose of betting.—Defendant said he was -going to start book-making at • Manchester on Monday,- and the articles found on him were .new ones. The betting slips related to Tuesday racing, and there was nothing on him relating to races on-Wednegday,'when he was arrested. Placed on Their •Honour.—Four girls were ' -summoned for disorderly behaviour in the , vicinity of Cockhedge Factory.—The girls were 'shouting at two women who would not join the union, but the Chief Constable said that if there, were no more disturbances within an adjourn- ment of eight weeks he would withdraw the sum- mons.—This course was agreed to. Strikers Bound Over.—John William Tones, 10, Chorley-street John Rogerson, 66, Cumberland- street; and Arthur Grayson, 1, Oldham-street, :all strikers, were bound over in the sum of .25 and ordered to pay costs for disorderly con- duct. —The Chief Constable, commenting upon the disorderly crowds who followed men who were being escorted•home, said that 40 men fol- lowed two workmen and threw half bricks at ti em in addition to demolishing all the win- dows and smashing the furniture in the front room of one of the men's houses.</p>
