      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>FARM ANL, 6AttIJEN.</p>
        <p>EASTER LAMB.</p>
        <p>Easter lamb has long ceased to be an exclusive delicacy of the upper classes, says "The Times." The epicure now demands his first joints of sucking blab at Christmas, and although the supply is small for a few weeks, the novelty of lamb for luncheon or dinner .has departed long before Easter Week. The, early lamb differs from most .other unseasonable commodities of the lerder or kitchen in its source of origin in 'that it is a genuine product of the English counties. The system of pastoral farming that places fat lambs on the market before half the flocks in. the kingdom have passed through the lambing Pen, was long peculiar to a district and a single .breed. The counties of Dorset and Somerset had virtually a monopoly of the spring lamb trade, an advantage conferred by their possession of a breed remarkable for its fecundity. The Dorset or Somerset Horn, ~which has a definite history of more than 200 years, in the matter of repro- Auction is the most amenable breed of sheep we possess: Alone of our some 30 varieties it is capable of producing two crops of lambs in the year; but rather than imperil its constitutional , vigour by. imposing upon it the excessive strain of two lambings in the year, the majority of its owners utilise :its exceptional qualities in the production of lamb at a time then it commands the price of an article Out of season.</p>
        <p>Breeders of Dorset Horns are experiencing a satisfactory seascin. Selling has been in progress since the first week of February, and At present about 800 lambs per week are distributed from Dorchester to London, Birmingham, and Cardiff, ' while a few go to Bournemouth, Weymouth, and other seaside towns. The prospects for Easter are good owing to the scarcity and dearness of Mutton, the best lambs making from 1.1. d. to 12d, per lb., and from 365. to 48s. per head for pure Dorset Horns, and to 50s. for cross-breds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ANIMAL DISEASES IN 1913.</head>
        <p>ANIMAL DISEASES IN 1913.</p>
        <p>_ The annual report of. the Assistant Secretary (Animals Division) of the Board of Agriculture: and 'Fisheries states that swine fever was less prevalent during the year than in 1912, the number of outbreaks having fallen from 2,920 to 2,573; a reduction of 347. The position as regards that disease, however, is still very unsatisfac- tory, and the returns do not compare favourably with Those of 1910, which was also a year of un- satisfactory results. In each of the .other ached- -tiled diseases the ieturris c'ornpare favourably with the last year!s.;.There were only to out- breaks of foot-nnil-mouth-disease during 1913, Viz., in November at Westham, East Sussex, and in December at ,Whitwell, near Welwyn, Hert- fordshire. Thanks to the prompt measures taken for isolation and slaughter of ...the animals affected, there was no local spread of the disease in either case. No definite clue to the origin of these diseases was discovered. In neither case was the infection attributed to Irish animals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>DUBLIN SHOW ABANDONED.</head>
        <p>DUBLIN SHOW ABANDONED.</p>
        <p>The Royal Dublin Society have abandoned their Spring Show on account of the outbreaks of foot-and-mouth disease.</p>
        <p>ViThile sympathising with Irish stockowners in their predicament, says "The Times," British breeders are determined to resist any change in , the precautionary measures that might imperil the , safety' of their own herds and flocks. The action of the Board of Agriculture in per- mitting the landing for immediate slaughter of cattle, sheep and • pigs from Ireland north of a line from Drogheda to Oranmore has called forth protests from the Royal Agricultural Society and other bodies, not only because of immediate danger, but also on the ground that Ireland does not reciprocate in like circum- stances. The preservation of a clean bill is as important to Great Britain as to Ireland, and there is much to be said in support of the con- tention in the resolution passed by the Council of the Royal Agricultural Society to the effect that Ireland should concede to Great Britain the same consideration as she expects and obtains for her own shipments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN,</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN,</p>
        <p>Plant corms of the glorious Gladioli, three to four inches deep, eight inches apart in clumps. Sow Carrots on ground manured for a former crop: After raking the soil level make it fairly firm and draw drills one inch deep. The seeds are liable to stick together, mix-them eith sand and rub through the hand to separate them. Sow thinly and scatter some wood ashes or fine soil over the seeds, after which cover and make level.</p>
        <p>Plant Montbretia corms in clumps, in equal party sandy loam, leaf-mould, and decayed manure, in a sunny position. Place the corms three inches deep and two inches apart. The corms should be lifted and replanted annually. Plant Cape Hyacinth or Hyacinthus candi- cans five inches below the surface. Sow Antirrhinums in shallow boxes of light soil, and place in gentle warmth. A 'sowing of Parsley may be made in boxes under glass. The seedlings may be transplanted.</p>
        <p>ORNAMENTAL GRASSES. Now that the time for the sowing of annuals is with us, it may be well to call attention to the value of the various grasses that come in so well as adjuncts to vases and bowls of blossoms. It is to be regretted that so few people reserve a little space for these, which can be used in so many ways and with so many flowers,. more especially those of a light and delicate charac- ter. What more useful and effective mixed with sweet peas, the foliage of which we hesitate to cut lest we lessen the crop of bloom. No better time than the present can be recommended for sowirig these, provided the soil is in good work- ing condition, selecting a sunny and open posi- tion, and sowing very thinly. Among the suit- able grasses may be mentioned Agrostis nebu- losa, Brims, nordeum jubatum, and Lagurus ovatus, all of which can be had from any seedss. man at a very cheap rate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>PRUNING _ROSES.</head>
        <p>PRUNING _ROSES.</p>
        <p>. ... Roses may now be pruned, with the exception, perhaps: of a few of the more delicate teas, which may be left till about the second week in April. It is very difficult to lay down any hard, and-fast rules as to how the pruning may be done, all depending on the object the grower has in view, while roses vary so much in their style of growth, some being very vigorous, while others are just the reverse. If one wants exhi- bition blooms, then hard pruning is necessary. A distinction should be made between weakly growers and those of robust habit. The former should be cut back to at least three buds, leav- ing the strong. shoots of the vigorous growers from 10in. to 12in. long. In both cases the weak and unripe wood should be removed.</p>
        <p>SOWING STOCKS AND ASTERS. Stocks and Asters._ aro-two of the most popular annuals, there being scarcely a garden, big or little, without either. For freedom of flowering and fragrance Stocks are par excellence. Asters are ideal for cutting. Both are half-hardy, and require Similar treatment in raising, which is best done in warmth. Boxes may be used, pro- viding good drainage by boring holes in the bottom of the boxes. The best soil to use is a Mixture of I6am, leaf-mould, and sand. Fill the boxes within half an inch of the top, and moisten the soil thoroughly before sowing the seed. Cover the seed lightly with fine soil, and shade the box. with paper until germination takes place. The shading is to conserve the moisture. If the grower has no greenhouse the boxes may be placed in a warm frame, placing glass over the box and shading until the seed- lings appear. • When the seedlings are large enough to handle, lift with a piece of stick, with a Y-shaped piece cut out. Prick .the seedlings out, l or 2 inches' apart, into shallow boxes "filled with a similar mixture, adding a little old manure and lime rubble. Do ; not allow the seedlings to become drawn. Admit ' plenty of air to the house or frame to assist in hardening oft. Keep the seedlings rather on the dry side, on account of damping. Sprinkle sand. and POwdered'eharcoal On the soil, which will to some extent prevent damping. •</p>
        <p>William Ho ho was in his 80th year, and who saw service-in the Crimean War, was buried-on Saturday at Weaste.</p>
        <p>Nearly fifteen million passengers on the Ruch. dale Corporation tramways during the past 12 months paid- £78,548 in fares, an increase of 1'2,78f. The distance ran by the cars was, approximately 1, million miles. , ,</p>
        <p>_</p>
        <p>-77"</p>
        <p>Depressed owing to a nervous breakdown, -Walter Shaw (38), living in Reed-street, Burn- ley, took his life, cutting his throat with a penknife. Last year the borough and school libraries of Darwen. distributed 149,734. volumes for home reading. The school libraries alone lent out over 61,000 books. . .</p>
        <p>•_•r • 1 ' r</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>44.1.+4.4441414144+4444+++ POULTRY PEN. ;</head>
        <p>44.1.+4.4441414144+4444+++ POULTRY PEN. ;</p>
        <p>Hints and Interesting Facts and</p>
        <p>Chat for Amateurs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>;44,14•14,14)14+44+014•14•14b11114•14,14,14,1014•14•14:</head>
        <p>;44,14•14,14)14+44+014•14•14b11114•14,14,14,1014•14•14:</p>
        <p>"EXAMINER " SPECIAL. The chickens for the first few weeks of their life are subject to several diseases, which are apt to cut them off, and-unless poultry keepers pay close attention to the birds the results may be most unsatisfactory. Damn is a great source of 'disease; and every effort should be made to keep the chicks as dry -as possible. Cramp is, perhaps, one of the most frequent complaints. Young chickens are especially liable to be affected, and as prevention is better than cure, the best means-of preventing cramp is to give the young birds access to loose soil or ashes where they can scrape to their hearts' delight. Should cramp appear, place the birds in a dry run, supplied with plenty of dry ashes, and give a small dose of cramp cure, which can be purchased from any firm dealing in poultry medicines.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>DIARRMEA.</head>
        <p>DIARRMEA.</p>
        <p>Diarrhcea is a complaint which chicken rearers must closely, guard against. It is in- ducednA dirty coop has *been k set up wpqauyiste an epidemic. Soft food allowed i to turn sour is a prolific source, and stale known n green food is also liable to cause the oto m disease.</p>
        <p>When the complaint makes itself apparent, remove all the stale food, and feed on thoroughly mixed barley meal and Wiled rice, sprinkled with powdered chalk. Green food should be rigorously excluded until the birds have thor- oughly recovered. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>GAPES.</head>
        <p>GAPES.</p>
        <p>Gapes is a very fatal complaint amongst chickens. The danger period is between the third and eighth week, and chickens affected in this period have to be carefully and persistently treated if their lives are to be saved.</p>
        <p>The symptom is a sneezing, which continues for some time, and afterwards the birds gasp for breath, hence the name "gape." Unless curative reniedies are applied at this stage there</p>
        <p>is little hope-of saving the chick. _ The cause of gapes is the presence of a small red 'worm which attaches itself to the wind- pipe, and gradually chokes the chicken. Where these worms come from is a mystery, hut in some districts they play much havoc amongst chickens,- •</p>
        <p>DRASTIC CURE. . A common, yet drastic, method of treatment is, as soon as the disease is noticed, to place the affected chick in a small box and blow smoke from an ordinary tobacco pipe into the box. The smoke inhaled into the windpipe either kills the worm or causes the chick to cough so violently that the parasite is dislodged.</p>
        <p>The cure has to be operated with care. No more than two or three applications of the smoke should be given, each on a different day. Cease blowing the smoke into the box as soon as the chicken begins to stagger, otherwise the cure may prove as fatal as the worms. Another cure is to push a small piece of cam- phor, about the size of a pin head, into the throat of the chicken. The camphor kills the worm pest.. A number of patent medicines for the same complaint are on the market, and these are said to prove effective cures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>YOUNG STOCK.</head>
        <p>THE YOUNG STOCK.</p>
        <p>All one's thoughts are centred during the next few weeks upon the rearing of young stock, and (says a writer in "Farm and Home") the poul- try keeper must be prepared—if he wants to achieve the most satisfactory resulte7-to devote a great deal of time to this extremely important branch of the work. The progressive poultry- keeper,•who'adopts the Most Up-to-date methods, has by this time one or More batches of chickens ranging from, perhaps, a few days -to several weeks old. Such birds represent considerable value, as they will be ready for marketing in the course of the next month or two, and will therefore participate in the extremely high mproincetshst Immediately they are largehat are procurable during the spring enough, they should be fattened and packed off to market, or else disposed of to the higglers for re-selling to the large fattening establishments. The farmer - who possesses' well-developed and white-fleshed chickens during April and May can almost ask heisenowonmopnrgiceth, easfatthteeoreivi:. point that should rivalry is so exceptionally h ifever be forgotten in this connection is that it pays much better to market chickens during the , spring weighing, say, 21b. to 211 b. each, than to keep them until they weigh half as much again, for by the time they have attained to this size prices will, in all probability, have fallen, and no more money will be secured for them, against which must be reckoned the cost of their food, the slapbsooeurooionnvpoliveded in attending to them, and the The very young chickens require careful treat- ment during the present month, for the weather is often so changeable that chills are of frequent occurrence. Whenever the weather permits, the chickens, even those that are only a week or a fortnight old, should have access to a grass run, and should have plenty of fresh air, as these are essential to successful rearing. So far as the older chickens are concerned—those that were brought out early in the year—they should be housed in warm and well-ventilated sheds, which are perfectly .dry and free from draughts.</p>
        <p>UTILITY POULTRY CLUB'S LAYING COMPETITION. The fifth month of the Twelve Months' Lay- ing Competition, at Sedleseombe, terminated on February 28th last, and we have now before us</p>
        <p>T can supply Sittings of Buff Orpingtons or -L White Leghorns at 58. and 3s. 6d. per dozen; good, honest value; only large eggs sent Out; unfer- tile replaced.—Coombs, Poultry Farm, Weston- super-Mare. -VAST OFFER.—Eggs from my prize White Orpingtons, White Wyandottes and White Faverolles, an reduced from 10s. 6d. to 3s. Gd. per sitting, very fertile.—King Frost, Artegas,„ Aughton, Ormskirk. . LEGIIORN (Black and White) Eggs, grand stamp, big, eggs, 2s. 6d. dozen, unfertile re- Placed.—Newall, Coal Merchant, V 7 ,rd. 12,LYMOUTH—ROCK EGGS, 2s. 6d. per sitting; at home 2s larger quantities cheaper..—Thomas Wright, Mill House, Mouldsworth, Chester. SPECIAL OFFER.—Eggs Pure Indian Game, 78. 6d. and Si. dozen; Rose and Single Comb Rhode Island Reds. Buff and White Orpingtons, Black Minorcas, Barred Rocks,. and Croad Lang- shans, 3s. dozen; Indian Game-Plymouth Rocks, finest cross obtainable, 2s. Gd. dozen; all on un- limited grass run.—Bugg, East Chinnoek, QUSSEk FOWLS, Speckled and Reds; fulfil all requirements; reliable eggs at 3s. sd. per sitting.—Leonard Riley, Marston,_near Northwich. VITHITE Leghorns (T. Barron's), Black Leg- V horns. Ss. per hatch; abundant layers.—Wil- cock. The Pingot, Westleigh Mill. Leigh. WTllTE—Wyandotte Eggs for hatching; from selected layers; 3s. fid. per sitting, free on rail, 3s. if fetched.—A,”ly James Sharrock, Blackmoor, Mawdsley, Ormskirk.</p>
