      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <p>MR up me so im pm en</p>
        <p>CYGLING AND</p>
        <p>Easter Touring. The few days' relaxation at Easter, which seem to signalise the departure of 'winter, not- withstanding weather indications to the con- trary, afford the first real opportunity of the year of a short tour. Under favourable condi- tions the Easter tour is, perhaps, the intik: enjoyaiile'of any. The weather is not too warni, the coualry has a peculiar freshness quit. absent duritg the summer mouths, and the roads are generally free from dust. Unfortu- nately most riders are hardly in the ;:ink of condition so early in the season, and this year for obvious reasons, cycling has been quite ini• possible during the last month or two. Mora:— the Easter tour must not be too long tr too , exacting if it is to be really beneficial and enjoy- able. Fortunate, indeed, are these whase homes lie within easy reach of the more omantit3 parts of our country, since for them enjoyment is at hand. The others who can afford it will do well to transport themselves and their machines by train away from the busy industrial areas before commencing their tour, as riding through drab and monotonous streets is merely waste of both time and energy. The Lamp and Its Care.</p>
        <p>1 The days are not quite long enough as yet to venture without a lamp, but no lamp at all is to be preferred to a defective one, as then the cyclist does know where he is. There is nothing more annoying than to find oneself miles from anywhere with a lamp which refuses to burn. With oil lamps a frequent cause of trouble is the stopping up of the air holes, which is more diffi- cult to remedy than is usually supposed. Poking about is no use at all; the only satisfactory way is to immerse the lamp in boiling water, con- taining a plentiful allowance of soda, and then scrub with a stiff brush. Once all the carbon deposit is effectually removed there should be no more trouble with foul-smelling smoke. The wick also requires some little care and should never be allowed to become stiff at the end, a state of affairs usually resulting from a bad wick, unsuitable oil, or both. For actual light- ing purposes there is nothing to beat acetylene, but if an oil lamp can give fairly satisfactory results without attention, acetylene requires constant attention, or it refuses to burn at all: In towns an oil lamp is sufficient, but the tourist requires a much stronger one.</p>
        <p>The Pneumatic Tyre. How old is the .pneumatic tyre? That is 'a question lam asked very frequently. It is answered very- easily. Mr. J. B. Dunlop, the enterprising Belfast veterinary surgeon, com- menced experiments in his native city about 1888 with, pieces of hose piping and a primitive valve. A patent was taken out the same year, but the pneuma.tra tyre was noel/I-general ;• until a year or two later. From the .I'7 enormous possibilities of the invention seemed obvious, and once the early difficulties of suit- able rims and methods of attachment were finally solved, the solid tyre for cycles disap- peared. The now universal valve was the inven- tion of a Mr. Wood, of Bolton, and bore his name until the -patent was sold. With certain minor variations the tyres on the 1914 machine are practically identical with Dunlop's early efforts. Treads vary almost from year to year, but do not seem to be any improvem-nit on their predecessors; in fact, many peOple say that the original tread was by far the best one. The two patterns, wired and beaded, enjoy about equal popularity, and if prophecs is ever reli- able, the tyres of twenty years hence will be very similar to those in use to-day.</p>
        <p>first-t4e]</p>
        <p>A New Type of Motor Cycle. Until just recently the use of the rotary engine has been confined to aeroplanes, where it has achieved conspicuous success, but the principle has now been applied to the motor cycle by an American manufacturer. As was the case in a former Singer pattern, the engine is located in the back wheel. The stationary crankshaft is concentric with the wheel, serv- ing as an axle, 'the three cylinders thus revolv- ing in the same plane as the wheel. The latter are geared to the wheel by means of two spur gears and an idler, the engine revolving i-tt four times the speed of the wheel. Ingenious though the design certainly is, I doubt very much if it will attain It-.Ey great popularity, at any rate on this side of• the Atlantic. Motor cycles are becoming more or less standardised as far as matters of general design are concerned, and what the rotary type gains in satisfactory transmission it seems to lose in inaccessibility. Also there is the important question of side-slip, which is likely to be a very real-danger, al- though from a purely mechanical point of view the innovation is an interesting one. What Is He To Do?</p>
        <p>There is nothing more annoying to the average motorist than to have some obstinate person on the road in front of him who absolutely refuses to move up to the kerb and let the car pass. Country carts, driven by persons with "opinions" about motorists, tradesmen's carts, and cyclists are bad offenders, and the remedy is equally difficult in every case. What is the driver to do? There is only one thing he can do; sound his horn and keen nn counding it until the culprit gives way. It is never safe to attempt a passage on the near side, as almost as sure as the car moves forward the driver of the vehicle in front will suddenly become aware of his duty and bring about a nasty collision. ! In such a case, no matter what the provocation I may have been, the motorist would be held, guilty of negligence. Technically, of course, anyone wilfully obstructing an approaching vehicle can be proceeded against, but on a country road who is to bear witness and how many drivers have any wish to be bothered with the trouble of a prosecution?</p>
        <p>An Exacting Race. Great Britain and the United States have certainly seen some exciting motor races during the last few years, but everyone of them pales into insignificance when compared with a recent 4 trial in Uruguay. The course in this particular ' event was over a distance of 1,800 kilometers, or, roughly, 1,120 miles, from Montevideo to Salto. No direct course was mapped out, for the simple reason that such would have been quite impos- sible, owing to the nature of the country. Re- peated detours were about the least exacting features of the contest; with certain few excep- tions,. the rivers had no bridges of any kind, and had to be forded, a distinctly unpleasant process in the heavy sandy beds. Indeed, one stream has such a bad reputation that the Eng- lish equivalent to its name is "Get out if you can I" In the vicinity of Montevideo the goods Were quite good, but once beyond civilisation, swamps, ditches, mudholes, Ste., were the rule rather than the exception, but of eighteen cars originally entered for the contest, three survived the, whole distance, to the great credit of both Melt manufacturers and drivers.. #`, -</p>
      </div>
