      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Some of the _Beauty Spots to be</head>
        <p>Some of the _Beauty Spots to be</p>
        <p>Found in Cheshire.</p>
        <p>BY "SILVER-STREET."</p>
        <p>I have had a leaflet uut into my hands lately that others ought to obtain before buying their new mount for 1914. - -</p>
        <p>I should like my numerous cycling readers_to examine at the shop of any agent who deals in the Raleigh machines, their No. 50. This is not mentioned in their 1914 catalogue, for it is a special make and a special offer for a limited Tinie7-16-iier cash price is 8 guineas, although their usual instalment system applies also. The specification is an all-black machine, lady's or gent's model, fitted with Sturmey-Archer 3-opeed gear, and Raleigh gear case, supplied with pump, oiler, &amp;T., and the tyres are Warwick, on West- wood rims.</p>
        <p>special make a</p>
        <p>The circular calls it a "special end-of-season line, which will not be repeated," and this most likely explains an offer which appears so un- necessary by so famous a firm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>REST HOUSES IN CHESHIRE.</head>
        <p>REST HOUSES IN CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>Booth Bank, Millington, near Bowdon. To get to Mrs. Duncalf's the easiest way is to ride through Broom Edge as far as the Altrincham- Knutsford road, then turn right towards Buck- low Hill. Turning right again opposite the road leading to Ilostherne Church, this pretty farm- stead is the third house on the right. It has a notice-board and all accommodation for both cyclist and steed is ready.</p>
        <p>It is easily found from Heatley Station by pedestrians in a walk of three miles. The road is taken to the Jolly Thrasher at Broom Edge, and at the gate of Agden Park the right-hand lane is followed along the south side of the park. A left turn is made at the Altrincham sign-post and Millington village is at the foot of the hill. For the return a splendid round, total 51 miles, Inv be taken past the Stamford Arms at Bol- lington to Dunham Massey Station. The grounds of Booth Bank Farm are just now yel- low with daffodils, and the front garden is bright with polyanthus, primulas, and contains a very peculiar purple flower called "Job's affliction." Can anyone tell me the usual name for this plant? for I suspect that name is only local. _As you walk down the bank to Milling- ton you pass the old oak tree under which John Wesley is said to have preached, and Millington Hall Farm is another fine old building worth visiting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>SANDIWAY.</head>
        <p>SANDIWAY.</p>
        <p>This -was the destination of 23 members of the Wesleyan Cycling Club last Saturday. At 2.30 it was raining, but nothing dented they set forth lind•were.pwarded. Mena.eNever stop at \orris for fear; only fOr rain, and4et it be heavy. At Acton Bridge a great improvement was noticed. The road that I mentioned last year has not been straightened, but the hedges are now closely cut. Another great and welcome change was effected last year. Wall Hill, the ' hill from the Bone Works to Acton Village, has been made much less steep, much less curved, and the hedges have been removed. Thus the two most dangerous hills on any road round Warrington have been greatly modified,. and thus do we cyclists obtain benefit from the heavily taxed motorists. At Sandiway the party had to break up into two. Fifteen had tel at Mrs. Everitt's and were highly delighted; the other eight had tea at another shop riot far away, and were not delighted. How is it that in such a district only one small shop can be found to decently provide tea for visitors? and two years ago even that was missing: Of course,- there is the Blue Cap, but then-7.-- CLUB RUNS TO-DAY. —Warrington Wesleyan, Chester, 9.30. • Sankey Bridges Wesley Guild, Woolton, 8.0.</p>
        <p>4oma for</p>
        <p>EASTER MONDAY. Warrington Wesleyan, Delamere, 9.30. Sankey Bridges Wesley Guild, Eaton Hail, 8.0. Lamp Time: To-night, 8.10; next Satur- day, 8.25.</p>
        <p>A verdict of "Suicide during temporary in- sanity" was returned on Saturday on Paul Green, of Mill-street, Runcorn, who was killed on the railway line at Norman-road. He was said to have been under the delusion that he had accidentally poisoned his children.</p>
        <p>In granting permission for mixed bathing in the municipal baths at certain hours, the Black- pool Corporation specify that no spectators shall be allowed on those occasions.</p>
        <p>a Sight-Testli</p>
        <p>The Effect of Eyestrain on the Nervous System and consequent,- ly on the general health is serious, it necessitates the greatest care in the Selection of Spectacles to give relief, and the Importance of Sight-testing cannot be over- estimated. -</p>
        <p>We have confidence in recom- mending our methods which are the result of over 25 years' prac- tical experience.</p>
        <p>F. A. NEUBERT. (F.S M.C. by Exam.) Consulting Optician,</p>
        <p>Exclusive') ing and Spectacle Business. .SP. .1" fE$ - -</p>
