        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY,</p>
        <p>APRIL 11, 1914.</p>
        <p>A DISCONSOLATE PARTY.</p>
        <p>This has been a disastrous week for the Unionist party. It may-not have revealed the full extent of the ghastly] failure of the imposture they, have attempted upon the country, but it has prepared the way in such a Manner that neither they nor their opponents will be. unduly shocked by anything that may in the future happen to shatter their ranks and mortgage their for- _ . tunes. The plot by which they hoped to 'frighten the people of England into refusing long-deferd measure of justice to the people of Ireland has missed fire. At the risk of being called " provocative " by the party, who are rapidly exhausting the supply of adjec- tives in the English language in their rage at seeing the monopoly of power slipping from their grasp, we cannot refrain from saying that they played their card a little too soon. The bogus revolt of the Army should have been reserved for a time when they were nearer their goal. The little excite- ment of three weeks ago—the warrants for CARSON, the coercion for Ulster, and the resignation of oflicers--cannot be reproduced_ except as a farce on the, stage; and the passage of the Home 'Rule Bill is yet some distance away. 'At the present rate- of progress the use for the CARSON Army will have disap- peared by the time the measure is passed, and Mime Rule will come into existence ,in comparatively peaceful . times.</p>
        <p>Let us look at the advances towards</p>
        <p>a state of sanity that have been made in a very few days. A party clamour- ing for an, election on Home Rule de- clined to oppose tile re-election of Mr. ASQUITH in East Fife. Why? It cer- tainly was not for love of the man. The most unruly tongues of their unruly host had been employed in the HOUse, in the work of reviling him, but they dared not follow him to his . constitu- ency, and take the verdict of his con-</p>
        <p>stituents on an uncomplicated issue.</p>
        <p>Instead,, Mr. BALFOUR, for the first time in his life, went to demonstrate in Hyde Pram, --to_ join a top-hatted rabble in declaring that they protested against</p>
        <p>the Kuso's law being maintained, and the KING'S peace being preserved in Ulster. The " coercion " of Ulster bubble has been burst, thanks to the premature aberration of the Unionist leaders and of their supporters in the Press, whose ravings provided a big and interested audiencelor those who, oti the Liberal side, were anxious to explain how matters stood. So far frOm coercing Ulster to accept Home Rule the Government have agreed to allow Ulster to vote upon the question whether or not Ulster shall be excluded for six years, and there will be two General Elections in those six years to decide whether or not that exclusion shall end or become permanent. Sir EDWARD CARSON will have permanent exclusion or nothing. What he fears is that Ulster, which now.returns more Home Rulers than Unionists, will asguredly in part, and may as a whole,</p>
        <p>reject exclusion. Mr. BONAR LAW nufmaged to squeeze into a fortnight's Nta4)rec e de n ted flow of unjustified -fabitse a suggestion that if the Govern- ant would have an immediate dee- Ind and the Government won, LORD</p>
        <p>LANSDOWNE "would allow" the Gov- ernment to place the Bill on the Statute Book "without change, without delay, and without depriving them of any of the privileges which they enjoyed under the Parliament Act!" The con- sent of the House of Lords to Liberal measures, and the acknowledgment of the right of the same august body to regulate Liberal methods, or in the alternative civil war, is a fine doctrine to preach in a free country in the second decade of the Twentieth Century: The House of Commons, like the PREMIER, proceeds upon its way serenely and has passed the second reading of the Home Rule Bill by a majority of 80. Perhaps the most serious phase of the present trouble has been the attempt of the " Constitutional " party to drag in the Army to assist them in their' dog-in-the-manger game. The great fanfare has only drawn attention to the fact that the Army and its officers know their duty if Tory politicians do not. When Mr. ASQUITH went North and again showed " provocation " by allowing his admirers to see him at stations on the way, he said: "The very fact that our Army rests on a voluntary—as I hope it will cantinue always to do—and not on a Jnercenary or a conscript basis, is the best safe- guard for its maintenance of the splen- did and untarnished traditions of its redoubtable past." That is what Liberals think of the Army. Tories think the Army would make its political conscience the measure of its military duty. We hope the Army, as a whole, will note this mean opinion that is held of it by the party to whom most in its ranks are supposed to belong. It is this I brazen and unscrupulous attempt to ' replace the fallen House of Lords by the Army as a block in the line of progress of the peopje that has stirred up the people to an activity and vigilance that will absolutely ensure Mr: BONAR LAW being kept •in that wilderness from which he does not expect to emerge f9r forty years.</p>
      </div>
