        <p>, ti....i.,1. --..4',...:t.:.:,.., .:-..:::..,,.,,:,</p>
        <p>THE IRISH PICNIC.</p>
        <p>THE CORK STATUE OUTRAGE. To the Editor of "The Examiner." Sir,—The following quotation from an Irish Nationalist paper will, I think, be a complete answer to both the "Ulster Guardian" and Mr. O'Brien on this subject "A statue at Cork, erected to the memory of those deluded Irish who fought for the British in the Boer War, has been decorated with tar by certain loyalists to Ireland."—(" Irish Free- dom," April, 1914.)—Yours truly, ONE OF THE APOSTLES. We reproduce the above letter as received with one exception, which we regret, mechanical difficulties standing in our way. In t 1 original, the words from "those" to " war " and again from "by " to "Ireland" are written in red ink. We hope this explana- tion will make good our lack of a two-colour newspaper printinz machine. But werhave sought to learn something of "Irish Freedom," from which this quotation is said to have been taken. It is not in any newspaper directory that we can find. We - have also gone to Mitchell's "complete alpha- betical index to British newspapers, maga- zines, reviews and periodicals," and "Irish Freedom" is nowhere to be found. We should like information as to this publica- tion, and then proof that it has said what it is alleged to have said. As our readers know, we have not much faith in what one or all of the Apostles say someone else has said. It will be noticed that the Apostle calls it an "Irish Nationalist paper." Might we inquire why the red ink? Is it the substitute for the Belfast blood, which we .are sure will not be shed?—EDITOR.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>„ ...... Siy,—lt must be very gratifying to the mem- bers of the 'recent deputation to Ireland to find the great” interest their report has again aroused in the local Press. I believe the members will agree that the criticism is very misleading. Dealing with Mr. P. O'Brien's complaint of not keeping our appointment with Mr. Campbell and party, this was entirely the fault of the Belfast Liberal Association. They altered the appointment to a later period in the evening, without asking ourselves, but I was given to understand Mr. Campbell knew of the altera- tion, so that no blame can be attached to us. In any case, if it is any comfort to Mr. O'Brien, I might say the members of the Belfast Liberal Association presented an infinitely better case than the officials of the Trades and Labour Council, who, by the way, only represent hn in- finitesimal portion of trade unionists in Belfast. The statements made by Mr. Campbell and his two friends were challenged as not being correct by two or three of the Warrington Libehls, who happen to be well up in trade unionist matters. This led up to an insulting re- mark about Englishmen by the assistant secre- tary of the Belfast Trades and Labour Council, so that Mr. O'Brien may probably judge why the authors of his correspondence failed to im- press them. They also incidentally remarked they were not meeting us in their official capacity. As regards Mr. T. McDowell's article and the cutting from the "Ulster Guardian," might I at once disclaim knowledge of any breach of faith by disclosing what transpired at our meet- ing with the Belfast Liberal Association? It is quite possible some member might have spoken innocently of what had transpired, although I am not aware that anything was diScussed that was intended to be private. With the exception of about five questions from our- selves the time taken by the members of the Belfast Liberals was all they required, and quite as long as any interview accorded to any Union- ists. It was a very interesting meeting, and if they failed to impress our Liberal friends then surely the blame was theirs. Will you kindly allow me to make a sugges- tion? If it is possible to convert a deputation composed of all Liberals (except myself) into anti-Home Eulers, by methods such as your cor- respondents suggest, why not send over a party of Conservatives under the guidance of a Nationalist, but with the same liberty we have The methodt ahm i Nn ga t oti ohf ne proving rrl npp gap party ourtryt of uco a sgr h el It falls tom o jump R toul at all times given? If you can succeed in ob- tattethen our agguannede. like this if their case is so good. I venture to prophesy they dare not do it.—Yours, W. U. BELLIAN. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 11, 1914. THE PASSING WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 11, 1914. THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>CARE FOR THE AGED.</p>
        <p>The case of Charles Cullen, ari old man still</p>
        <p>fit to do some casual work very well, who be- lieved himself entitled to the old-age pension, but was unable to prove his age, shows to wha treuble those who are administering the Act will go, and the success which attends their efforts. Cullentlid not know where he was born, but he had an idea where his yoilth was nursed. Mr. Tilling (clerk to the local Insurance Committee) took the matter up, and in due course the mes- sage came from a remote parish in Ireland that Charles Cullen was stated in the census, March 31st, 1851, to be nine years of age, which meant that he was born before March 31st, 1842, and therefore entitled to his pension. During the • negotiations• Cullen, who is employed at "The Examiner" Office, was paying his insurance contributions, and in addition to being placed on the pension list, he has had a goodly return of money " repayable on account of such con- tributions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>BURNING AND A SHINING LIGHT.</head>
        <p>A BURNING AND A SHINING LIGHT.</p>
        <p>A tablet was unveiled last Saturday to the memory of the late Mr. F, Monks, in the pre- sence of many who have been actively asso- ciated with philanthropic work in our town. Affixed as it is to the building which constitutes. the Nurses' Home, the tablet will be an abiding monument to the work of a large-hearted man, who, through a long and strenuous life, never forgot the sorrow and suffering of his less favoured brothers and sisters, and was always ready to hold out a helping hand to those over- taken by sickness or misfortune.</p>
        <p>The occasion was unique in more respects than one. The cost of the memorial was borne entirely by the employes of the firm of which the late Mr. Monks was the founder and head, and, in addition, they handed over to the Endowment Fund of thNursing- Alsociation the handsome sum of X6o. The tablet was unveiled by the son of the man whose meraory it is to honour. As we listened to his just and modest tribute we felt that he would worthily maintain the high standard of duty and sense of personal responsibility which was so marked a feature in his father's char- actor.</p>
        <p>We have been much interested in reading the I records of .the proceedings of the committee of the Nursing Association from its inception to the present time, and we find that on the initia- tive of the late Mr. Monks a number of gentle- men met together on April 23rd, 1897, and decided to form an association, undenOmina- tional and non-political, for the purpose of supplying !grained nurses, to nurse the sick poor in their own homes." So rapidly was the scheme proceeded with, thanks to the unfailing energy of Mr. Monks and those associated with him, that in three months from the date of the ,meeting referred to the 'first case was being nursed, and by the 18th September following 515 visits to sick folk had been paid by the two nurses who constituted the staff of the Institu- tion.</p>
        <p>In her report to the committee, under date September 30th, 1897, the then Superintendent Nurse writes:</p>
        <p>If there should linger a doubt in any mind 'of the need of District Nurses in the town, or as to whether this work is appreciated, such doubt would be immediately dispelled by hearing a hundredth part of the fervent expressions of gratitude with which we are greeted on every hand, and if the blessings which are daily called down upon us and upon those who are the pro- moters and supporters of the association be realised, it will be a rich harvest indeed. For eleven years after its inauguration Mr. Monks was the guiding spirit and director of the operations of this association as Honorary Treasurer and Chairman of the Committee of Management, he was invaluable. He never begrudged either time or money to the cause which he had so much at heart, and with the aid of a band of noble men and women, who have unobtrusively carried out this great labour of love, thovsands of cases of intense physical pain and weariness have been ministered to by educated and gentle women with a skill and devotion which cannot be appraised in words. Endowed with great will-power and physical resource, Mr. Monks remained at his post in business and public work for many years longer than falls to the lot of most men, but the pressure of years of hard service told at last, and in 1908 he was compelled to hand over the reins to younger.hands. He and many others of those who helped to establish our Nursing Association have joined the great majority, but their legacy to us is a memory fragrant of good deeds and an inspira- tion to perpetuate with increasing power and usefulness, a cause which has an irrefutable claim upon all who sympathise with those upon whom the heavy hand of affliction has fallen.</p>
        <p>CONCERNING CHUACII COLLECTIONS. • Easter offerings and Easter Vestry Meetings will soon set us talking about church revenue. "Say, for argument's sake, there are 400 people in church. The average number of coins collected is then about 250, including. silver coins, and a quite unnecessary number of halfpennies." Thus wrote a certain vicar a while ago. The aforesaid vicar added: "I do SO hope I may not have again to draw your attention to the too large number of non-givers. I say nothing about the 'bag-rattlers '—the trick is beneath contempt." As a popular commen- tator explained at the time, the bag-rattler is one who gives nothing, but, to use a Chaucer- ion phrase, "h maketh a countenance as if to give." - • -</p>
        <p>One can hardly sink beneath that—unless, indeed, one shoUld contrive to appropriate the receptacle for alms itself.. Nive have-somewhere read—in an American paper surely—how a hat was passed round a certain congregation for the purpose of taking up a collection. After it had 'made the circuit of the church, it was handed to the minister, who had exchanged pulpits with: the regular preacher, and he found not a single penny in it. Ile inverted the hat over the pul9it cushion and, shook it that its emptiness might be known. Then, looking to- wards the ceiling, he exclaimed with great fer- vour "I thank the Lord that I got back my haf from this congregation." Such meannass can be paralletied here and therd in our own country. We remember ,a story of one—a Methodist, too—who went to hear Peter Macken- zie, and, putting a half-sovereign in the plate, applied afterwards for Os. 6d., as he "couldn't afford it." He had enjoyed a good dinner, along with the preacher, at the house of the steward, and a supper at the end of the day, with a few choice Cigars and a "refresher" thrown in. So his host sent him an 'account -for "one dinner, price so and so; one Supper, ditto; cigars, &amp;c., so and so; total Os: 6d., and receipted the . account Whereupon the offended gentleman sent the excise officer after his host and charged him with selling some- thing without a licence! Is there a remedy for the too prevalent evil now deprecated? Let us turn again to the Wild West for a moment. A preacher out there had been bemoaning the scanty collections at church for some time past, and one day " Broneho Bill," the local "terror," whose repu- tation was that he had shot more men than he had fingers on his hands, undertook to take the collection plate round the following Sun- day. Somewhat dubiously the preacher con- sented. At the service the backwoods clerical made an impassioned appeal for a more gen- erous response, and then " Broncho Bill" pro- ceeded to move down the church with the plate. His bloodthirsty reputation for shooting a man on sight on the slightest provocation acted like magic, and dollar-bills fairly bulged over the side of the plate, but one hapless worshipper, being a stranger to the plaie, unfortunately ten- dered a solitary cent. Instantly Bill's revolver whipped out, and in a voice which had often made a saloon full of men tremble, observed, "Say, stranger, guess you've made a mistake. This is a dollar touch." The stranger gave one frightened look into Bill's ferocious eyes and meekly laid down a dollar. Of course, that sort of thing would here be as impracticable as it is undesirable. What, then, of the novel scheme a while ago adopted by a minister for bolstering up the church collection, which had been diminishing? He informed his congrega- tion, just before the plates were passed round, that the members litho were in debt were not expected to contribute. The collection that day was double the usual sum. To this we may just add that Mr. Andrew Car- negie had an amuSing experience the last time he was in Florida. He attended a service in a little negro church, and at the collection slipped a five-dollar bill into the box. After the con- tents had been totalled up, the clergyman arose and announced: "Brethren and sisters, the col- lection this evening seems to figure up six dol- lars and 44 cents., and if the five-dollar bill contributed by the gentleman from the north is genuine the repairs on the sanctuary will begin immediately I"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>APPLETON, A FISHERMAN'S PARADISE..</head>
        <p>APPLETON, A FISHERMAN'S PARADISE..</p>
        <p>....... _ About two years ago Appleton Reservoir was emptied "a,nd cleaned and cleared of coarse fish. Then the Town Clerk put in the water 60,000 fario and rainbow trout from the hatcheries in the Park. So well have these fish thrived that great sport has been experienced of late by a numerous body of the local disciples of Izaak Walton. Mr. Williams, of the Hippodrome, is credited with having caught the biggest fish, a fine trout of 31b., but, as il was of the rainbow species, for which the close season 'does not end, till June 16th, he returned his catch to the water. Dr. Anderson, an ardent trout angler,, has along with many others, done very well these last few days. It will be well if those who may feel inclined to try their luck at Appleton bear in mind that it is not ,a free water, but that tickets must be got from the Walton Estate Office. The reservoir belongs to the ~town, but the fish belong -to Sir Gilbert Greenall. The worm, and not the fly, has been responsible for the successes we record, an attempt made to restrict the reservoir to fly fishermen having failed.</p>
        <p>LORD CROSS'S WILL. The late Lord Cross left net personal estate in the United Kingdom apounting to over £60,000. Several of the provisions in the will are of more than ordinary interest. For in- stance, the testator forbids the publication of correspondence which he received from mem- bers of the Royal Family and from the Viceroy of India. All statues, pictures and other presents received from Queen Victoria are ordered to devolve as heirlooms with the peerage. Lord Cross's successor to the title, his grandson, has a settled legacy of £40,000, the real estate being settled In tail male. The residue of the estate also goes with the title after the satisfaction of certain bequests to testator's children, servants and others. Death duties payable amount to over £7,000. Lord Cross was Homo Secretary in the Con- servative Government from 1874 till 1880, and from 1885 till 1886; and Secretary for India from 1886 till 1896. He had a political pension of £2,000 a year for over twenty years, and up to January, 1913—the latest figures available— he had drawn £40,760. In order to get a pension an ex-Cabinet Minister has to declare that his means are insufficient to maintain his position. All ,these political pensions are held by Conser- vatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>YESTERDAY'S FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>YESTERDAY'S FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD WIN CUP.</p>
        <p>Fine Victory at Altrincham. Latchford Association team last night came back triumphant to "VV.arrington bearing with them the Altrincham Amateur Cup, which they won by defeating Broadheath by three goals to nil.</p>
        <p>The first half was evenly contested, and there was no scoring up to the interval. Early in the second half the Latchford forwards gave a greatly improved exhibition, and Smith forced a corner, and this being got away, they quickly gained another, from which P. Webb opened the score for Latchford. This reverse caused the Heathens to make desperate efforts to get on level terms, but the Latchford defence proved too good. The Latchford players took more command of the game. Fogg made no mistake and beat Pearson with a good shot. P. Webb practically made the issue safe by regis- tering a third goal for Latchford. Result: Latchford, three goals; Broadheath, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S REVENGE ON DEWSBURY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S REVENGE ON DEWSBURY.</p>
        <p>The visit Of Dewsbury to Wilderspool yester- day afternoon proved a popular event, and there were some 5,000 spectators present. Warrington played'the usual team, with the exception that Baker, who did smart things with the "A" team, substituted Ruddick at right •wing threequarter. Renwick, Warrington's old man, turned oili-for Dewsbury. The game opened in a scrappy manner. Todd was penalised for not playing the ball. Jolley tried a long kick at goal, but the ball went outside. Chesters was next penalised for off-side without any result. A fine bit of footwork by the Warrington forwards was marred by Fearn- ley kicking too hard, and McCraw saved: Way- well made a clever, but unsuccessful, attempt to drop a goal. Some fine passing was witnessed on the part of Warrington, but it did not gain what it merited —a try. Waywell, who played a dashing game, was obstructed. Jolley's kick went wide.</p>
        <p>THE FIRST GOAL. Renwick was penalised for not playing the ball and the referee promptly awarded a penalty. Nicholas piloted through a fine goal.. Waywell, with a brilliant run, was pulled up for some off-side infringement. The game at this stage was entirely in favour of Warrington. Cliesters, after, a scramble, rolled over with a clever try, and Nicholas easily got the additional goal. Warrington continued to have the greater share of the game and they could do everything but score. Mid-field play ensiled for a long time, and half-time was near ere Warrington got on the Dewsbury line. Jolley was badly obstructed in the last minute of this half. A free-kick was given' and Nicho- las landed a goal. Halftime: Warrington, 3 goals 1 try (9 points); Dewsbury, nil.</p>
        <p>FINE WARRINGTON PASSING. The second half opened with a fine bout of Passing, in which all the Warrington threequar- ters participated. Baker unfortunately was run into touch, but the result was that War- rington hovered around the Dewsbury citadel for a time. Evans only found relief with a long kick into touch. Soon after Jolley was forced to touch down from the best bit of play Dews- bury had yet shown. GAME OF SAFETY. Good forward work by Warrington set Fearn- ley away. He was tackled on the line, and let the ball roll over, for Macgraw to kick dead. Sharples broke out with a fine run for Dews- bury. Clare bowled him over and Jolley found relief.</p>
        <p>- ..... . Jolley was playing a game of safety in the few attacks made by Dewsbury, and twice touched down as a precaution. Renwick and Todd, the high-priced Dewsbury men, were very indiffer- ent. In fact, Young was a much better "stand off" than the much-praised Todd. Tranter lost a chance of scoring by parting with the ball too quickly almost on the Dews- bury line. Baker failed with a brilliant at- tempt to score. Rhodes, who was injured, was an absentee for the greater part of the second half. Result: Warrington, three goals one try (nine points); Dewsbury, nil. A WELL-DESERVED WIN.</p>
        <p>Warrington well deserved the victory, and the only regret was that the score was not larger. Twice tries were lost through slowness in the second half. All the Warrington team played well. They were much superior to Dewsbury at half and threequarter back. Waywell was splendid, Brookes sure, and Baker made a favourable impression. Tranter was. useful Nicholas and Young were much superior to Todd and Davies. Jolley was generally sure, although twice he made awkward slips.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>LIBERALS TWITTED.</head>
        <p>LIBERALS TWITTED.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett and the Progressive Council Members. EXTENSION OF LOANS.</p>
        <p>The unique spectacle of a Conservative Alder- man defending the Liberal Government's Town Planning Act and denouncing.the Liberals for not doing likewise was witnessed at the War- rington Town Council on Tuesday. The incident arose during the debate on the following resolu- tion proposed by Councillor Pemberton That, in view of the continued diminution in the number of dwellings available for the work- ing classes in urban areas, and in order to en- courage private enterprise to meet the demands for such dwellings, this Council urges upon the Local Government Board the necessity of grant- ing an extension of the time allowed for the repayment of loans taken up by local authorities to meet the cost of making and paving private streets from seven years to twenty years, and, that a copy of this resolution be forwarded to the Prime Minister, the President of the Local Government Board, and the Member of Parlial ment for the borough. Councillor PEMBERTON said all the members of the Council must be in agreement with the principle embodied in the resolution. The strange feature was that, under the Public Health Act, the Local Government Board granted long terms for repayment—in some towns as long as 25 years. For some unaccount- able reason, notwithstanding all the efforts of the Town Clerk's department and the Paving and Sewerage Committee, the Board had de- clined to allow longer than seven years for loans I under the Private Street Works Act. That was a peculiar attitude in view of the fact that the Private Street Works Act was really framed to amend the Public Health Act with regard to paving, so as to make the charges on each owner more equitable. The Local Government Board had done things which were not in the Act.</p>
        <p>EXPLAIN THE EXPLANATION. Councillor HENBHALL seconded. He was afraid they were asking for too much in asking for 20 years. It was more poSsible that they would get a concession if they, asked for ten years. At a recent inquiry in Warrington they had advo- cated ten years for repayment of a loan, yet with the callous indifference of the average Govern- ment official the Inspector did not take a bit of notice. (Laughter.) One of the curses of the country was the power of bureaucracy—the per- itanent Government official. Councillor ROBERTS. I was wondering what you meant. (Laughter.) Councillor HsxsitALL : I thought I had bettei explain. (Laughter.) Alderman JAMES EVANS : A good job you were able. (Laughter.) Councillor HENSHALL : I am not like that Bishop of whom Byron said—" Bishop Berkley has been explaning metaphysics to the nation. I wish he would explain his explanation." (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>"SEDUCED AND BEGUILED." They don't erympathise and have no know- ledge of our districts. They might give ten years to the unfortunate investor who has been seduced and beguiled into investing money in bricks and mortar. (Laughter.) Alderman TINNiox : Look at him! (Laughter.) Councillor HENsnAtt : Let the Council pause to think how well I should have looked if I had not invested in that way. (Laughter.) I should have been a Falstaff. (Laughter.) It is a matter of real life and death to those people who have invested their money in property. Alderman TINNION said from what he knew of the Local Government Board it was preposterous to ask for 20 years. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>LIBERAL GOVERNMENT'S WISDOM.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL GOVERNMENT'S WISDOM.</p>
        <p>Alde-rinitrii—BE;;;;TT-- "appreciated the wis:lo;m. of the Liberal Government in passing such an Act. He only wished the Liberals in the Council were brave enpugh to back up the Liberal measure. There was only one who did so—Councillor Ifaw- thorn. (Hear, hear.) He the Alderman) claimed to be Progressive, and he was afraid he was more so than some of the Progressive Liberals on the Council. Councillor ROBERTS: What about the Ulstei Covenant?</p>
        <p>_Aldermilia BENNETT :Alit is another thing. The MAYOR : You had.better keep to the reso- lution. (Laughter.) Councillor HAWTHORN said he would like to see the time extended, but he had not much hope of getting 20 years. ,</p>
        <p>The resolution was carried, as well as a second one to invite support from other local author. ities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>EXHIBITION AT PARR HALL. A Place of Interest, Music, and Mirth.</head>
        <p>EXHIBITION AT PARR HALL. A Place of Interest, Music, and Mirth.</p>
        <p>The scene in the Parr Hall since Tuesday has been one of hurry and bustle, quite in keeping with the busy Industrial Exhibition. Here you will find dainty young ladies willing to address you on the superiority of the articles. of their particular firms; there are women and children, be it said, also men, standing at the tasting stalls, and farther down the building can be seen agents for the many manufacturers repre- sented, explaining their labour-saving devices. The avenues are constantly crowded with people, who, as their eyes are attiacted by a particular stand, halt and examine, or taste. Suddenly the hall is plunged into darkness, and from the stage multi-coloured spouts of water begin to dance higher and higher. The effect is pretty and even7changing, for the water makes fantastic leaps and is ever illuminated by dazzling colours. The fairy fountain ex- hibition concluded, the hall is filled with sweet music, and an upward glance reveals the musical dames—almost as sweet as their music. The idea of the exhibition is not to realise a</p>
        <p>MR. W. R. MOORE, The Manager. ,</p>
        <p>profit, but to make people acquainted with valu- able food preparations and methods- of cooking them, so that the health and happiness of families may be benefited; to- demonstrate by working exhibits, lectures, &amp;c., the latest domes- tic, sanitary and scientific appliances and inven- tions that make the wheels of life run smoothly; and to blend the visits with bright interludes of healthy entertainment. Thus, without obliga- tion, it is possible to judge how far the various exhibits meet the various needs and remove diffi- culties, and also learn how to use valuable domestic appliances to the best advantage. One of the most interesting working exhibits is the potter's wheel, showing the method of making pottery as handed down by succeeding geßerations from early days. The Hall of Mirth where mirrors reflect the onlooker in such gro- tesque forms, is always responsible for a laugh. Daily there are cookery lectures, which should be of interest to housewives, for here the latest and most up-to-date methods are explained. The exhibition is well worth a visit.</p>
        <p>Annot JOHNSTON CARS are excellent value; 4-seater complete £2Bs.—Sole agent for War- rington and district, J. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street. Warrington.</p>
