      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>GUARDIANS' BREEZE. A Question of Medical Extras. THE EX-MASTER'S CASE.</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS' BREEZE. A Question of Medical Extras. THE EX-MASTER'S CASE.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held on Wednesday, Mr. E. L. Bent presiding.</p>
        <p>"CHICKENS AND BEER."</p>
        <p>The principal discussion took place on the decision of the Finance Committee to request Mr. Brown, the late Master at the institution, to pay for certain chickens and beer consumed by him, which the committee stated were extras. _</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell mentioned the matte' when the minutes of the 'previous meeting had been read. He asked if there were any record on the rednutes of Mr. Brown's case. The Clerk : I said the resolutions of the Finance Committee were approved and adopted.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: This was a Board matter, and the Board sanctioned what had been done by the Finance Committee. It was a special suggestion, so far as my memory serves me, that if Mr. Brown did not pay he was to be proceeded against. The Clerk: That is so. The Chairman: That transpired at the meet- ing.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: I shall refer it back.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: There is nothing in regard to this matter which differs in any shape or form from any matter that has been brought before you by any committee at any time. It is not required to put in the Board's minutes all the details of the Finance Committee.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: A long statement was made in this matter and published in the papers. The Clerk: Statements are not put in minutes, only resolutions. Questions were asked at tht. last meeting and were answered. They may appear in the papers, but that is not a resolution of any committee or the Board. I might say "this is a matter which is assuming serious dimensions," but I shall not put that on the minutes, except the Board direct me. The mat- ter will be brought before the Board later. on. The minutes were confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>LETTERS FROM MR. BROWN.</head>
        <p>LETTERS FROM MR. BROWN.</p>
        <p>At a later stage, when the correspondence was read, the Clerk said he had the following letters from Mr. Brown, "Re Medical, Extras." The first was: "1 am in receipt of your communicit- tion of the 26th inst. Before stating definitely what I propose to do, I desire to first seek advice from the Executive Council of the Asso- ciation of workhouse-Masters, as it occurs to me that a certain principle is involved, which is of the utmost importance to the whole of the Workhouse Masters throughout the country." In a further letter Mr. Brown stated: "Re ferring to your letter of the 26th ult. respecting the medical extras, I have since read the report of the Guardians' meeting in the Press, and if that version is a true copy of what actually took place at that meeting I most strongly pro- test at the way in which the members of the Board were kept in entire ignorance of the ex- planation contained in my letter. I don't doubt but that the committee who went into the matter may have been in possession of my explanation, but I maintain that as the full Board were the ultimate tribunal to consider this matter my letter should have been read to them in its entirety. I should also like to point out that it has never been the custom- to report any officer (who was receiving medical extras) to the Board, neither has it been the custom to report any sick officer unless he Waft off duty. The only reason for reporting .an officer off duty, ill, was to procure the sanction of the Guardians to appoint temporary assistance, should they deem it necessary. Furthermore, I take exception to you threatening me with legal proceedings for the recovery of the cost of these medical extras (duly recommended by the resi- dent medical officer), when you must know, according to the statute governing Poor Law Accounts, the proper procedure is to acquaint the Local Government auditor of any matter arising out of Workhouse Masters' books, and it is for that official to determine the legality or otherwise at the half-yearly audit. I shall be glad if you will lay this letter before the Board at their next meeting, with the request that the matter be left over for the .Local Government Board's auditor to determine, so that I have an opportunity to argue my case, Si he see the necessity to surcharge me."</p>
        <p>i Boa: I havi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>NOT ASKED FOR.</head>
        <p>NOT ASKED FOR.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said the letter of Mr. Brown was considered by the Finance Committee, and in the report of that committee it was stated that Mr. Brown's letter, and also that of the Local Government Board, was read. The letter would have been read at the Board meeting if it had been asked for.</p>
        <p>I am not, Aid the Clerk, going into this matter at all, but I ask you to refer it to the Local Government Board. There are other matters, and they are matters I would like to bring before the Board, and I quite agree with Mr. Brown the matter ought to come before the Local Gov- ernment Board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>"CONTENTION QUITE RIGHT."</head>
        <p>"CONTENTION QUITE RIGHT."</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell : As regards what the Clerk has said he has made it quite plain it lE our mistake in not asking for the letter to bG read. I think Mr. Brown's contention is quite right, that if the matter is brought before the. Board, the Board ought certainly to have 116 letters before them. I think before what seems. to me a rather drastic move—referring it to the Local Government Board—be taken, it would be well to discuss the matter openly before the Board,,l think we ought to know more than we do know. Is Mr. Brown's contention a correct one, or is he incorrect in saying that medical extras. have been granted in this institution without the matter being before the Board? Has it been quite sufficient for the doctor to certify that an officer should receive Medical extras. If that has been the custom, and if it has been the practice I think we are not dealing with Mr. Brown quite as we ought to, and it is cal- culated to injure him. We ought to know what has been the practice in the institution as to the granting of medical extras and after that we shall be in a position to extras, what ought to he done.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: 'have to say "What do you •ap- point committees for?" The Rev. A. M. Mitchell : Oh The Clerk: This matter has been dealt with very carefully by the Finance Committee, and all the details have been gone into thoroughly. Are you going to consider the matter again and go into all particulars that the committee have considered? You have adopted the recommenda- tion of that committee as to a certain course of procedure.</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles: Mr. Brown is agreeable to go to the Local Government Board. The Clerk: He asks for it. Mr. Knowles: I think that is the proper course. (Voices: "Oh, no !") The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: I think he said it was a duty for the auditor. If that has been the practice in the past, why should there be any difference now?</p>
        <p>Mr. Jolley: If it has been in the past. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: Quite so. I asked a direct question, and I would like an answer to that question.</p>
        <p>"WANTING. CAREFUL CONSI DERATION." The Clerk: I would like to go further in this matter. I don't want Mr. Brown to teach ins my duty in any shape or form. This is a matter wanting careful consideration, and I am going to be guarded as to what I say in the matter. I would like to ask this question, knowing as I do that a few nunnbers of the Board have been supplied with correspondence from Mr. Brown. I would like to ask Mr. Mitchell if he has re, ceived a communication in this matter from Mr. Brown?</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: I don't treat that as a fair claw—Lion. Mr. KnOTales : I think it IA a hard question to ask. I have not had one. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: I ask the Clerk a question as a Guardian in connection with the Board transacting its business and I cannot get a definite answer. The Clerk gives me an Irish- man's answer by asking me another question, and he has no right to do it. Mr. Brooks: I think it is a waste of time dis- cussing it here if we are going to send it to the Local Government Board. I move we send it to the Local Government Board. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: Had that letter been read at the last meeting it would have put us in a different position. The Clerk : It is not ours or the Board's mis- take. Mr. Mitchell is speaking personally. No</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
      </div>
