        <p>t,kli ~ Sons of Temperance. ti- e ~,-, jilt rest was added to the: quarterly 1.-„' the h Warrington Division of the Sons tl44l'll(l)_esi-,Anc e, which was held in the Bewsey: ittil:itt"ti%leyan. - .11 chrch Room, by the visit of Am Nrokal DivisirtOrerng,aniser, Bro. Mclntyre. of Earlestown, oceu the the oe' G.W. a referred to the work r, o„ lair, and briefly . ",taam‘ganiser was doing in the Earlestown nih°l:l r l'io to which the Warrington Divi. t 7.4 .411)41.1k dri' I tracing the growth of stehne. '% '3. elk.l I tnre said that the tit 4 ~1 the ner NyY- main; teeribu- ' not so much the di.s . n llntt tli;' kenewta-ry- benefits, but the dissemination 11-4 Iztion,v.tinciples of temperance. The question f the ih4l4 6111 g the gulf between the leaving. o 1.-''ta(ithOf 11 I) Y the children and their entry .o tfi`4leit." 4'o°4 13- en and women, still true..t.. tinkitebblirftalleaes mpledge, had been sumessfull 0 cad 1 the Sons of Temperance. Through tel,7l(l.4t,,seetions the Order was defending the ta.e. i`r°4l the insidious attacks of the drink the same time the principles of total were being taught from a scientific and this was resulting in a retention j-56 e-99°, Per cent. of the cadet members at the 'itteOe,l. Years. As far as the adult section was i°,s4g iLed, there had been for many years . a progress, and when the yearly !e- -147tezatnined it was found that the num- t/tpulsion only averaged two per 1,000. `4.0r.f,c1, the Warrington iington brethren to take a I,tt.eZtivo interest in their branch, and sug 13 - eit VatY ways in which an increased interest 1,78 4 vo stiinulated.—Bro. R. Jones, in propos- 0, tis.,l,te„, of thanks to the Organiser, mentioned 'lp tle "imeulties which surrounded the division l„ th,s4tr, itge_sent time, especially the fact that tn° bre' •"'" I"- as one of the foremost centres of tl'ortl.° twlieini ii/idustry. Still they were prepared ItallltAis e,„ °est in order to help forward .the for which their Order was estan- lirs Baston 