        <p>1,6, ROT ‘lq4le C4ll'B are World renowned. 4-seater L.: Nzae dYnamo electric lighting outfit, -'11„ 1344, agent for Warrington and district, • 51 to 55, Bridge-etreet, Warring-</p>
        <p>• PREMIER IfoToa. CYCLES. Light-weight Model, £36.—Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge- street, Warringtou.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>Warrington School Girls in France. A party from the Secondary School, consisting of eight girls and two teachers, Miss Clark and Miss Forsyth, on Thursday started for their Easter Holidays, which are to be spent in France. They saw as much of London as pos- sible on Thursday afternoon and evening, and they set out for Folkestone early on Friday. morning. After crossing to Boulogne, they make their headquarters at a Boarding School at Vincennes, on the outskirts of Paris.</p>
        <p>King's Bounty for Triplets. As stated in "The Examiner" last week, the Rev. W. Bracecamp, vicar of St. Paul's, applied for the King's bounty in respect of Mrs. Daw- son, 11, Longshaw-street, who gave birth to triplets on Wednesday week. On Sunday the rev, gentleman received .f. 3 from His Majesty, a gift which was highly appreciated. Mrs. Daw- son, besides getting the 30s. insurance maternity money—which in cases like this ought .surely to be £4 10s.—has received kindly attention from the Vicar and other friends. Her husband is a fitter on the railway.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Coffee and _Chicory.</head>
        <p>Coffee and _Chicory.</p>
        <p>Discussing the analyst's report.' at Tuesday's meeting of the Warrington Town Council, Dr. Joseph drew attention to the adulteration of coffee. There were 17 samples examined, three of which were adulterated with chicory, and the percentage of adulteration was high. All these samples were informal, and he was sorry that that was so, because there could be no prosecu- tion. Chicory was quite opposite in its effects to coffee, which was more or less a tonic of the heart's action, whereas chicory was a depres- sant. It would be wise to obtain formal 'samples, so that they might prosecute offenders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>St. Paul's Sale of Vtrork..</head>
        <p>St. Paul's Sale of Vtrork..</p>
        <p>- _ An important and interesting sale of work is to be held by the St. Paul's congregation in the Schools, Kendrick-street, on Saturday, the 25th April. The sale, which will have stalls laden with dainty and useful articles, will be opened by Mrs. Wray, of Timperley, whose father was once Vicar of St. Paul's Church. Splendid entertainments have been arranged by Mrs Stone, of Brook House, Wilderspool-eauseway, and there will be many other attractions. The proceeds will go to the Additional Clergy Fund, to aid parochial organisation, and a grant to the £l,OOO required for the Day Schools. it is hoped those interested in the splendid work done at St. Paul's will rally in large numbers to make the sale a great success.</p>
        <p>Motor Cycle Club. The initial run of the newly-formed Warring- ton Motor Cycle Club was held on Saturday, when a start was made from Victoria-square, Stockton Heath. Shortly after the commence- ment one of the members had to retire, suffering from magneto trouble. A halt was made ..t Whitchurch, where 25 partook of tea in the Victoria Hotel. The return journey via Chester was attended by trouble to two members, one suffering from a burst in his back tyre, and the other being delayed for nearly two hours with carburettor trouble. To-day a few of the mem- bers are meeting at Victoria-square for, a run to Axe Edge to witness the open hill -limb of the Stockport Motor Cycle Club. Non-members are cordially invited. On Monday there will be a run to Rhyl, starting from Victoria-square. Stockton Heath, at 9.30 a.m.</p>
        <p>"Buffs" Entertain the Ladies. The "Tom Sangster" Lodge Order of Buffaloes held the annual ladies' night on Friday, when about 100 were entertained. The chair was occupied by the "Right Honourable Sir" Thos Davies, assisted by his Worship the Mayor (Bro. Peacock), and " Sir " William Bradbury (the Provincial Grand of this Province). A splendid progra.tame was gone through until the interval, when. the ladies were entertained to refresh. ments. Afterwards the remainder of the pro- gramme was carried out, the artistes being: Mr. and Mrs. Saunders, whose humorous sketch and beautiful singing was greatly appreciated; Messrs. Handley and Brobbin, concertino solo- ists, whose rendering of "Poet and Peasant" was well applauded; the Misses Lawton, Mr. Dutton, Mr. Kerridge, and Mr. Gorse, all of whom contributed beautiful singing. The accompanists were Bro. Fred Wolfenden and Primo George Lomax.</p>
        <p>Four-Minute Meeting. Four minutes! That was exactly the length of time it took to conduct the business at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Insurance Committee on Thursday evening, held in the Town Hall, Dr. Joseph presiding. It was merely a formal meeting, the minutes of the various committees being confirmed without question.—At a meeting of the Finance Com- mittee on April 7th, accounts to the amount of .22,252 lls. id. were passed.—At the same meet. inga letter was read from the Divisional Officer for Unemployment Insurance indicating that there was no possibility of the Board of Trade being able to grant office accommodation for the Insurance Committee in the hew premises (the old Empire Skating Rink), inasmuch as the new premises had been designed to actually fulfil their requirements, with a small Margin foi times of exceptional pressure.—The Clerk was instructed to make inquiries with regard to any premises in the town which he considered suit. able.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Vicar.</p>
        <p>The parishioners of St. Peter's Church recog- nised, in a tangible manner, the 21 years' ser- vice of their Vicar (the'llev. B. Percival) in the 'John-street Schoolroom on Monday evening. The presentations took the form of a purse con- taining £25 in gold, and a beautiful case of cutlery.—The Vicar's Warden (Mr. T. S. Steel) presidej, and in the course of his opening re- markVsaid that all in St. Peter's parish had extended a good feeling towards Mr. Percival, who was.a man of broad sympathies and kind- ness.—Mr. J. J. Hands, an old member of the congregation, made the presentation, and said the Vicar, in spite of several offers of new liv- ings, had decided to remain with them. (Ap- plause.)—Mrs. Wroe and Messrs. Greaves, Pad. more and C. Rigby also eulogised the good work done by the Vicar and his wife.—ln returning thanks, the Vicar said there was no such thing as an eight-hour day for a clergyman, who had to be at the disposal of people at all hours of the day and night. The sick had the first claim, then the communicants and the congregation who regularly attended the church, and then the whole parish.—Mrs. Sephton, Mr. Attwell and Mr. Bibby contributed to an excellent musical programme, the accompanists being Mrs. Greaves and Mr. Padmore.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>An Honoured Servant.</head>
        <p>An Honoured Servant.</p>
        <p>An interesting event took place in the private office of Cockhedge Mills on Monday afternoon. Mr. Ziba Armitage, having come down specially for the purpose, handed to the cashier, on the anniversary of his entering into their employ- ment, a handsome gold watch and chain, the former bearing his monogram on the back, and the following inscription: "Presented to Mr. Peter T. Winskill, on April 6th, 1914, by Armi- tage and Rigby, Ltd., on his completion of 40 years' efficient and faithful service, as a token of their respect and esteem." In handing the gift, Mr. Armitage expressed the satisfaction of the directors, congratulated the recipient on his length of service, and hoped that many more years might be added to those already gone. Mr. Winskill thanked the directors for theii great generosity; for the kindness manifested on very many occasions; for the happy relations which had always existed between the heads of the firm and the general office; and, lastly, foi the pleasant working between office, manage- ment and employes. The managers, Messrs. C. H. Hibbert and J. Ainsworth, joined in very cordial wishes for the future. Mr. Ralph Armitage added his congratu- lations, and Mr. S. Rigby Armitage intimated his appreciation of what had been said; and hoped for the long continuation of Mr. Win. skill's connection with the mills.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 11, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 11, 1914.</p>
        <p>Solicitors' Examination.</p>
        <p>At the recent Solicitors' Intermediate Exam- ination held by the Law Society, Mr. F. 0. Stansfield; articled clerk with Mr. J. Longland, was successful in Law and Accounts.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Sale of Work. An attractive sale of work, an annual function in connection with the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, will be held on the 22nd of April. Th( Mayoress will perform the opening ceremony.</p>
        <p>New C.L.B. Sergeant-Major. Sergeant-Drummer H. Goodwin, of St. PauPs Company, Church Lads' Brigade, has been appointed Battalion Sergeant-Major. He has filled every rank from private.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Musical Success.</head>
        <p>Musical Success.</p>
        <p>At an examination of the Associated Board of the Royal Academy of• Music and the Royal College of Music; recently held in Liverpool, Miss Gwen Bennett, daughter of Dr. Bennett, passed in the primary division. •</p>
        <p>Old English Fair. A meeting of the Old English Fair Committee of the Warrington Infirmary Centenary Million Penny Fund was held on Wednesday night when it was decided that the fair be held in Victoria Park on July 18th, 17th and 18th, on the lines of the previous event.</p>
        <p>Honour for a Warrington Man. Mr. J. McNichol', a Warrington man, was installed President of the Manchester Junioi Gas Association at the annual meeting on Sat- urday. Mr. McNichol' has been assistant go:: engineer at Warrington since 1906. His pret.i. dential address occasioned much interest amongst the members of the association.</p>
        <p>Buckley-street P.S.A. The monthly meeting of the above was held on Sunday. The chair was occupied by Mr. J. Richardson. The speaker was Mr. W. Tipping, of Liverpool. Two solos, "Lean upon Jesus" and "When the lilies I consider," were well ren- dered by Miss B. Ball. Mr. G. H. Cox was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>Cheshire County Council Year Book. The County Council Year Book for Cheshire, which Mr. Reginald Potts has just issued, fox 19144915, is a handy little volume containing every detail connected with the administration of Cheshire affairs. Lists of members, officials, magistrates, committees, and meetings, and the reproduction of by-laws and the like, give every. information for the convenience of those con- cerned in any way with the business of 'the county.</p>
        <p>For the Widows and Orphans. The annual tea and•concert promoted by the Warrington branch of the National Union of Railwaymen in aid Of the widows' and orphans' fund, was held last evening in the Friars' Green Schoolroom, when a large number were pres- ent. Conncillor Henshall presided at the con- cert and entertaining items were contributed by Miss H. Howell, Miss C. Rigby, Mr. W. H. Rigby, Mr. Rutter, and the St. Ann's Juvenile Choir. Mrs. Forman was the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Working Men's Committee: The New Year.</head>
        <p>Working Men's Committee: The New Year.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the newly-formed Work- ing Men's Infirmary Committee was held at Toft's Assembly Room on Wednesday night, Alderman Burton presiding over an attendance of more than 60 members.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. J. H. J. Hampson, the honorary secretary, reported that the Infirmary Governors had passed a vote of thanks to the committee and the workers of the town for the handsome sum they subscribed last year to the funds of the Infirmary. Mr. Lionel Fairclough had written to say that the Musical Society would give an open-air concert in aid of the committee to- wards the end of June; and fifteen choirs of the town and Crosfield's Band fiad consented to give an open-air musical festival in the Park, Sun- day, August 16th. It was decided to ask the Mayor of Warring- ton, year by year, to be President of the com- mittee.</p>
        <p>-Messrs. Daintith and T. Wadsworth gave theit experiences as members of the Board of Man- agement, and said they had found everything at the Infirmary conducted in the best possible manner, there being nothing but praise from patients for the way in which they were treated. Mr. Wadsworth said the best ward in the build- ing was still empty through lack of funds. The other wards were full, and it was difficult to think how they would Cope with an accident on a big scale.</p>
        <p>_ The Executive Committee was re-appointed. Mr. George Walker analysed the statement recently issued by Dr. Fox and stated that tfie figures quoted by the Doctor proved that there were many people wha required attention whc, were not insured. Even those who were insured in ease of accident needed the Infirmary. They, the working men of the town, recognised this, and were still willing to give of their best fof the good of the Institution they had at heart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>COMI L LOR, PLEASE!" WORKERS MID THEIR MOVLMOITS.</head>
        <p>COMI L LOR, PLEASE!" WORKERS MID THEIR MOVLMOITS.</p>
        <p>An Amusing Interlude Between Councillors Roberts and Pemberton.</p>
        <p>WATER ENGINEER'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>A proposal to increase the salary of Mr. Gray, the water engineer, was discussed at the War- rington Council on Tuesday, and this led to another lively interlude between Councillors Pemberton and Roberts. Councillor Roasnrs moved the Water Com- mittee's minutes, which recommended that Mr. Gray's salary be increased by the sum of £25 per annum, with three annual increments of £25 each.</p>
        <p>He said in 1894 Mr. John Ross, the then engineer, received .8400 with certain allowances, and the undertaking now was of a far more comprehensive and important nature, and yet Mr. Gray was only receiving .8300. When Mr. Ross was engineer the income was £14,000 a year, and now it was £30,000. Mr. Gray's re- sponsibilities were much greater than those of his predecessors.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON OBJECTS. Councillor PEMBERTON, while not wishing to say anything detrimental to Mr. Gray, thought it 'was not fair to draw the inferences which Councillor Roberts had drawn. -Although the undertaking was so much less in Mr. Ross's time it paid a jolly good dividend to the shareholders. The capital expenditure on the undertaking had been enormous, so enormous that the committee -put up the water rates, and there had been no „return to the ratepayers of Warrington all the years they had had it until last year, out of a capital of £400,000, with a monopoly and no opposition. Yet the miserable amount of .C. 500 was paid to the ratepayers. (Councillor Gough: "Shame.") He wanted better results, and he was sure no business man would be satisfied with such a return. When they had better results he would give praise where it was due. "AN EXTRAORDINARY SPEECH."</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS said he did not know how to reply to such an extraordinary speech as that made by .Mr. Pemberton, who was one of the members of the committee and was present when the matter was discussed. He was one who agreed to the unanimous vote of the com- mittee in favour of the increase of salary. To compare the undertaking of to-day with the undertaking in Mr. Ross's time was about as big a farce as any man could be a party to, and Councillor Pemberton had made as arrant a dis- play of ignorance that night he could possibly have made. It was true that the water under- taking had paid a good dividend, but on what capital? Councillor PEMBERTON : On a great deal less.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>"KNOWS NOTHING ABOUT IT."</head>
        <p>"KNOWS NOTHING ABOUT IT."</p>
        <p>C0111;;;110-1:-ROB-EBTS : Of course, it was -a great deal less, but if you know anything at all about the undertaking you must know that the Cor- poration had to buy the undertaking, not on the basis of its capital value, but of its dividend earning capacity, and what the waterworks of that day did was to screw every possible penny they could out of the consumers of water and give you as inefficient an undertaking as possible consistent with their responsibility to the town. And they carried out that policy until they were able to pay maximum dividends, and not till then did they come along and say " We are pre- pared to sell our undertaking to the Corpora- tion." Mr. Pemberton knows nothing about the subject. Councillor PEMBERTON : Councillor, please! (Loud laughter.)</p>
        <p>"COUNCILLOR, •THEN."</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: -do-uneillor Pemberton, then. He talks about a monopoly and levying a water rate. We don't levy a• water rate at all; we never did. There is no such thing as a water rate in Warrington. If Councillor Pem- berton understood the meaning- of terms and words he would use the right words in the right place, but he always uses the wrong ones at the wrong time. (Laughter.) There is a charge for water, but it is on the assessment oh the annual rateable value of pro- perty. It is quite true that we• have a' mono- poly, and that no one else can supply water, but there is nci mifrtopoly in the sense that we are able to charge what we like. If Councillor Pem- berton knows anything at all he knows that our income is restricted by statute. Councillor PrintEsToN : Hear, hear.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: He is everlastingly "grousing "—if you will pardon a slang word— because we cannot make it better. (Hear, hear.) Councillor PEMBERTON : Yes, hear, hear. "HE IS SUCH A—"</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: He is such a-- . Councillor PEMBERTON : Hear, hear. (Loud laughter.) Councillor ROBERTS: He is such a magnificent</p>
        <p>Continued in preceding column</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>OUR PREMIER WAR MINISTER</head>
        <p>OUR PREMIER WAR MINISTER</p>
        <p>THE TRADES COUNCIL. The Question of Labour Representation Discussed.</p>
        <p>WAKES WEEK SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Several important matters were discussed at a meeting of the Trades and Labour Council on Tuesday night, when Mr. Randles presided.'</p>
        <p>LABOUR REPRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>The Executive Committee recommended the adoption of the following resolution relating to Labour representation "The committee recommend that we invite the co-operation of the Independent Labour party and the British Socialist party, with a view to 'obtaining working class representation on all legislative and administrative bodies." Mr. Charles Dukes (secretary) said the corn- mittee4had gone thoroughly into the matter and recomilaended the resolution, seeing that, in all probability, the three bodies concerned would be contesting the next municipal elections, if they did not go even further, and they therefore wanted to get unanimity amongst the ranks. If it was agreed a common programme would be decided upon. A halfpenny a month would be sufficient to pay for the expenses. In Ear lestown they paid 6d. per half-year per mem- ber, but it would not be necessary to go so far as that in Warrington. The men they chose for the contests would not be seeking for honour, but work. (Hear, hear, and applause.) The resolution was unanimously carried.</p>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE DELEGATE. A letter was read from Mr. Plinston, the Council's delegate on the Education Committee, regretting his inability to attend on account of business London, especially so as it was the Council's dc.,:re to hear his attitude with re- gard to the caretakers' grievances. Mr. Shimmins said he thought Mr. Plinston had had long enough notice to attend. He moved they go on with the business and come to a conclusion, so that they oTild instruct Mr. Plinston how to vote. Mr. Hewitt secoudFd...</p>
        <p>Mr. Helsby stated that Mr. Plinston was away on the business of his employer. The proposition was withdrawn on hearing that explanation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>TARIFF REFORM INVASION.</head>
        <p>TARIFF REFORM INVASION.</p>
        <p>..... . . _ A letter was read from the Tariff Reform League offering to give a lecture upon one of a list of subjects. . Mr. Shimmins proposed that a lecturer be invited. Mr. Grierley moved an amendment that the letter lie on the tabre for the reason that a statement in the letter about the conference of 81 trade unions was a tissue of falsehoods. The men who were supposed to be at the conference had been seen, and they stated they knew noth- ing about it. If a body would do such things they would stoop to anything, and they ought not to hear their man. That party was not for the working men. Did they believe that the funds from the £l,OOO banquet were for the Iworkers? "The whole thing is a hoax," de- clared Mr. Grierley, with emphasis, "and I object to them coming down here to hoax us." (Loud laughter, and bear, hear.) Mr. Helsby seconded. He was convinced that Tariff "Reform" was a red herring, and that it would be absolutely a waste of their time and the lecturer's time. (Hear, hear.) It was no use trying to change the fiscal system, it was the wage system they wanted altering. (Hear, hear, and applause.) The amendment was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>MARKET WORKERS.</head>
        <p>MARKET WORKERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor, in proposing a resolution asking the Markets Committee to have the Market closed at 8 o'clock on Wednesday and 10 o'clock on Saturdays, said that they could not expect the shops to close early if the Market was allowed to be open until after 12 o'clock on a Saturday night. (Hear, hear.) Most of the hands employed in the Market were young girls, and it was not only a shame that they should work so late, but it-was damnable. The trades- men were asking the Mitrkets.eommitten to close the Market at 10.30, but the assistants were pressing for half an hour earlier. Mr. Grierley seconded, and said it was a scan- dalous shame. Ten o'clock was quite late enough; in fact, he thought the assistants were: very considerate in stating 10 o'clock. The resolution was unanimously zassed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>WAKES WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE WAKES WEEK.</p>
        <p>The report of the delegates to the conference at the Town Hall with reference to Wakes Week was given. Mr. Dukes said if they took the aggregate earnings of the workers they would find it was depleted by short time, but if the employers knew beforehand when there would be a holi- day they would have the men working more full time to make a stock which would be disposed of during the holiday. The workers would not lose any money, by the proposed holiday. Many of those at the town's conference seemed to over- look the fact that a holiday was a human neces- sity. Some employers seemed to consider which would be the best time for themselves, and never considered the workers' standpoint. Eventually it was decided that they should strongly demand the holiday, and that the dele- gates should try either to have Walking Day included in the week, or as an alternative that Walking Day be added to the holiday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>TRAMWAY MEN'S WAGES. , Committee Offer Id. Per Hour for Lost Sunday Wage. DISCUSSION IN TIIE COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TRAMWAY MEN'S WAGES. , Committee Offer Id. Per Hour for Lost Sunday Wage. DISCUSSION IN TIIE COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Tramways Committee 'at the Town Coun- cil on Tuesday recommended an increase of £2l and £26 respectively in the 'salaries of the eleIC trical engineer and tramway manager. They recommended id. per hour increase in the wages of the tram drivers and guards, in consequence of the loss incurred through the curtailment of the service on Sundays. Councillor Broadhurst moved that the matter about the men's advance be taken back. The men asked for id. per hour, and had been offered Id. The reduction in the Sunday service had made a serious loss to the men 4-anging from 4s. 3d. to Bs. 6d. per month. He was surprised that councillors, who at election time posed as the friends of the workers, could vote in com- mittee for Id. increase. They would not dare face the electors and say they had done so. They were giving handsome increases to the officials, and yet offered Id. per hour to the men who made the profits. (A Voice: "No, they don't.")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>CHEESE-PARING POLICY.</head>
        <p>CHEESE-PARING POLICY.</p>
        <p>• • ---- - - - Councillor Chrimes seconded, and said id. per hour advance was little enough. Councillor Gough said he could not under- stand the committee adopting a cheese-paring policy as regarded the men, but when it was a case of an official they acted differently. Councillor Osborne said a great deal could be said for the suggestion to send the matter back. At the committee meeting he had stood out for an advance of id., as he felt the men were fully entitled to it. As they had increased the offi- cials' salaries he felt the men were entitled to some consideration in the matter. (Hear, hear.) Councillor Pemberton said the men well de- served another id. per hour. Councillor Dickenson said the men were losing 4s. 3d. a month, and yet they had advanced the officials' salaries 10s. a week. A COMMERCIAL UNDERTAKING.</p>
        <p>Councillor Lindley said the fact seemed to be lost sight of that the comrdittee were in charge of a commercial undertaking. They paid their tramway employes as well as any similar under- taking in the country. The id. had been given to recompense the men, who were now only working six days instead of seven days per week. Their men were paid well up to, if not above, the average pay for tramwaymen in Lancashire. Councillor Joseph said the committee had con- sidered the matter most carefully. They had the fullest information, and found that the drivers were paid up to the average for Lanca- shire and the conductors were paid more than the average. The men, through the Sunday change, were losing about is. 3d. per week, and the id. advance would produce is. ed. to is. 9d. per week, which made it better for the men. Finally he withdrew the minute in regard to the men, and the officials' advances were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>STRIKE AT FLETCHER'S. When Peace Seemed Assured a Hitch</head>
        <p>THE STRIKE AT FLETCHER'S. When Peace Seemed Assured a Hitch</p>
        <p>Arises.</p>
        <p>RE-INSTATE IiENT TROUBLE. , At the end of six weeks the strike at Messrs,. Pletcher, Russell's works continues. Certain conditions, as reported below, have been ac- cepted by the men, but now the question of recognition has been overcome, the trouble of re-instatement has arisen. Early on Wednesday morning the Strike Committee, in a letter to the firm, asked for another interview, but the reply stated that "they (the firm) did not see any need for a further conference," as they had done all they could to bring about an amicable settlement.</p>
        <p>The firm have posted the following notice in front of the works: "The works will be re- opened on Tuesday morning, April 14, at nine o'clock. Men will be wanted for all depart- ments. All applications to be made at the titne office. The directors hope to be in a position 'shortly to re-instate the majority of the men." Late on Thursday the Strike Committee issued a manifesto calling upon all the strikers to be loyal to their leaders, and to form a strong picket body on Tuesday morning. Last night the men were reported to be de- termined on the question of re-instatement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>MAYOR INTERVENES.</head>
        <p>MAYOR INTERVENES.</p>
        <p>• There seemed to be prospects of a settlement of the strike on Tuesday, when the Mayor (Councilor P. Peacock) used every endeavour. Re interviewed the men's leaders and proceeded to the firm, when the fallowing new conditions of settlement were offered :</p>
        <p>Any complaint must be made in the first place to the foreman. Failing settlement, a deputation of three men in the shop will be seen by the foreman. Again failing settlement, the deputation 893 es to the manager. If the matter in dispute cannot then be settled, one of the three men will be seen, along with an outside official, representing the particular shop involved, by the manager of the works and a director.</p>
        <p>At a mass meeting of the men on the same night, at which the above conditions were ac- cepted, Mr. Dukes said unfortunately there were conditions which he thought did not bring thorn any nearer to a settlement. Mr. Hutchison said that if the terms were ac- cepted the firm would re-open the works next Tuesday, but they could only find immediate employment for 109 men—(loud laughter)-50 moulders and employes in the other depart- ments_ according to that proportion. " The_ point we have arrived at," said Mr. Hutchison, " is : Are you prepared to start work next Tues- day to the number of 109?" (Loud cries of "No, No.") A resolution, stating that the men would not resume work until they could do so en bloc, was passed unanimously. A hearty vote of thanks was passed to the Mayor, on the proposition of Mr. Dukes, for the efforts he had made to bring about an amicable settlement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>SON FOLLOWS FATHER. Remarkable Record of Two Warring ton Fire Fighters. UNITED SERVICE 017'84 YEARS.</head>
        <p>SON FOLLOWS FATHER. Remarkable Record of Two Warring ton Fire Fighters. UNITED SERVICE 017'84 YEARS.</p>
        <p>The Turner family of Warrington closely rivals the Tozer family of Birmingham as fire- fighters. While the latter have had three gener- ations as heads of the Fire Brigade, with a united service of 90 years, the Turner family have now two members who have put in no less than 84 years as firemen. This is a record of which Warrington is proud, for it is very rare to find a son following hip father as chief, and, so far as can be ascertained, this record has only been equalled in the case of the Tozers in Bir- mingham.</p>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell. THE NEW SUPERINTENDENT.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday, Mr. Frank Turner was unanimously elected Superintendent, on a twelve months' trial. - '</p>
        <p>He las had 31 years' service in the brigade, is an active and intelligent officer, well versed in all the modern phases of fire-fighting, and is popular with the members of the brigade, as well as with the general public. He is Well known as an old Rugby footballer. • At the same meeting the resignation of Mr. Turner, sen., was accepted with regret. Fox 53 years he has been engaged fighting Warring- ton fires, and has had narrow escapes from injury. He has been appointed Inspector of Fire Appliances.</p>
        <p>Alderman BURTON said no soldier in the British Army had rendered more faithful and more valuable services than "this old'fire fighter" for the last half. century. They wished him good health and hoped he would be spared for many years. (Applause.) Alderman BENNETT said it gave him quite a shock when he realised that Mr. Turner, sen., was 80 years of age, because he seemed to be so young and energetic. He hoped he would live to be a nonagenarian. (Hear, hear.) Alderman SMETHURST referred to Mr. Turner, sen., as "a wonderful old man." A better "fire fighter" no one could wish.</p>
        <p>---- ' -5,-''.5,•?,'- A --'",":, ,-;--;--„q"--,i4„,,,, 40A /Ifr. x fe4-' oat*. ~111 .. . ' f ,' 0 0 Zi # # 90 0 ~. f .- , .. . ,': ‘ ,• „` 1000- .„</p>
