      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>THERMAL WATERS AT LE ZOUTE.</head>
        <p>THE THERMAL WATERS AT LE ZOUTE.</p>
        <p>A l miLt iai en sze or of flt Zoute spring n woe waters recently sht yebr:e*de Vale,n hd ai vs ci oßnygoeytr eahtde, Chateau Guyon and Pongues les Eaux. A care- ful analysis of the mineral waters at "Le Zoute Knocke," made by the Doctor Van der Berghe, shows that by its composition, the large propor- tion of bicarbonade of soda and chloride of sodium, places it in the first rank of alcaline table waters. The beneficent action of these two mineral salts on the digestive organs is well known, and the general effect of the Le Zoute mineral water is tonic, digestive and purifying for the blood. We may therefore confidently predict that with the valuable asset of its mineral spring, the popular seaside resort of "Le Zoute Knocke" will rapidly rise to the rank of a first- class summer watering-Place. It is already well known all over Europe as possessing one of the finest 18 holes golf courses on the Continent, with plenty of natural hazards and a comfort- able club house that takes some beating. A second course is about to be completed, and will be ready for play for the 1915 season. There is quite a numerous British colony residing permanently at this popular summer resort, and an English Church has recently been built for the convenience of residents and visit- ors alike. An exceptional season is promised for the summer of 1914, CHARLES KNOTT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>gx.l„</head>
        <p>gx.l„</p>
        <p>St. James' Church.</p>
        <p>The choir of St. James' Church last night gave a splendid rendering of Stainer's " Crucifixion." Mr. E. Davies presided at the organ, and the following soloists dfsposed of their parts in an exceedingly able manner : Messrs. Dobson, Pevitt (senior), Holbrook, J. Tatlock. Paget, Eckersley and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Billiards League: A Tie.</head>
        <p>Billiards League: A Tie.</p>
        <p>The Conservative Seniors and the Conserva- tive Juniors have tied for the "Harold Smith" Challenge Cup in the first division of the War- rington Billiards League. The deciding match between these teams will be played at the Liberal Club next Thursday at eight o'clock, when all members are requested to attend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Shakespearean Players.</head>
        <p>Shakespearean Players.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Amateur Shakespearean Players- will, in the week commencing 20th of .April, present on alternate nights "Much„._Ado About Nothing" and "Merry Wives of Wind- sor," in the Royal Court Theatre. The com- panies will include Miss Gladys,- Adanis, Mr. Charles D. Law, and Mr. Ryder Boys.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>BUrma—Leperland.</head>
        <p>BUrma—Leperland.</p>
        <p>Last night an interesting lecture was deliv- ered ill connection with the Foreign Missionary Anniversary at 80ld-street Wesleyan Church by the Rev. A. H. Beatall, the deputation, on "Burma—the Land of Lepers and Rubies." Mr. C. E. Parker, who presided, said the general opinion was that Burma was like the rest of India, and its people much the same as Indians. That was an erroneous opinion. There were fundamental differences, which Mr. Bestall would explain. The lecturer dealt with the customs of the Burmese, and gave a graphic account of what the missionaries were doing , for them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Good Friday.</head>
        <p>Good Friday.</p>
        <p>Good Friday was quietly observed in War- rington yesterday. A number of citizens pviceeded to the country by motor, horse 'bus and other vehicles, Lymm proving a favourite resort as usual. Appropriate cinema films were shown in the PPodrome and other places of amusement, e antt, thewhHic ih were fairly well patronised. The booking office clerks at the stations were evening kept very busy. At the Central Station the bookings were very good for Manchester, Liver- pool and New Brighton. A large number took advantage of the excursions to Grimsby, Hull, Gainsboro' and Cleethorpee, and on Thursday night about 30 left for Douglas, which is a good number at this time of the year. At Bank Quay, also, bookings were brisk. Blackpool was the principal resort patronised, and about 70 persons booked for week-end and long date. There were. not many bookings to London, but the "local" trains to Frodsham, Lymm, Preston Brook, Zic., were well filled, a large number going to Frodsham, where, on Overton Hill, there was ulna gaiety. Services were held in Established, Roman Catholic and Nonconformist Churches, and there were good congregations. '</p>
        <p>CO UN C I LLOR, PLEASE '—continued.</p>
      </div>
