      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>ACQUISITION OF LAND.</head>
        <p>ACQUISITION OF LAND.</p>
        <p>BY HORACE E. CRAWFURO,</p>
        <p>Head Organiser of the Urban Land Inquiry. •'ln Part 11. of the Urban Report the com- mittee deal with the conditions under which • Land is acquired. The first chapter is occupied with a discussion of the compulsory acquisition by public and quasi-public bodies of land which is required by local authorities to carry out the duties which have been imposed on them by Parliament for the provision of the various public services. Thus, the supply of an 'adequate and efficient system of drainage means that land .must be had for sewage works. To provide a town with water it is necessary to acquire land where reservoirs can be constructed and the water gathered. The niunber,of places suitable for this purpose is limited and confined to particu- lar districts, and it often happens that the water must be conveyed many miles to a point where it is wanted. Here again, land, and easements under the land, are necessary in order to lay the pipes to convey the water. . Gas works, electrical stations, fire stations, . police stations, municipal offices, &amp;c., must all be accommodated on land in a suitable position. The provisions of the Housing Acts, too, have imposed many duties on local authorities for the performance of which land is essential.' ROADS AND OPEN SPACES. One of the most pressing needs to-day is the opening up of new roads and the widening of many existing thoroughfares to cope with the increased bulk and greater speed of modern traffic. Equally important is the provision of ' parks and open spaces and of more and larger playgrounds fur children, particularly in the larger towns. For all these purposes, local authorities need land both in town and country.. The Government Departments, too—particularly the War Office and the Admiralty—find it neces- sary to acquire land for various purposes con- nected with the defensive forces of the country. Apart from public bodies, there are others of a semi-public nature which provide what are, in effect, public services, and which are armed with compulsory powers for the purchase of the necessary land. _Chief among these are the rail- way companies. Other bodies of this kind in- 'elude gas companies and water boards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>COMPULSORY POWERS.</head>
        <p>COMPULSORY POWERS.</p>
        <p>- As the duties carried out by all three bodies are in the interests of the public, the machinery for obtaining land compulsorily should be as simple, cheap, and expeditious as possible. The first step in the process is to get the com- pulsory powers, and this forms the first section of the chapter. Formerly, in order to obtain oompulsdry powers, It was necessary in each - ease to go direct to Parliament and obtain a special Act for the purpose. Later, certain Gov- ernment Departments were empowered to make Provisional Orders for the compulsdry purchase _</p>
        <p>of land which, when contested', had to be con-</p>
        <p>firmed by Parliament by the same procedure as in the case of a Private Act. The necessity for the confirmation by Parliament of such a Pro-. . visional Order has been—dispensed with under a f few recent statutes—such as the Housing, Town Planning, &amp;c., Act, 1909, in the case of small areas, the_ Small Holdings and Allotments Act, 1908, and the Development and Roads Improve- ment Fund Act, 1909. In spite of the modifications indicated, for most purposes compulsory powers can still only be acquired by means of a Private Act or a</p>
        <p>Provisional Order confirmed by Parliament. This procedure, involving passage through a Committee of each House, the payment of fees, 013Portunities for obstruction and opposition, and the necessity for having plans, maps, Sm., ready at a particular time of the year, is costly and tedious. It is recommended that in the majority of cases it should not be necessary to have recourse to Parliament at all; hut that local authorities should be able to acquire land either simply by resolution, or with the consent of the appro- priate Government Department, and that only where the particular scheme involves large or novel issues should if be necessary to go to Parliament for a Special Act. Even then the procedure for obtaining such Acts should be greatly simplified. ••,- - •</p>
        <p>WHO SHOULD FIX THE PRICE When the compulsory Powers have been ob- tained, the next step is to fix the price or com- pensation to be paid for the land. The Report sets forth the procedure under the Lands Clauses Consolidation Acts by which, in most , cases, this compensation is fixed. Then after presenting a mass of evidence, drawn mostly : from-official and professional sources, the Report makes a number of recommendations for reform •• in the law. The conclusion is arrived at—that a tribunal consisting of arbitrators appointed by the two parties, with an -umpire, selected by agre„wot ' between them, is unsatisfactory in its nature, and still more unsatisfactory owing to the costs , incurred when each side has, in addition to its - arbitrators, a number of expert witnesses and counsel. The suggestion made is that in all , C3SeB where the compensation to be paid can- not be settled by agreement, the amount should ' , be fixed by the Judicial Land Commissioners. • THE BASIS OF COMPENSATION. ▪ Again,' the basis on which compensation is " fixed is unfair. At present the basis is defined a.t the "value to the owner"—that is to say, it •</p>
        <p>is nominally the loss which the owner incurs, or may incur, by reason of the land being taken from him. This loss is held to include not only the present market value of the land, but also the future value which may arise from a num- ber of causes which are exceedingly problem- atic in their working. The belief of the committee is that compen- sation should be assessed on the basis of market value, and they point out that this would include any future rise in value probable enough to be taken into account by the ordinary purchaser. The method suggested of arriving at this basis is that adopted for making the. valuations for total value nada- the .Pinitn..,e, (1909-10) Act, 1910. PURCHASING IN ADVANCE.</p>
        <p>At present, if a municipality wishes to buy land it. has to define exactly the purpose for which the land is required, and, save under eiceptional. circumstances, it is not-allowed to acquire more land than is wanted for that pur- pose. Hence a municipality is never able to buy land when it is at a low value and reap the benefit of any increment that may accrue from the activities of the municipality itself. Nearly always it has.to wait until the value of the land has gone up, and so invariably purchases at a higher figure. It is recommended that local authorities 'should be given a general -power to acquire land in advance either to use it them- selves for public purposes as necessity arises, or to lease it to other persons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>PRIVATE INDIVIDUALS OR COMPANIES.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE INDIVIDUALS OR COMPANIES.</p>
        <p>The committee also consider the problem of private individuals or companies who wish to acquire land for industrial development or for housing, and are unable to secure it at a reason- able price, or, as happens in some cases, to get any lancFat all in a suitable and convenient position. As is pointed out, there is a difference between giving compulsory powers to bodies who are compelled by statute to rrl•ride services for which land is required, and tlo private indivi- dual who wishes to acquire land.for a purpos.e which will result in a profit to himself. At the same time, the development of land for industrial or housing purposes is, of course, beneficial to the community. The authors of the report feel themselves unable to recommend that any powers should be given to private individuals for the compulsory purchase of land belonging to any person; but they suggest that where it can be proved that the applicant is unable to acquire other land suitable for his purpose, and that the use to which he proposes to put it is in 03 WILY pre- judicial to the public interest, the Land Com- missioners should be enabled to give him com- pulsory powers for the leasing of the land. Moreover, as stated above, the local authority will be able to buy land and let it to private individuals. This suggestion extends to the grant of easements of all kinds and to the com- pulsory granting of mining leases, as well as the right to prospect for minerals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>EFFECT OF. THE RECOMMENDATIONS.</head>
        <p>EFFECT OF. THE RECOMMENDATIONS.</p>
        <p>In general, the effect of the recommendations is to aim at cheapening and simplifying the whole.. process by which land .is acquired com- pulsorily by public bodies and providing that 'compensation for land acquired for these pur- poses should he on the basis of the market value.. Secondly, a new departure is made by recommending that private individuals should be given compufsory powers .under certain con- ditions for getting land which is needed for developmevit, either industrial or commercial, or for the building of houses.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 11, 1914.</p>
      </div>
