      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>DISTRUST OF RELIGION.</head>
        <p>DISTRUST OF RELIGION.</p>
        <p>Dr. Stanley Mellor and Religions Life of the Age.</p>
        <p>MATERIALISM DEAD.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the successful past which the Warrington P.S.A. can claim, its leaders are ever- on a forward march, striving to make its influence a real and powerful asst to the town. In accordance with their forward movement, they had made last Sunday an open Sunday, and in consequence there was an encouraging at. at the Park Picture Palace, where the service was held. Mr. W: H. Sherburn pre- sided, and he was supported by the Rev. Dr. Stanley A. Mellor and Mr. Clegg, the secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>HOPEFUL AGE.</head>
        <p>A HOPEFUL AGE.</p>
        <p>_ The CHAIRMAN said they heard many people say they were living in a degenerate age. There were indeed many signs that gave colour to such an.idea if the person's outlook was not broad enough, but on the other hand there were a number of things which seemed contrary to that idea. He thought it was a hopeful age. It seemed to him that. the one thing Dr. Mellor had tried to do during the short time he had been in the town was to get hold of young men and women and lead them to think for them- selves, and not instil into them any dogma. They, had •on their hymn-books • _the word "Fellowship." Men and women realised the power of that, word, for great numbers were to be found meeting Sunday after Sunday iv. their different .assemblies. If fellowship was prac- tised and understood in every department ; of life, the world would soon be what they wanted it to be. (Applause.) • ;</p>
        <p>DISTRUST OF RELIGION. Dr. MELLOR said they would all have to admit that there was in the world at present a wide- spread distrust of organised religion. Despite what the Chairman said-about the numbers who gathered together ,Sunday after Sunday, it was nevertheless a • fact that there was an extra- ordinary amount of distrust of religious institu- tions and churches and organised religion gener- ally. Churches, after all, were not crowded; there Was generally -room for plenty of people, whilst on an ordinary Sunday there was, per-. haps, too much room.</p>
        <p>The membership of the great religious organi- sations showed a decline, so that the subject was worth a little consideration. Why was it that men a,nd women did not turn to religion in the sense of eager zeal one might expect? There were, of course, many extraneous reasons: People said it was a pleasure-loving age. So it was, but for his own part he saw no harm in pleasure if taken moderately, and he had no 1 sympathy with those who said life should be un- pleasant. Quite a lot of religious people were miserable and when he encountered them he always doubted their religion. People said that their Sundays were becoming demoralised and degenerated, and that they did things which their forefathers would not think of doing. They played golf or went away for the week-end; others went in their motor cars, and •those who could not afford motors went fishing. '</p>
        <p>Search for Fresh Air. If the reason was considered they could not blame those people for that kind of thing. If a man had been in the office or workshop for six days out of seven, it seemed very natural that he would search for fresh air. He did not think that was a reason for the distrust or the decline, they could understand it better if they looked at the great changes which had come over thought and life during the last fifteen or twenty years. There was a great gulf fixed between the 19th and 20th centuries. They thought differently froha the men of, say, 25 years ago; indeed, he sometimes wondered how elderly people man- aged to survive .nowadays, for some of them received so many shocks to their nervous system. (Laughter.) The present was an age of unrest, but it was a curious thing that; on the whole, modern life and thought was peculiarly favour- able to religion. There was nothing irreligious in the present situation if one, saw it broadly and 'deeply enough, for .religion, as he under- stood it, depended upon two or throe fundarnen- tal- convictions. It .depended upon the belief that the universe was spiritual and meant good; secondly, that the deeper thoughts of man were primarily those, of the things that mattered, that life was not a heaping of material goods—it was the spiritual thing that matteted. The best thought of the day in science and philosophy agreed with that.</p>
        <p>Materialism bead.</p>
        <p>Materialism, 'which was a popular creed some years ago, was now dead and done with. No intelligent man who knew what he was talking about_ and who professed to have any genuine education was to-day a materialist. In science it would be laughed out of court, in philosophy it would not have a place. To profess material- ism was to profess ignorance. The present un- rest was ft demand for a freer and fuller life, a more satisfying and personal life, free from the enslavement of lower things. If modern thought was so encouraging towards religion, why was there that distrust? People were growing tired of formalistic and creed- bound religion, and did not like e'religion which contradicted ,science or stated that the Bible was infallible. Religion had got to be a genuine, Personal life, and had to rise above dogma and creed. Then again there were many good people who believed that the organised churches on the whole were, and always had been, on the side of the privileged class. There was a great deal of justification for that view, and there was no doubt that that feeling was abroad in the world.</p>
        <p>• Leisure for, Religion. The chief reason for the distrust was that men and women were coming more and more to think and believe that religion and everything else in life should flow from a personal basis, and that in our present social System there was very little room for that religion. They said that they should concentrate and get their social evils put right, and then they would have time and oppor- tunity and leisure for religion. Of course, in that very attitude there was something religious. There were things they all ought to realise. They were discovering what it cost to run an indus- trial civilisation—not in money, that was noth- ing—but in the sacrifice of leisure, fineness and beauty, not to speak of the sacrifice of health and strength demanded by a materialistic way of living, and they were deciding that the price was too high ,and the goods they received not worth while.</p>
        <p>_ A revolt against practical materialism to-day was not an academic, but a democratic thing, which belonged not to the study, but to the workshop. Unrest meant a kicking against the burden of materialism—a refusal on the part of the awakening spirit of personality to any longer submit to slavery beneath dead external- isms and materiality. Genuine religion needed leisure. The worship of God could not be pushed into- a corner, crowded into a few odd moments out of a life devoted to other ends. A true religious man would work to amend the social evils of the day.</p>
        <p>Creeds and Dogmas. Personally he thought that the apparent hos- tility to religion would pass away, but with it would go the creeds and dogmas of organised churches. Men would find that even for their social work, they needed religion, and as their social work succeeded and -achieved its end, so -their chance and opportunity for religion would become greater and greater. Meanwhile they had to make religion free and fine—a genuine personal thing. Those who did that could regard the present situation with Out misgiving and with good hope for the future. (Applause.) The P.S.A. orchestra, under the conductorship of Mr. J. Hannan, rendered in pleasing style a couple of selections which were accorded hearty praise, whilst Madame Jennie .Armstrong's con- tribution was full of merit:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>MANCHESTER SMOKE.</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER SMOKE.</p>
        <p>Manufacturen' League to Attack the Nuisance. It is proposed to form a league among the manufacturers in the Manchester district for the purpose of increasing the efficiency of, and diminishing the smoke emitted from the boiler plants of its members. Over twenty manufac- turers have already" expressed their interest and have promised to attend the first meeting. Such a league is already in existence at Ham- burg, and the. Manchester Smoke Abatement League urge public-spirited manufacturers in the Manchester district, who have difficulty in keeping their chimneys smokeless, to band to- gether in this way for their own and the public good. "We do not deny for a moment that our object in advocating this is to secure that the Manchester atmosphere -shall be improved, but we feel convinced that manufacturers would at the same-time securedirect pecuniary benefits."- _</p>
        <p>"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>TOWNSHIP 'I II I LE-TATTLE.</p>
        <p>The tramp season has arrived. The "Weary Willies" have Visited the Heath. They have, however, found the police alert. Inspector„Breqe•and his merry men Have almost a national reputation - .For the adroit way they handle such folk. They are now living up to their reputation. Motor cyclists invaded the district on Satur- day. It was the first spin of the Warrington motor cyclists. , The "meet" aroused a great deal of interest. They repeat the "meet" on Easter Monday. The Parish Council's annual meeting takes place net week. It promises to he a very peaceful meeting, Called to elect the chairman and overseers. Garden implements have teen in request. Good Friday and Easter Monday are great Days with the Heathen amateur gardeners.</p>
        <p>Parish Council—The annual Meeting of the Parish Council will take place in St. Thomas' School on Wednesday: The -business will .be chiefly tormal,liamely, the election of chairman for the ensuing .year .and the appointment of o erseers for the parish.</p>
        <p>Sleeping Out—The police are keeping the maundering community under observation. •At an Occasional Court on Tuesday, James Hop- kins pleaded guilty to sleeping out at Whitley Superior and was ordered to " prison in" for seven clays.</p>
        <p>Tramp Visitors.—ln consequence of the nuis- ance tramps create by calling at both front and back doors of the Heathens'"" houses, the police have received complaints, and one of the offenders, who stated that his name was John Magarth, was charged at an Occasional Court on Wednesday with begging in Gaskell-street. Stockton Heath, and was sentenced to seven days' hard labour. .</p>
        <p>"Alleged Assault.—At an Occasional Court Walter Payne, a tramp, was charged with sleep- ing out in an outbuilding at Preston on th' Hill. It was stated that a man answering the descrip- tion of the prisoner stopped a man in Chester road, Stockton Heath, the previous night, and aftel making a futile attempt to obtain money, is alleged to have severely assaulted him.—Pris- oner was remanded to the Petty Sessions on Tuesday next, when he will be charged with the assault.</p>
        <p>Pest Sentenced.—At the Chester Quarter Ses- sions on Wednesday, Thomas Lewis, of no settled abode, was sentenced to twelve months' hard labour for objectionable behaviour in the pres- ence of a lady at Stockton Heath on January 20. He was committed-for trial from the Stock- ton Heath Petty Sessions of February 10th, as an incorrigible rogue, under the Vagrancy Act. The prisoner had previously been convicted, under the name of James Crane, at the Liver- pool City Police Court, for being a rogue and vagabond, and sentenced to three months' hard labour.—Mr. R. Harrison, instructed by Messrs. H. Greenell and Co., prosecuted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>TO REMAIN IN CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>Fine Victory in the Great Boundary Inquiry.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE WITHDRAWS CLAIM.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the Runcorn Board of Guardians and Rural District 'Council reported On the county. boundary question at the meeting at Dutton on Tuesday. He said that on the last day the Clerk to the Lancashire County Council said that when he opened the case on Wednes- day the evidence he then had before him led him to the conclusion that area No. 1 required fur- ther sanitary attention, and that the adminis- tration of the area could be better and more con- veniently administered by the Rural District Council of Warrington than by the Rural Dis- trict Council of Runcorn, but he was bound to confess that the evidence given on behalf of the Cheshire County Council and the Runcorn Rural District Council would not, in his opinion, jus- tify him in further pressing upon them the de- sirability of the Local Government Board deal- ing with the proposed alteration of the bound- aries as regarded that area. He therefore begged, with the inspector's permission, to with- draw the application as regarded No. 1 area. The Clerk added that on the Monday morn- ing areas two, three and eleven were withdrawn. Area two was the remainder of Thelwall.</p>
        <p>SATISFIED WITH THE RESULT. The Chairman We have to congratulate our- selves that this momentous piece of business has taken such a turn in our favour. It may have appeared a small matter to many members of the Guardians, but if they knew the ins and outs of the matter they would realise that it has taken those in charge of our affairs all their time to run this in the right direction. It would have meant a great deal to us' if we had lost, whereas now we have the whole of our area intact. Mr. Linaker, sen., said he was at the confer- ence all the time, and the case presented by their counsel on the instruction of Mr. Ashton was a very complete one. Mr. Ashton had gone to a great deal of trouble to get all the data to lay before the inspector, and there was not the least doubt that the evidence given by Mr. Diggle and Mr. Ashton completed the rout which finally took place. The Lancashire County Coun- cil came to curse, but finally blessed them. It was rather lamentable that district councils which had done their duty should be penalised to have to go to such a large expense to defend their rights. He thought they ought to get something to compensate them for their trouble. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>MR. KAyrs “STAGGERER." Mr. Lister-Kaye said he was one of the un- fortunates who sat there for four daze. It was entirely due to the clear case Mr. Ashton got up for counsel that they won the case. Whenever a question required asking counsel was always ready to ask it. Mr. Linaker : Mr. Kaye very modestly spoke just now, but I think you should know that he put a very pertinent question which was a stag- gerer to the Lancashire County' Council. Mr. Wood, one of their witnesses, giving evidence, said that he was sure that if area' No. 1 were under the charge of the Lancashire County Council or the Warrington Rural District Coun- cil, it would not have taken all this time to renledy the nuisances there. He thought it would have been remediedlong since. When he had been cross-examined, Mr. Kaye received an answer in the affirmative to the quesqon he put as to whether lie could ask a question. Then Mr. Kaye said: "You seem to be familiar with this district and the Lancashire County Coun- cil's and the Warrington Rural District Coun- cil's action in this matter?" Mr. Wood an- swered "Yes." Mr. Kaye then said, "You are quite sure if this area had been under your jurisdiction the nuisance foil state would have •been remedied long _sirl6('?" Mr. Wood an- swered "Yes," and then Mr. Kaye finally said: "Then, can you tell me how it is that the gross nuisance at Padgate which has-existed for the last 20 years remains unabated." (Laughter.)</p>
