      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>Late Mrs. W. Groves.—The funeral of the late Mrs. W. G, Groves, who was highly esteemed in Lynam, took place on Ifonday.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Social.—The members of the Wes- leyan Choir held their annual social in a pret- tily-decorated schoolroom on Monday evening; Games, music and refreshments were thoroughly enjoyed.</p>
        <p>A Rector's Departure.—ln the current issue of the Oughtrington Parish- Magazine, the Rector, the Rev. E. P..Wetherect, confirms the announce- ment of his acceptance of the living of Stayer- ton, in Northamptonshire.</p>
        <p>Church Anniversary.—lli,gher-lane Baptists celebrated their church anniversary on Sunday, when the Pastor, the Rev. E. E. Lovell, ocCu- pied the pulpit both morning and evening. At the latter service Miss Ina Jenks, of Warring- ton, was the soloist.</p>
        <p>Election Result.--The result of the 'Contest in the Heatley Ward was declared•at the Council Offices on Saturday, when Mr. W. Mullard; the returning officer, announced the llgures as fol- lows: Mr. Septimus Critchle,v, 211; Mr. James Jolley, 89. The two candidates, thanked their supporters, after which'Mr. C. E. Cowper, the new member for Booth's Hill Ward, expressed similar gratitude for his election.</p>
        <p>"Olivet to Calvary."—After the Rev. E. Mor- gan had -conducted. a short preliminary servlee in the, Wesleyan Church on Sunday evening, an augmented choir rendered Maunder's "Olii-et to Calvary." The soloists were the Misses Pear- son and Winstanley, and Messrs. R. Stewart (tenor) and G. Smith (baritone). Mr. Jr: Brazendale .was -the conducter, and Mr. H. S. Brown the organist.</p>
        <p>YOUNG LADIES' ENTERTAINMENT</p>
        <p>A Successful Congregational Effort. The young ladies of the Congregational Sun- day School held a very successful entertainment on Monday night in aid of the school funds. The arduous duties of training the performers ' had been carried out by Mrs. F. P. Cooke, the pianist for the evening. Concerted songs were given entitled "Grandmothers of old," "House maid sweepers," " Suffragettes " and "Take a little tea." The song by the three Quakeresses, Misses A. Miller, F.-Sutton and J. Skelhorn, in which they were joined by Messrs. J. Hewitt, G. Willan, and E. Weikel', *afl so well received that an encore had to be given. "The shadow on the blind" was the title of a,clever recitation by. Miss Annie Miller. A garland dance was contributed by eight girls, in which Miss Elsie Miller was the soloist. A solo 'dance by Miss Irene Cooke was encored.• Popular dialogues were "An unexpected fright" and "Apartments to let," the former contri- buted by the Misses Ada Herrington, Alice Ker- foot, Annie Radcliffe, Lora. Seymour, Jessie Skel- horn and 'Alice White, angel the latter by Misses Maggie Knowles, Elsie Miller, Cissie Statham, Annie Webb, and Blanche White: Refreshments were served during the interval.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>'WEDDING OF MR., JAMES WEBB. A Pretty Spectacle in a Manchester • Church.</head>
        <p>'WEDDING OF MR., JAMES WEBB. A Pretty Spectacle in a Manchester • Church.</p>
        <p>• FULL LIST OF PRESENTS. A wedding of exceptional interest to Lymm and Heatley people took place at Cheetham Hill Wesleyan Church on Wednesday, when the con- tracting parties were Mr. James Webb, jun., only son of Mr. and MTB. James Webb, of Had- ley Hill, Heatley, the hon. secretary of the Lymm May Queen Committee, and Miss Ada Wragg, youngest daughter of the late Mr. George Wragg and Mrs. Wragg, of Cht.ethain Hill, Manchester. There would be about 400 persons present to witness • the ceremony, at which the Rev. F. W. Shirley was the officiating minister. • -</p>
        <p>The bride, who looked charming in a gown of ivory charmeuse, trimmed with orange blossom and carrying a sheaf of lilies, was given away by her brother, Mr. Walter Wragg. The brides-. maids--:Miss liditli Viriagg and Miss Florence' Burrows—were dressed in faded rose charmeuse, trimmed with grey brocade. Mr. H. Sedgewick acted as best man, with Mr. G. C. Lovett and Mr. F. Cowsill as groomsmen. Mr. J. H. Far- rell presided at the organ. • The Heatley B.P. Boy Scouts, with whom Mr. J. Webb, jun., was for several years a Scout- master, were in attendance as a guard of honour, under Scoutmaster T. Nash, and formed an archway with their staffs as the bridal party left the church. The bridal party and guests, numbering about 70, partook of the wedding breakfast at the Cheatham Hill and Broughton Oddfellows' Hall, after which the happy couple left for London, where the honeymoon is being spent. The guests spent an enjoyable evening in speech-making, singing and dancing. Mr. and Mrs. J. Webb, jun., are to take up their residence at lleatley after the honeymoon.</p>
        <p>THE WEDDING PRESENTS. The following were amongst the presents re. ceived</p>
        <p>Bride to bridegroom, Jay seal; bridegroom to bride, cameo ring; bridegroom to bridesmaids, pendants and chains; bride's mother, sewing machine and household linen; bridegroom's mother, silver tea and coffee service and carpets; bridegroom's father, purse to bride, cheque to bridegroom; Mr. and Mrs. J. H. Farrell, inlaid overmantel ; Mrs. and Miss Abbott, trinket set; Mr. and Mrs. W. G. Jones (Heatley), toilet set; Miss S. Hoskinson, trinket set; Miss F. Burrows, eiderdown; Miss E. Wragg, table cover; Mr. G. C. Lovett, cheque; Mr. A. Davis (Heatley), pair of blankets; Miss L. Thomas (Southport), d'oyleys; Mrs. Barnes (Heatley), satin table centre; Master J. Jones, hand-painted pin cushion; Mr. R. Cheltenham, table; Miss L. Burrows and Mr. W. Rowlands, china inlaid bowl and servers; Mr. and Mrs. T. Horrocks, tea and dessert spoons; Mr. and Mrs. Tonswell (Sale), crumb tray and brush; Misses D. and L. Warburton (Heatley), jam jar and spoon; /Aim. Bateman, rose bowl; Mr. F. Eaton, silver flower vaies; Mr. H. Sedgewick, tea service; Mr. J. Stokoe (Oxford), ash and pin trays; Mrs. Codman (Wellington), Coalport china; Mr. F. Cowsill, couch; Miss A. Webb and Mr. C. Thompson, household utensils; Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Webb, clock; Miss N. Hobson (Clifton), table centre; Miss M. Sykes, glass centre piece; Mr. J. and Miss M. Wragg and Mr. Critchley, fish-eaters; Miss N. and Mr. F. Brookes, silver fruit dish; Misses L. and D. Hobson (Manchester), china and d'oyleys; Mrs. A. Kershaw, satin cushion cover; Mr. G. Wragg, knife machine arid picture; Heatley B.P. Boy Scouts, silver egg cups and stand; Mr. A. B. Edwards, photo frame; Nurse Dunbill, cheque; Rev, G. J. and Mrs. Lovett (Chorlton), parcel of household goods; Mr.-W. Partington (Higher Broughton), cheque; Mr. and Mrs. 3. W. Lockwood, cut- glass decanters; .Mr. and Mrs. Walter Wragg, silver and glfiis centrepiece ;_Miss Hardstaff, jam tray and spoon; Mr. and Mrs. W. Partington (Whitefield), oxydisefl plant pot; Mrs. Austin, photo frame and table cover; Councillor T. War- burton, clothes basket.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—The usual monthly devotional meeting was held on Monday evening. A 'WY thoughtful paper was read by Mr. Davey-Smith on "Why pray?" The toll was called by MT. H. Tomlin, and a solo, "Rock of Ages," was ren- dered by Miss Ward. Mr. W. E. Plinston, Who presided, voiced the thanks of those present to speaker and soloist. • Recital.—ln the Wesleyan Schoolroom on Sat- urday evening, Miss Gertrude M. Sproston, Of Leeds, gave a dramatic and humorous recital. The proceeds were in aid -of—the church and Sunday school funds, and--a large audience assembled in anticipation of a treat. These ex- pectations were fully reali§ed, and it is quite safe to prophesy that if :Miss Sproston should ever visit Padgate again She may rely on a large. and appreciative gathering. Songs were ren- dered by the Misses .Mason and • WinstanloY. 'Miss Weaver presided at the piano, in the place of Miss M. L. Sproston, who was unable to be present, though " billed" to appear. The chair was occupied by Alderman Bennett, who ex- plained that the entertainment had been genet- Indy provided by a well-known member of the ,urch. Mrs. John Smith. At the close the Aairman responded on behalf of the artistes and himself to a rote of thanks..</p>
        <p>1 0118 'chi CIL</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Tunstall.—Mrs. Tunstall, of 1 Victoria-road, Stocks-lane, passed away on Monday morning after a long, lingering illness of eleven weeks. A native of Crouton, she has been.a resident in this district for a number of years.</p>
        <p>A Narrow Escape.—The second son of Mr. Harry Burtham, the well-known sportsman tenant of the Ferry Inn, Fiddler's Ferry, had a narrow escape on 'Wednesday morning at San- key Bridges. A/3 far as can be gathered the lad was closely foflowing a motor lurry, and at- tempted to cross the road without making sure that no vehicle was approaching in the opposite direction. Consequently the unexpected hap- pened, and he found himself nuclei. another motor. Bruised and shaken, he was quickly re- moved to the Infirmary and attended to. Later in the day he was allowed to proceed home.</p>
        <p>Farm Fire.—A fire occurred on Thursday in a hay loft over some stables belonging to Mr. Walter • lielsall, - farmer, 'of Warrington-road, which had caught fire from some unknown cause. The penketh Tannery Co.'s firemen were quickly on the spot. The Warrington Brigade soon followed with their motor engine, thereby increasing the strength of the fire-fighters. The smoke from the loft penetrated some dwelling- houses adjoining, causing great excitement and fear of a further extension of the outbreak. A number of the tannery workmen forfeited part of their dinner hour in lending aid in the re- moval of furniture, Sm., from the threatened premises. The ,tannery firemen returned, after an hour's stay, leaving the Warrington -Brigade in charge. This is the first fire outside the borough at which the motor engine has attended and also. the first fire since the new superin- tendent was appointed. The damage is esti- mated at about £l5O, and is covered by insar- ance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>SCHOOL ATIILETIC SPORTS.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATIILETIC SPORTS.</p>
        <p>Close Contests in Some Events.</p>
        <p>A long series of events in connection with the Penketh School wAre carried out in a character- istically business-like manner on Saturday afternoon. Great credit is due to all the offi- cials conceined, both in regard to-the working out of the programme and to the efficiency of the competitors, some of whom are very pro.. fusing subjects. s Many of the events were con- tested in a remarkably keen manner. Owing to the length of the programme, several races were postponed until the following Monday after- noon. The officials were as follows: Judges, Miss F. D. Priestman, Miss A. M. Rutter, G. J. Baker; starter, T. H. Brunyee; referee, R. J. Malcolm; timekeeper, C. B. Thorne; marksman, C. D. Wilkinson; clerks of the course, Edith Escolme, Ethel Eckersley, Marjorie Gresty, Ridgeway, Kelsall and Davy.</p>
        <p>The winners were:- ' Throwing the Cricket Bell.-Senior Boys: 1, Hardern, 65yds. 3ft.;. 2, Kelsall; 3, Reniso'n. Junior Boys: 1, Lumby, 53yds.; 2, Punchon; 13, Seddon. •Senior (flrls 1, D. Shipley; 2, M. Gresty. Junior Girls: 1, P. Parker; 2, E. Kinder; 3, A. Kelsall. 100 Yards, Junior Boys.-1, Leather, 131 sec.; 2, Andrews; 3, Lumby. 100 Yards, Senior Girls.-1, M. Gresty, 14isec.; 2, J. Griffiths; 3, I. Seddon. 100 Yards, Senior Boys.-1, Cosway, 13sec., scratch; 2, Davy; 3, Bransby. 109 Yards, Junior Girls.-1, D. Mannington, 151 sec.; 2, E. Hill and F. Norcott, tied; 3, A. Kelsall. Long Jump, Junior Boys.-1, Punshon, lift. Bin.; 2, Lumby; 3, Andrew. Quarter-mile, Junior Girls.-1, D. Manning-. ton, lmin. 331 sec.; 2, P. Parker; 3, D. Lean. Long Jump, Senior Oirls.-1, M. Gresty, lift. 61in.; 2, 3. Griffiths; 3, V. Thompson. . Quarter-mile, Senior Boys.-1, Cosway lscr.), lmin. 91sec.; 2,. Bransby ; 3,, Bate. Skipping Race, junior Girls.-1, C. Gresty; 2, 1). Mannington; 3, E. Hill. Long Jump, Senior Boys.-1, Davy, 14ft. lin.; 2, Cosway; 3, Kelsall. Long Jump, Junior Girls.-1, C. Gresty, 10ft. lin.; 2, P. Parker; 3, A. Kelsall. Tripod Race, Boys, Open.-1, Davy and Lumby; 2, Andrews and Mills; 3, Bransby and Benison. . , ' Quarter-mile, Senior Girls.-1, J. Griffiths, lmin. 251 sec.; 2, V. Thompson; 3, I. Seddon.</p>
        <p>Quarter-mile, Junior Boys.-1, Leather (60yds. start), lmin. 12see.; 2, Cooke; 3, Andrews.</p>
        <p>Potato Race, Junior Gir D. Mannington; 2, A. Kelsall; 3, P. Hartley. High Jump, Senior Boys.-1, Kelsall, 4ft. Sin.; 2, Hardern; 3, Palmer. High Jump, Senior Girls.-1, M. Gresty, 3ft. llin.; 2, V. Parker; 3, V. Thompson. Winner a good, clean juniper. Railway' Race, Junior Boys.-1, Hartley- 1., 2.Ransoine; 3, Marques. Skipping Race, Senior Girls.-1, M. Gresty; 2, V. Parker; 3, J. Griffiths. High Jump, Junior Boys.-1, Lumby, 4ft.; 2, Seddon; 3, Andrews i. High Jump, Junior Girls.-1, D. Lean, 3ft. 51n.; 2, C. Gresty; 3, R. Arannington. Quarter-mile, Senior Boys.-1, Cosway (son); 2min. 50sec.; 2, Brdnsby; 3, Renisoti. . Tripod, Senior Girls.-1, M. Parkinson and L Seddon; 2, D. Mannington and H. Price; 3, J. Griffiths and E. Escolme. Railway Race, Senior Boys.-1, Bransby; 2, Hardern ; 3, Palmer. Potato Race, Senior Girls.-1, V. Thompson; 2, J. Griffiths; 3, M. Gresty. Tug-of-War.-Penn's team won. Handkerchief - Race, Girls. - Captain M. Gresty's team. The sports were continued on Monday, in 'boisterous weather. The results were:- Quarter-mile Race, Junior Boys.-1, Cooke 1 • ~ : ~ 3min. lsec•; 2, Lumby; 3, Hoicroft. Mile Race, Junior Boys.-1, Cooke i 6min 26sec.; 2, Lumby; 3, Holcroft.</p>
        <p>Mile Race, Senior Boys.-1, Cosway, smin. 55sec.; 2, Bransby; 3, Palmer. Relay Race, Boys.-1, Fox's; 2, Fry's; 3, Penn's. Wheelbarrow Race, Boys.-1, Cosvtay and Mills; 2, Davy and Ransoms. School Champion.—Cosway. House Champions.—Penn's, 95 points; Fry's, 33 points; Fox's, 30 points.</p>
