      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>World's Tribute of interesting</head>
        <p>The World's Tribute of interesting</p>
        <p>Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSIIIG.</p>
        <p>Observations made in sections of the world where ,there are many wireless stations indicate that birds are disturbed in a singular way by the wireless waves, says the Mardi "Popular Mechanics Magazine." If is stated that gulls are apparently the principal sufferers, but that also large numbers of doves are in some way prevented from finding their way home when there are wireless stations in the line of flight. This strange phenomenon is attributed to some effect of the ether waves not yet understood.</p>
        <p>Champion Boxer's Winnings. The lucrative nature of the boxer's •profession when the boxer is a champion is strikingly in- stanced in the case of Georges Carpentier, who, though barely out of his teens, has already won U 6,500, the "Daily- News" says. In 1909, when lie begaittp he won £36; in 1910, £100; in 1911, £1,320; in 1912, £7,000; in 1913, £8,000; and this year it is asserted that his winnings will be higher still. The Press reckons that if Car- Pentier had remained a miner's apprentice at Yens he would have had to work about 197 years at the rate of seven francs a day to earn this sum.</p>
        <p>- • Bayonet Fencing a Soldier's Sport. Fencing with bayonets is the most popular as well as the most dangerous game among soldiers in the Regular Army. Those who participate must be strong and skilful, and the game is not one for weaklings, says the March "Popular Mechanics Magazine." The regulation outfit is a Ask, heavily padded coat and padded gloves. The bayonets used are tipped with rubber, but despite all these precautions, men are often hurt in the set-to. Each regiment has its champions, selected by a process of elimination from the various companies. When these champions meet on one of the field days, often held at brigade posts, the rivalry is intense.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Wrong Man Operated On.</head>
        <p>Wrong Man Operated On.</p>
        <p>A hurried Melbourne doctor, faced with many )ut-patients and tantalised by agreeable social invitations, made a brave rush through his work the other day. One of the "outs," a stolid Chinaman, was hurried On to the operating table for post-nasal operation. The Chinaman (says the "Sydney Bulletin") protested in pigeon English all the way. Nevertheless, the opera- tion was performed swiftly and neatly. It was only after the 'bandages were finally fixed that doctor and nurse discovered John to be merely a pal of the real sufferer, come to help his brother through the hospital ordeal: •</p>
        <p>• . A Grocer's Revelation. • A: Bill to prevent married women charging whisky in grocers' accounts as butter and eggs !las been introduced in the New York_ State Legislature by Mr. Henry Schiedemann, a mom- her of the Progressive party. The Bill, said Mr. Schiedemann to a " Daily Mail" correspondent, strikes at the very foundation of the high costs of living. "I am a grocer, I know what I am talking about, and I say deliberately that women habitually deceive their husbands by Making them pay for bottles of whisky billed as coffee, eggs, or butter." The Bill declares the sale of Whisky by grocer illegal.</p>
        <p>Umbrellas for Hire. Brussels is giving a lead in an interesting business. A company has been formed, with a capital of £200,000 for the purpose of lending umbrellas. A subscriber pays a premium of 4s. a year, and in return receives an aluminium disc. A rainstorm suddenly comes up while he is on his way to the office. Instead of making for the nearest shelter he drops into the first shop lie comes to, be it a cafe, a restaurant, or a tobacco shop. He presents his aluminium disc and receives in return an umbrella. When the shower is over, all lie does is to step into any shop displaying the sign of the umbrella organ- isation and turn in his " gamp," in exchange for which lie receives another disc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST —continued.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST —continued.</p>
        <p>circumstance fit the temper of a people( or it is simply an encouragement to cant."</p>
        <p>The Intemperate Bow-Wow. A story which was current in Heywood when I lived in the Rochdale district may appeal to a certain prominent member of the Warrington Board of Guardians. A person who was in re- ceipt of out-relief and who was under strong suspicion had just carried into her house a big jug of beer, when a local clergyman was. seen ap- proaching, accompanied by his little dog. The jug was popped under the table, and as the parson talked his dog drank the contents. Sub- sequently he remarked upon the animal's un- usual behaviour, and, as ' he was unable to account for it, he called for expert opinion. The " vet." declared the 'dog to be unmistakably in- toxicated, and ,as he and his master had only been at the house of the lady who was at the moment bemoaning the loss of her beer, the latter soon found her allowance from the com- mon fund stopped. Eli? No, it is not a fish story; it is a: dog story of the truth of which I have repeatedly been assured: However, just as you please. Puss, Puss. Now it is the cat's turn. I once lived in a neighbourhood where several of the cats had un- usual names. One of them upset a window box of mine in the night, and the havoc thus wrought set a little girl next door speculating as to the identity of the offender. • Said she to me, "Do you think it'll be Topsy Helliwall?" "I don't, really," I replied; "you see, Topsy is a nice, well-behaved cat, and never stops out all night." " Well, then," she ruminated, " I think it must be either Aubrey Greenwood or Fred Sutcliffe." "Who's Fred Sutcliffe?" I asked, for I happened to know Aubrey. " Have you never seen a nasty, dirty-looking pussy come round this corner—a sort of brown?" said she. I confessed I had noticed it. " Well," said she. "that's Fred !" Then she told me a wonderful tale about a neighbour's cat named Billy Howard. Billy, she explained, had grown so old (" Father William," indeed !) that the family agreed the time had Come to destroy him. They arranged for a man to call on the following day to take him away. Billy was present all this time, but, of course, nobody thought he was listening, and certainly they did not notice that he looked sadly and reproachfully from one to another. After a while he went to the door, and the daughter of the house, in letting him out, murmured "Poor Billy ! It's the last time." And it was. William saw to that. He took good ear a not to go back, and was never seen there again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>WORLD OF LETTERS. CHILDREN'S CORNER • Conducted by UNCLE JACK. k.)=-9-442torwawitooemovoltgat' ewtovolgar..,</head>
        <p>THE WORLD OF LETTERS. CHILDREN'S CORNER • Conducted by UNCLE JACK. k.)=-9-442torwawitooemovoltgat' ewtovolgar..,</p>
        <p>Xly Dear Children,— You are all on your holidays now, so I shall not trouble you with a long letter. I hope you will have a splendid holiday. . . Your loving - UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>THE CALL OF THE</p>
        <p>By E. S. Fleming.</p>
        <p>Smdding before the wind in a blaze of sunshine</p>
        <p>. weather, White sails billowing softly, bearing the boat along, We breathe from the winds the scents from the gardens of Araby blowing, As, cutting the waves in twain, we list to the ocean song. .</p>
        <p>Far, far from the golden West is the glamour of fancy calling - Over the restless waves, in a message loud and free. . From far, in the sca-gull's cry, a wonderful tale•is winging, A tale of alluring wonders, adventure, and mystery.</p>
        <p>Hear how the foam-spray whispers, and mur- murs its gentle pleading, Come, let us away to the land that is free and far! Swiftly over the deep our bark will bear us thither, Guided through day by the sun, through night by the pale North Star.</p>
        <p>Alas! RS longing we linger, the chains of</p>
        <p>memory bi-nd-us. Voices break in on our dreamland, calling in</p>
        <p>tones cf woe. Now-, through the last faint gleams, and the zephyrs about us sighing, Hie we again to the shore in the sunset after- glow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>AN ADVENTURE WITH A BEAR.</head>
        <p>AN ADVENTURE WITH A BEAR.</p>
        <p>In a backwoods township of the Western I States lived two pioneers, Strong and Everest, and their families. Early in the autumn of 1776, when there was as yet no sant, the two men had to go to -a town some distance away for proviSions. Pioneer women seldom worried, but for saint reasoa Molly Strong felt uneasy after her husband had gone: She and her sons got in a good supply of fuel, and finished their work early.</p>
        <p>When they came back to the cabin, the baby was crying for his supper, and Mrs. Strong gave Urn a cup of 'warm milk, and sat him down in front of the fireplace. She had just swung the kettle from the fire, when- she heard a noise. Looking round, she saw the blanket that served for a door swing, aside, as a great bear thrust her head into the room. The children sereanied. and the. bear baeked out in haste, but Molly Strong knew that she would return. "Quick, children !" she said, as she caught up the baby. " Climb the ladder. Let sister go up first—now Johnnie now Frank hurry hurry !" When the others were safe in the loft, Mrs. Strong climbed up With the baby, and drew the ladder after her. She laid it across the hole, and then she and the children sat down and waited. The floor of the loft was made of round poles laid closely together, but not fastened; it was dangerous to move about on it. Peering down through the pole floor, they lis- tened and watched, for the bear to come back. They could hear her moving round the house. and once a big paw.;craslied through the oiled oaper ,at the window- Ito] e. Finally, e. came to the door, and, after blinking uncertainly at Lire fire, walked in. Two cub followed her. The old bear presently upset the pan of milk on the table, and the cubs begifn to lap it up eagerly. , "I'd like a taste of that myself," Frank wlds- pered.</p>
        <p>---- "So would I," Johnnie replied. "I'm awful hungry." Next-the bear found the pudding pot, and took 1 mouthful of the boiling contents. Jumping back with a cry of pain and rage, she broke the pot with a swift blew of her paw, and then sat °l) on her haunches, growling and whining, and began to dig the pudding out of _her mouth. The cubs sat and watched her in grave wonder. That was too much for the children, who burst' into laughter. Instantly the bear gave a loud roar, and rushed toward the hole with the ladder across it. Mrs. Strong gave hurried orars :</p>
        <p>"Get me a pole, Prank, 'quick ! Now get another. Punch her if she. tries to climb up. Be careful—don't fall through. . The baby, awakened by the noise, began to wail, and Mrs. Strong soothed him with .one hand, while with. the pole in theother she warded off the bear. The little girl clung, cry- ing, to her mother's skirts; but the boys each had a long pole, and prodded the bear through the rungs of the ladder. The big brute finally ihuffled off toward the door. She pulled down the blanket and tore it into shreds; then went out, followed by her cubs. "She's gone!" Jollllllie said. "Do you think she'll come back, mother?" "I expect she will, son, but we can manage her—if we keep awake." "1 won't go to sleep'!" groaned Johnnie. " I'm too hungry!" Nevertheless, the silent wait in the - dimly- lighted loft matte them all drowsy, and before the bear. returned, Mrs. Strong knew by their heavy breathing' that the children were asleep. It was near midnight when the bear came in and renewed her, efforts to reach the loft. 31 rs. .Strong rapped her sharply_with her pole, and there was a roar that brought the. boys to their feet. Dazed, and only half-awake, Johnnie ran across the loft. His foot slipped into a gap between the poles, and, as lie fell, other poles spread apart. Down he went into the room with the bear and her cubs. The bear did not see him fall, but she faced about at the noise and started toward him.</p>
        <p>----- Quick as a flash Mrs. Strong brought her pole down on the nearest cub, and pinned him to the floor. The bear turned to rescue her squeal- ing offspring, and Frank drew his brother. up into the loft, unharmed. No injury to herself could have weakened the old bear's courage like the attack on her cub, and although at intervals she slouched in and out of the cabin, she made no further attempt to reach the loft. As it grew light, Mrs. Strong,! watching through the chinks in the wall, saw her lead, her cubs across the clearing into the woods..</p>
        <p>As soon as she thought it safe, she lowered the ladder and the family came down. They saw no more of the bear, and when Mr. Strong came home he made the cabin 'secure with a strong wooden door.</p>
        <p>AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>New Books.</head>
        <p>New Books.</p>
        <p>John Calvin. By Hugh Y. Reyburn, B.D. London: Hodder and Stoughton. 10/6 net.</p>
        <p>_ . An adequate, and yet critical, account of the life— tind work . of John, Calvin has long been needed. Calvin has .suffered neglect owing to the association of .the doctrine of Predestination with his name, and the conse- quence has been that the real work he accom- plished in his own lifetime and the immense in- fluence which his system of church government has exerted on the modern world are hardly appreciated by any but historical students. He stands for much more than the theological doc- trines, which are named after him. Though his religious controversies stand out very promi- nently in his life, it is not, in reality, the views he held about Election,, Predestination, the Sacraments, Grace, &amp;c., which are of import- ance in forming our estimate of Calvin's place in history. The important thing for the modern student to note is the place which the members occupied in the government of the church in Calvin's system. Calvin's aim was to return to the purity of the primitive church; and this necessitated an entire reorganisation of the church as it existed in his time. The laity played no part whatever in the government of the church. Calvin restored to them their original position: But the ultimate effect of this was "to lay the foundations of modern demo- cracy," as Mr. Reyburn says. If the authority of the Pope might be disputed and the govern- ment of the church rest in the hands of the people, might they not claim the right to govern themselves in secular concerns? Calvin did not foresee all this, of course, but it is true, never- theless, that the rise and development of modern democracy can be traced to the churches which sprang up wherever his influence spread. But ,! Calvin exerted another influence on the world, ' a moral influence of the first magnitude; and this is the more remarkable, as the effect one might anticipate from the fatalism of his theo- logical creed is exactly the reverse of the one actually produced. As Mark Pattison said, " The religious instinct of Calvin discerned the crying need of human nature to be a social dis- cipline rather than a metaphysical correctness. The scheme of polity which be contrived, how- ever, mixed up with the erroneous notions of , his day, enforced at least the two cardinal laws of human' society, viz., self-control as the foundation of virtue, self-sacrifice as the condi- tion of the common weal. . . It was a rude attempt, indeed; but then it was the first which the modern times had seen, to combine imW vidual and equal freedom with strict self-im- posed law; to found society on the common en- deavour after moral perfection." Mr..Rey-burn's work, we think, meets the need to which we have alluded. His book gives a very full account of the work of the Swiss Re- formers, of whom, of course, Calvin early be- came the leader. He puts us into possession of the facts of the historical situation, and describes, in ailequate detail, the course of events as the work of the Reformation pro- ceeded. The reader, ignorant of the history of the period, is thus enabled to follow the inci- dents in Calvin's life with comparative ease, and with the tolerable certainty of forming a fairly accurate estimate of Calvin's share in the work of religious liberation. We have nothing but admiration for the masterly manner in which Mr. Reyburn has marshalled his materials. Naturally, the greater part of the work pre- sents Calvin in his severer aspect, but Mr. Rey- burn does not omit the more human touches, and it will not be without surprise and some amusement that the reader will hear of Calvin's efforts to Secure a domestic servant for his friend William Farel, or to find wives for various acquaintances. He was always endeavouring to help someone. As Mr. Reyburn says, "There was no end to the service which Calvin rendered his friends; he got tutors for gentlemen's families, he got help for the -destitute, he wrote' letters of introduction for boys going to board- Uig schools. . No one aSked liis advice, his. assist- ance, in vain,, if he were able to give it." .., Mr, Reyburn does not attempt to minimise 'alvin's share in the fate of Sery us, the t inci- dent which has always been con dered as the great blot on Calvin's fame. He ells the whole i story, giving both sides very fairly, and we think his judgment on the matter will Meet with general approval. "That Calvin made a great mistake," he says, "is now admitted and lamented by all Protestants. It is true that Servetus was only one more added to the multi- tude who died violent deaths at the hands of persecutors in the 16th century. But the multi- tudes died at the hands of the Romanists, who persecuted on principle. Servetus died at the . hands of those who persecuted in unconscious contradiction of their fundamental principles. If Scripture is self-evidencing, as Calvin clainis it to be, it does not need to be thrust down any-' . one's throat with the point of the magistrate's sword. And if it is the only authority in mat- ters of faith, it is sheer tyranny on tbe part of any man or body of men to impose any inter- pretation on their fellow believers by the pains , and penalties which are the arguments employed , by the _civil power."</p>
        <p>The last three chapters on Calvin's personal characteristics, work and theology are particu- larly interesting, although in the last one we do not find ourselves in entire agreement with Mr. Reyburn's view of the trend of modern thought. But this is a small matter and does not hi any way affect the value of his book as a Sound esti- mate of Calvin's life and work. 'We hope it will have the wide circulation, of which in every way is is deserving.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>New Music.</head>
        <p>New Music.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Ascherberg, Hopwood. and Crew, Ltd.</p>
        <p>18, Mortimer-street, London. Two Scottish Songs.</p>
        <p>(a) "My Heart is Sair for Somebody." Words by Robert Burns.</p>
        <p>(h) "The Laddie we the sloe-black een." Words by Eliza Cook. Music by Kenneth Graham. Price 1/6 net. The first song is written in key Kfiat, and the second in the open key. The first thing that strikes the peruser of these songs is the com- Plete understanding of the poets' meaning ex- hibited in the music. It seems as though the musician absorbed the poems before be began to clothe the words with the music, thereby giving a fine effect to the melodies. These songs aref very suitable for concert work ald should be- come favourites.</p>
        <p>Dram. de Cur Valse Serieuse-Boston. By K. Walewska. Price is. 6d. net. The opening bars of this valse are very effec- tive in their plaintive minor key; and the stac- cato in thelcommencement gives an additional pleasing effect. The let movement is in the open key and follows with a change into A major, which gives quite a cheery impression. This movement is very melodious and pleasing. The valse goes back again to the original key, and finally closes with a lento minor melody.</p>
        <p>Valse Sombre. By Ernest Adams. Price is. 6d. net. _ _</p>
        <p>This is a wonderfully pretty valse, and one which ought to create a furore. It is written in key F. The opening is very melodious and the harmony alluring. The 2nd movement is in B flat, then changes to the original key,, where the melody is maintained to the end. The composition is distinctly good, and the con- sonance.sweetly beautiful, clearly bringing out the "sombre" effect which the composer evi- dently wished to perpetuate., Both these valses are quite suitable for those who are cognisant with the rudiments of music. and are quite useful studies for practising.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <p>T CAN'T BEAR TO HEAR YOUR CHILD CRY. Keep his stomach in order and you will soon find his temper improved. Next time he -seems irritable, try a Stcedman's Powder. Nothing else is just as good, Observe the Ez in Steedman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Literary Gleanings.</head>
        <p>Literary Gleanings.</p>
        <p>FILMING THE INFERNO.</p>
        <p>" Kinematographing an awakening volcano, at eloso quarters appealed to my imagination," says Mr; Frederick Burlingham in " Pearson's Maga- zine," where he gives a thrilling account of his descent into the crater of Vesuvius. Ho adds laconically: •</p>
        <p>"When I mentioned the matter to Mr. Mc- Dowell, director of the British and Colonial Kinematograph, lie said enthusiastically: Go on; "After looking down the inferno for the first " Burlin but don't get killed time, however, I telegraphed back to him to gim/n: !• insure the camera." His subsequent adventures make hair-raising reading.</p>
        <p>The chief danger was one of asphyxiation,'' he writcs.</p>
        <p>" We•were below the precipitous portion of the crumbling crater wall, and had left all thc rope dangling behind in order to climb back U!) the precipice. We were on a fairly steep inclined slope, filled with loose debris, which' started moving as we slid along and tumbled into the void beneath. 'Almost directly below us were the thick yellow sulphur ftirmaroles, sCceenn the smoke of which already was giving us con- cern, while out in tho crater basin rose still t "This is great:boc column of corrosive central column has no equal. In a semi-circle above us rose hundreds of i hydrochloricncT the secondary furmaroles, silent but appalling in 1 r k e gas. tra n e, their grandeur:</p>
        <p>" Suddenly there Caine 'the sharp crack of an avalanche and the sound of falling debris. Part of the crumbling cone had •caved in 4,000 feet above, and the ruass was leapiag towards us."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>PANAMA CANAL SCENERY.</head>
        <p>PANAMA CANAL SCENERY.</p>
        <p>As a feat of engineering, us a mechanical triumph, the Panama Canal has been heralded throughout the civilised world, but so engrossed have folk been in the stupendous task accom- plished that little attention has been paid to the scenic wonders of the great across-the- isthmus waterway. The thought of the diffi- culties overcome, the gigantic problems solved in the completion of the work, stimulates the imagination; but now that the inner workings of the great locks are to be hidden from view by water, tourists to the Canal Zone will be able to give a greater share of their attention to the picturesque phases of 'Panama and the wonder- ful scenery through which the canal makes it way. In the comparatively •short trip across the isthmus a remarkable variety of scenery is to be observed. For part of the way the banks are lined with low-lying tropical swamps, where the vegetation is all but impenetrable. It is in this ! country that it has been proposed in Congress tc set at liberty a number of pairs of hippopotami, in the hope that they will thrive and multiply. The idea is that the animals will keep the short vegetation cropped off and thereby aid in pre- serving sanitary conditions in certain parts of the Canal. Zone. As they would be protected from sportsmen by law, it .is likely the beasts would gradually become comparatively fearless and might be frequently seen by passengers from the decks of vessels. This would indeed add to the picturesqueness of the scenery.</p>
        <p>• Part of the route lies between towering cliffs and mountains, and at other points rolling hills rise one above the other, partially covered by tropical vegetation and partially barren and scarred with rocks. It will be an impressive scene, the first procession of lead-gray battle- ships going through the canal, and perhaps no less remarkable will be the sight of giant steam- ships being raised or lowered through the locks, which have come to „hold a place among the man-made wonders of the world.</p>
        <p>THE POLICEMAN'S LITTLE EXTRAS. It is usual to refer to His Majesty's seamen as the "Handy Men," but after reading the annual report of the Chief of Police•in Manchester we are disposed to wonder whether the title had not better be conferred upon the police: Here are some scraps of information; In each case of the more than two thousand licences issued to marine store dealers and the 223 certificates given to pawnbrokers, the police have to make preliminary- inquiries. During the year they have served 758 summonses and warrants for other police forces. The 148 com- mon lodging-houses, which house nightly an average of 8,315 persons, are regularly visited by them: They have, in compliance with the re- quest of the Medical Officer of ;Health, reported 133 cases of nuisances noticed by them when on duty; in the coursa of the twelve months they have discovered 7,825 premises 'insecure and un- 35fe, and liberated 177 persons who had been unintentionally locked up in offices and so forth. There were also 4,361 lost children found and restored to their parents, and the police also took charge of 1,474 boxes of warehouse keys which were deposited with them for safety. Lost, wandering, and stray dogs, to the number of 3,129, were 'seized by the ,officers; who also impounded 373 horses which were straying, unfit for work, or in charge of a drunken driver, and rendered first-aid to 3,389 persons who sustained accidents or were attacked by sudden illness in the street or other public places. And these are rely only a few of the little extras which clime in the policeman's day's work.</p>
        <p>THE ANTIQUITY OF THE GREYHOUND. The moment one attempts to deal with the antiquity of the greyhound one is literally em- barrassed by the wealth of material to hand. It is amusing to notice how each young writer on the history of the domestic dog sooner 01 later makes the great discovery that the grey- hound is an extremely ancient variety. There is something quite refreshing in the log she great " find" causes—to one person, at any rate. The discoverer straightway announces the amazing fact that a dog almost identical with a Waterloo Cup winner as 'beloved- by the i Greeks! True, quite true, • oh! industrious searcher in the archives of the IPast; but, since we are dealing with doggy matters, may I in doggy language urge you to "try back." It s true that the dog's graceful form appealed very strongly to the Greeks—they would have admired an English thoroughbred—but they adopting a ck7ce far, far older thant iga n themselves. a-er e o "Try . How far? Now that is the difficulty, because practically every race of people which has left us anything in the form of a record seems to have had the greyhound very much as he is to- day. When one has indisputable evidence that the dog was popular amongst the Assyrians, when an excellent portrait• of a Waterloo Cup type turns up in an Egyptian tomb, it seems somewhat futile to attempt to grope about in the mists of the Has-Peen allY longer. A writer of authority declares that the greyhound has existed for at least seven—or is it eight ? thousand years. We may as well leave it at that, only we must not overlook, the fact that to whatever date is fixed on, must be added an indefinite period during which the breed was being evolved. It is assuredly man-made, not a natural one. Nature does not recognise the type. Shape of body, length of head, ear car- riage, coat, colour, all show the direct influence of artificial selection. It is an arresting thought that the dog fancier of seven thousand years ago knew so much and did his work so well.—Wil- liam North, in "The Weekly Tels'graph."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>RUNCORN COUNCIL ELECTION.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNCIL ELECTION.</p>
        <p>One ward, that of Newton, was contested in connection with the Runcorn District Council election on Saturday. Mr. W. Shaw, jun., ran as the Liberal candidate, end the Conserva- tives ran Mr. T. Egerton, an hotel keeper. Result: Shaw, 267; Egerton, 262.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <p>-</p>
        <p>Why Are Englishmen Beaten by</p>
        <p>_ the Foreigner?</p>
        <p>BY SILVER-STREET.</p>
        <p>' With all the new-fangled methods of printing . . „ intrOduce3 during the last thirty•years, nothing ciin oust P.O.P. as the favourite method of a beginner for printing his negatives. Nine out of every ten commence by buying it, and then as skill is acquired in the printing, the separate chemicals are bought and mixed, and a new pleasure is found in making one's own toning and fixing baths. For, as a rule, they are used separately. The only argument that can be urged against the use of a combined toning and fixing bath is the IA ell-known charge of impermanence. This, L am convinced, says Mr. U. T. Barris in the !' British Journal of Photography," has been too much insisted on. Three or four years ago, after careful experiments, I•threw over the use of separate toning and fixing for postcard work, and have had no cause to regret doing so. The results are every bit as good as with separate toning—indeed, with some brands of cards, better; the time saving in the course of the year enormous, and cards finished in this manner four or five years ago, exposed to a steady north light continuously for months, are still as bright and crisp as when first produced. The only change is that purple-brown cards have gone very slightly warmer in colour. The bath used is: Sodium hyposulphite, 210z5.; acetate of soda, loz.; ammonium sulphocyanide, toz.; gold chloride, 3grs.; water, 150z5. The average time taken to reach purple brown with the cards I use is about tell minutes. Of courte, care has to be exercised when using a combined toning and fixing bath that it is not overworked. I would consider the 'above quantity should tone and lix a little over a hundred cards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>"HAPPY MOMENTS" COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>"HAPPY MOMENTS" COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Kodak, Ltd., have now published the list Of awards in their great cash prize competi- tion for " Happy Moments." A first prize of £l,OOO and £l,OOO divided into 1,000 smaller prizes were offered for the best sets of Kodak snapshots, and the judges were to award the prizes according to the evident -happiness de- 'Acted in the photographs. - The famous company has made a recprd for entries in any competition of this kind, for many thousands of sets were sent in. The Brit- ish judges were Lord Ilawke, Mr. ¶J. P. O'Con- uor, M.P., and Mr. John Hassan, the well-known -Posttr-artist, and perhaps no better three public men could have been chosen whose judgment would be given according to the principles in- sisted on by the authors of the competition. The first prize, £l,OOO, goes to Germany; the two of £lOO each to Hull and to Lille, France. Six prizes of £5O have all gone abroad, ,and out of fifty prizes of £5 each, only five have been won by Britons. The competition was only open, ! be it understood, to residents in the British Isles, Europe and Egypt. This result of a test so much boomed by our own photographic and daily papers, is rather humiliating to us who had an idea that England was the land of photo- graphy. Perhaps "we take our pleasures sadly," after all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>SNAPS.</head>
        <p>SNAPS.</p>
        <p>Under the title of " Photographs for the Papers," a useful little book has been issued by Messrs. A. and C. Black, of Soho-square, W., and written by J. Everard. The hook, price one shilling net, contains a number of half tone illustrations, and deals with the production Of photographic illustrations for the dailies, the weeklies, the monthly magazines .&amp;c.</p>
        <p>megArs. John J. Griffin and Sons, Ltd., Kings- way, London, have now introduced two toning preparations, in tube form, for red to brown and green to blue tones on bromide and gaslight paper. •</p>
        <p>. - Messrs. Griffin, Ringsway, London, are issu- ing a new brush for Bromoil and oil pigment- ing. It is of the dome-shape of pigmenting sur- face found most efficient in oil printing. An addition to the series of stains for prints, introduced by the Vanguard A. Manufacturing Company, Maidenhead, has been made in this preparation, giving a fine and realistic green tint to prints, and very suitable for seascapes,</p>
        <p>moonlight effects, and similar subjects, particu- larly those of strong light and shade.</p>
        <p>IMPROVEMENT IN CINEMA TECHNIQUE. Habitues of the modern picture palace, par- ticularly those who know sontething of photo- graphy, have been pleased to witness a remark- able improvement in the quality of English films during the Past few months. For many years British films have been criticised very severely, as they compared very badly with the. many beautiful examples coming from America, Franco, and efen Italy.- The productiOns of such firms as. the American " Vitagraph " and "AIL" and the cross-Channel " (taumont " and. "'lathe" have long been famous, and when the most perfect examples of them were held up to the British producer as being patterns t.o be fol- lowed, the critics were told that our English climate was against such work, and that while we were handicapped in such a way there could be no hope of equalling across-seas work. Few people, if any, know the vagaries of our climate better than a photographer, and many of us got accustomed to look upon the excuse as a per- fectly legitimate one. • A short time ago, how- ever, at leapt three of the American producing firms planted companies of actors, producers, and operators in our midst, and proceeded to produce comedy; drama and scenic film pictures of equal merit to those the -very same firms turned out in countries ,having a more favour- able climate, while the French firm of Patho started a studio in London and proved to us that the most perfect interior work was possible in spite of our climate.—" British Journal of Photography." •</p>
        <p>Photographers 1</p>
      </div>
