        <p>_ STRANGE STORY OF SUPERSTITION. Japan's bridge of ill-omen has been exorcised of its evil spirit, and no longer is it known as the Bridge of .Strange Disappearance. Hitherto no bride would cross: it, for it was the firm belief that if she did so she would surely be divorced. Recently the Shinto priests came in procession to do battle with the forces of dark- ness. Duly robed and equipped with potent charms, they formed themselves into two parties. One recited prayers while the other Passed to and fro over the bridge, striking with long staffs at the spirits which they alone were empowered to see. This done, the bridge was pronounced " open " and safe for the most timid bride. The incident to which the bridge owes its ill-omened reputation dates from the thirteenth century. In the neighbourhood, says tradition, lived one Nakano, a wealthy but un- patriotic man, wiles° constant fear was that his goods would be seized to swell the national war chest. SO he buried his treasure in boxes by the river, slew the servant who had assisted him, and cast the body into its dark waters. His sin was visited upon the daughter, who, thrice mar- ried, was thrice left a widow. Distracted, the unhappy girl fled from her father's house, but . in crossing the bridge she suddenly disappeared, and was never seen again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>COUNTY COURT CONGESTION.</head>
        <p>COUNTY COURT CONGESTION.</p>
        <p>The committee appointed by the Lord Chan- cellor to inquire into the pressure of work and business in the County Courts of Lancashire and neighbourhood hell its second meciing for the taking of evidence on Saturday, at the Man- chester Assize Courts. The Vice-Chancellor of the Duchy (chairman or the committee) pre- sided. Several suggestions for amalgamating existing Courts were made, and the inquiry was adjourned for a week,</p>
