        <p>in wimple circumstances, sir, n:s no rignt ti tie his son down to his own level. So I don't interfere in his plans. He has done better for himself than ninety-nine young fellows out of a hundred."</p>
        <p>Frorta which I gathered that the &amp;Inc&amp; and hi a parents had not been mutually sub- mitted fur approval. So smart Mr. Johnson, Junior, remained co me 3 mild mystery until one day a Stroll down Bond Street solved it. I was paszifig the shop of a well-known jeweller and stopped, to look at the pretty things in then. window. A carriage with a c*-rone-C on the panel was standing there, and as I was _about to pas on, the gimp door opened and ,an 'in- firm old lady on the arm of a younger one tottered out. I naturally waited to la them cross the pavement in front of me to the door which the footman held open, and as I did so who should I see following. the . bare-headed, and earplug jealously a precious white parcel, but Mr. Johnson, Junior. I in- stiuctively turned back to the' window that he m:ght not recagnise me, although I was not sure whether he knew me by sight. He tithed with his characheriotic importance waging till the ladies should be seated in the carriage, then with a nice mixture of dignity and courtliness he handed in the package, adding a graceful bow as they drove off. I should not have thought much of my chance discovery had it not been that the young fellow and his people had always been 8) careful to conceal his calling. Surely, I thought as I strolled on, there is nothing to be ashamed of in a post of that sort. Young Mr. Johnson did not rise in my estimati?n, and I wondered whether he wai equally reti- cent with the young lady at Clapham. Of course I gave no hint of my discovery to my worthy landlord or his wife; the- unobbish- ness surely did not originate with them. An evening or two afterwards tli 'hansom (lathed up to the paternal door, and I fell to marvelling at the liberal salaries and com- missi-na earned by smart salesmen at fashionable shops. It was the last time that Ih ohwa swagger cab was ever seen in Gainsborough R oad; and in a few days I hal ceased to wonder palolr tduonni et . On that last a t c.. oc- casionit was anep y of a ce quaintance with the glorified young jewe:ler, for havino. occasion to speak with my land- lord, the old man with evident pride tem- pered by his constant respectfulness said, •"This is my son, sir," and intr 'du ce nae. From the few commonplaces we exchanged I found young . Johnson's speech corresponded with las manner; there was reother t()., much 'side.' but this wag toned clovn by a</p>
        <p>certain taste ana tact wnien accountea ler the position he held in that high class Bond Street establishment with its aristocratic custom. This was just the man, not s.‘ easily found in these days of off-hand manners, of Cockney , twang and slang—to -wait -upon a fidgety duchess and submit to her Grace's inspection a choice array of baubles, or with his grand .manner to silence any barg,aining propensities on the part of a millionaire fin- ancier. The man's personality was worth hun- dreds a year to him, and perhaps thousands to his employers. The next I heard of Johnson Junior was in th-e newspaper, and, alas! in that column de- voted to , police :reports. I read the case through as it caught my eye without at first connecting it with my landlord's son. They always spoke of him as Cecil, and this pri- soner's name was g:iven as Charles Johnson. The story of his fall was as f Mows. He had offered in pledge to a pawnbroker in the City a small packet of unset gems. Now unset stones are fairly safe commodities Tthhetrsatiliorcy ignirl&gt;cf t(Y);l1d1 b Onyly know their vane. g the tigioersys7l:: plausible enough. , e pawnbroker felt no hesitation in takin tshwee el NI ayaes The . what considerable sunA asked. When the customer hiti driven off and the pawnbroker was about to lock the packet into his safe it occurred to him as ,being somewhat curious that the stones sho)uld be wnapped -up jewellers' fashion in jon'el.lers' paper. This trade touch, slight in its2lf, was quite at variance with the story Johnsoni had told. Ile thon.ght it over with the, result that when Mr. ,JAnson dashed up again a few days later with a few other similar stones ha had discovered in the secret drawer of a desk left him by a deceased aunt, there was some little delay caused by a question as to the genuineness of a certain emerald. The stone was submitted to a neighbouring expert, a simewhat nondescript keen-eyed man, wear- ing a light overcoat and bowler hat, who walked briskly into the shop, and straight- Way proposed to waive the question of the st,,ne a genuineness until the pledger should have taken a drive with him to the nearest police station. There two hours "later Johnson was confronted with the head of the Bond Street firm, and the game ended, as such ventures usually d), being played out at Po- lice Court and Old Bailey where the plunger rose a loser to the tune of twelve months im- prisonment. At first, as I have said; I was not absolute- ly certain as to the identity of the prisener with my landlord's son. Everything tallied except the Christian name, but as the case went through its sad stages all doubt left my mind. Had any lingered, the mute distress of the old couple under whose roof I lodged would have dispelled it. They avoided me as much as possible., Johnson never mentioned "my son now, no hansom waited at the Or, and once or twice when I passed Mrs.</p>
