      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>COMPLETE EXAMMER STORY.</head>
        <p>COMPLETE EXAMMER STORY.</p>
        <p>CALL RIGJITS RESEIZVED.) THE PATH OF THORNS.</p>
        <p>SIR WILLIAM MAGNAY (Author of " The Pride of Life," "The Master Spirit*" etc.).</p>
        <p>The tall, gi:od-looking young -man who used to drive up occasionally in a smart hansom to our door and keep the cab waiting for an hour or two until he was ready to return to town, to the wonder and admira- tion of the district of Gainsborough Road, , Hampstead, was !fly landlord's son. In ap- pearance and grooming he was almost irre- proachable. I met him once walking in Picea- -. dilly with such an implrtant air that I said to myself, " If I did not know otherwise, Mr. Johnson, I might set -you down as a Tory Under-&amp;icretary of State." Neither his father nor mother in our frequent chats ever told , me what he was or how he earned the where,- withal for these artistic frock coats and reck- less cab hires. AU the information they 'ever vouchsafed me on the subject was that their son occupied a high pooition of trust in The West End; which; alter all, was not incom- patible with my idea of the Tory Under-Sec- retaryship. Ile had evidently forbidden his parents to enter into details, and they al- lowed their intense admiration for him, his varnished b)ots, his glossy hats—l shall never forget the looks ot rapture with which they used to see, him swagger into his hail- earn and bowl away—to overcome their nat- ural garrulity. My son, sir," the- old man weu:d say, "always goes to Rutland and Hablett for his hats." Or, This coat lam wearing, sir, was built by Sturts ,of Conduit Street; one of my son's, ;that he got tired of. .. Young'lnen like a change." Or ' I'll ask. my son, air, next time I see him what polish Dobb recom- mends for patent leather." But he, always stopped short at the" sub- ject of this glorious young man s vocation. One day, however, Mrs. Jofinsbn let out tnat her son was engaged to be married to a young lady who lived at Claphapi Common. day or two afterwards I ventured to ex- press to the old man the interest I felt in the news. He was very guarded. "My 500,-sir, lives a very different life from ours. I like to see a young man get on in the world. and hold Ilia beAd*llie.i. A Ina n</p>
      </div>
