      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>ERRYWEATHERS'</head>
        <p>ERRYWEATHERS'</p>
        <p>4:9 4:0 9:9 9:9 •:• 11:9 9:4 9:4* 4:1, 9:9 9:9 9:9 4:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:19 9:9 9:0 1:0 9:9 9:9 4:9 0:4 9:41 4:40 9:99:9 9:9 9:9 0:4 9:9 .:.,:4 4:4 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:0 9:0 9:0 4:9 9:9 9:* 9:9 9:9 4:9 49:9 9:9 9:9 9:0 9:9 9:9 0:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 4:9 9:9 9:9 9:4 4:9 9:4 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:11 9:9 9:9 9:4 9:9 0:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:11 4:9 9:* 9:4 9:9 9:4 11:4 9:4 9:9 44 9:9 9:9 9:9 ':9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:4 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:4 9:4 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 9:9 WO 4:4 9:4 19.9 9.19 9:9 9:9 0:9 9:4 'lf 5 5 .:..:. .:...:. - .).:. , +4. .... e. . .. WORLD. .:..:. .:..:. ~..:. _ .:. .:,.</p>
        <p>• • • • • •</p>
        <p>, The internal decorations, and appointments of the house are most beautiful and in perfect .taste, yet Sir. Hubert began his career a poor e. n d obScUre man,'and painted his first _success .whenin-poverty and Sorrow. His masterpiece, " The Last Muster," brought him European fame and renown; in addition to a fortune.</p>
        <p>there is an appreciable increase in width round the waist and to the hips. By means of the Jounce, narrow skirts may be brought up to date, and given the semblance of greater full- ness. Plain taffetas frills are shown on figured silk skirts, the colour either matching the ground or the predominating colour of the de- 31gn. In ;some instances, the flounces are of black taffetas, even on coloured silk, and the effect is excellent. Festooned flounces overlap- ping each other are also seen at the hem of some of the new models. Black on green is one of the prettiest of combinations, and will be a favour- ite this summer. Black and white is as popu- lar as ever, and there' is every probability that it will remain in vogue throughout the spring and following season. Much ,black, with, filmy white lace or net on the corsage and sleeves, and touches of the same on skirts, is seen in London and Paris, where well-dressed women congregate in the afternoon and evening... Prevalence of Flat Foot.</p>
        <p>Flat-foot, or that condition of the feet which makes walking a penance, and standing an im- possibility for any length of time, is raid to be alarmingly on the increase among children and women. In the case of the former, the doctors assert that the fancy dancing children are now so generally taught is to blame, for part of the training consists of various foot exercises that necessitates the turning out of the toes in a very exaggerated fashion. A habit of throw- ing out the feet too widely when walking or running is thus acquired, and this induces flat- foot. In women the tight skirt is largely re- sponsible for the infliction, for in order to walk the feet and the toes have to In turned out too much. Flat-foot is exceedingly painful, and always affects the general health, because it makes walking impossible, throws the spine out of place, and in some cases is the cause of curvature.</p>
        <p>Fashionable Dogs. Fickleness is a characteristic of fashions of all kinds, and as theAor, breeder is well, aware, nothing is more liable to change than fashions in dogs. One season the Porn reigns, the next he is superseded by the Pekingese. The pug at one time, and the poodle at another, have com- manded fancy prices, and carried all before them. A very lovely dog is the little Maltese terrier, and in order to bring it back to favour, a club has been started, .With Lady Morshead fy president, and other well-known "doggie" ladies as vice-presidents, who intend to carefully watch over the interests of this, the oldest toy dog in the world, which from neglect is threat- ened with extinction. Although only a year old, the club has already interested numbers of people in this charming little animal, that is almost as pure in breed to-day as it was in the days of Aristotle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>The Girl Guides.</p>
        <p>The Girl Guide Movement is second only to that of the Boy Scouts, in being copied in for- eign countries. It has extended to Norway, Sweden, France, Belgium, besides other coun- tries, and this week a smart little party of fifteen Girl Guides from Germany has been en- tertained by the heads of the organisation in London, and shown round the leading sights of the Metropolis. The Girl Guide movement is everywhere gathering recruits of its own accord and its rapidly growing vogue throughout the civilised world is a sufficient proof of its value. Although it is in the main an ambulance corps, the whole spirit of the movement is a domestic one. The girls are taught to be helpful to others in various ways, but they get badge 3 for cooking, needlework, nursing, laundering, dairy work, and so on, and there is nothing in the training that can be objected to, on the score that it is unfeminine. Nor is there any class ,distinction in the Girl Guide movement. it has been taken up by many of the big Schools, and the Guides come from the humblest homes, the houses of the. well-to-do, and the families of the aristocracy. The only thing wantimi• is more "girl officers." Thousands of Guides are waiting to be enrolled, and there is everywhere , an excellent chance for energetic young Women with plenty of leisure, to become leaders in this most Useful and active work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Versatile Personality.</head>
        <p>A Versatile Personality.</p>
        <p>Perhaps no man ever had a greater number of hobbies than the late Sir Hubert Herkomer. e was a painter, a teacher and composer of music, an actor, a cinema actor, a stage scenic artist, a film-maker, a lecturer, an etcher, ti player of the zither, and an architect. He de- signed his remarkable residence at Bushey en- tirely himself. , Its gates swing open automatic- ally, setting, as they do so, an organ playing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>NIEMS. FOR AI(YIIIERS. Tempting An Invalid's Appetite.</head>
        <p>NIEMS. FOR AI(YIIIERS. Tempting An Invalid's Appetite.</p>
        <p>Occasionally in the healthiest hinnies there is • a• sick child, and the nursing of it through the convalescent stage is quite an art. Particular. care should be shown in regard to the food. Anything to tempt the appetite may be chosen, but, if carelessly cooked; and'placed on a coarse - looking Plate, there will be 110 appetite or in- clination to take it.. If the eye is pleased, half the battle is won. Always Use thin glass and china, and if a tray is required let this be COV- .ered with a snowy, glossy cloth, or even one of those pretty white papey tray "cloths,'!. which are so dainty-looking and so cheap. ;: . _ - A few sandwiches (intended as a little stir- )rise) will be the more enjoyed if cut very thin, in unusual shapes, and. garnished with a sprig or two of parsley.: ,</p>
        <p>Pretty Knitted -Lace. ), ' A. lace, which makes a pretty edging, can be knitted as follows Cast on 33 stitches, and knit plain:</p>
        <p>First row—Knit 3, purl 22, oVer rthroad knit 2 together, over-knit 2 together, Over-knit 2 to- . • • gether, over-knit 2 plain. .Second row—Knit until 3 from end, and turn. Third row—Purl 23, ,overthread, Init 2 to,. gether, over-knit 2 together, over-knit 2 to- gether, over-knit 2. Fourth row—Knit until 3 from end, and turn: Fifth row—Knit 24, overthread, knit .2 to- gether, over-knit 2 together, over-knit 2 to- gether, over-knit 2: Sixth row—Knit 9, purl till 3 from eml,4ltu'rn. Seventh row—Knit 1, overthread, knit 2 to- gether, over-knit 2 together, _over-knit 2 to- gether, o'er-knit .2 together, till 2 from end of. row, over, and the last 2 plain. • Eighth row—Knit 9, purl. till 3 from end, last 3 plain.</p>
        <p>Ninth row—Knit all plain (when 37 stitches should be en), . . Tenth row—Cast off 4, knit plain till end. Begin at first row again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>)id Fashions Become New.</head>
        <p>)id Fashions Become New.</p>
        <p>That there is nothing new under the sun re- ceives verification every day. Few people even know of the exquisite treasures ia the shape of ancient jewellery that have from time to time found their way to the British Museum. But London jewellers display copies of some of these beautiful specimens of' ancient art, and label them "novelties." The latest thing in watches was inspired by an Egyptian jewel at least four thousand years old, and some shops are now dis- playing newly-made ornaments labelled "2,000 years old "! Nor is it only in jewellery that Dld fashions become new. There is no doubt that the display of dresses °of a bygone age, which is so niarked a feature of tho London Museum, will influence fashions this season and in the future.</p>
        <p>Fashions at the ,Albert Hall. Surely the importance a -dress is recognised now as never before in the history of our people, who are beginning to realise the truth of Bob Acre's dictum, that " dress does make a differ- ence." No pains are being spared to provide a liberal education on the subject by the pro- moters of the pageants lately held at the Albert Hall, and the thousands of women who were present will have no excuse for wearing un- becoming clothes, after witnessing such exquisite displays. Although a number of leading French arms exhibited some of the most beautiful taking part in the exhibi- gowns, it is gratifying toanote that dresses from the London houses, Lion were ill expression, taste and style, quite equal to the most celebrated French designers and couturieres.</p>
        <p>The Flounccd Skirt. The flounced skirt is the precursor of the fuller skirt. The tendency everywhere towards greater fullness is so very obvious, it would be foolish to ignore it. The newest models in the best houses still keep to the peg-top effect, but</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>COOKERY CORNER. _</head>
        <p>COOKERY CORNER. _</p>
        <p>Some Attractive Dishes.</p>
        <p>Here are scane very cheap nice little rock cakes which even a 'child could make. Rub two ounces of lard or dripping or butter into half a pound of flour, aod add two ounces of sugar, two ounces of currants, a teaspoonful of baking powder, pinch of salt. Mix with an egg and as little milk as you can use t'ci hold it .together. Have a ready 'greased tin and place in small rough heaps, hake in a quick oven for 15 to 20 minutes, according to Size. • A Rhubarb Pudding. - • .</p>
        <p>_ Put a sinall!eup in the centre of a pie-dish, and round it place plenty of rhubarb, cut small. Rub into 7 tablespoonfuls of flour 2 tablespoon fuls of butter,' and add 1 teaspoonful baking powder. Now break 2 eggs into a basin; add 4 tablespoonfuls of sugar, and beat well.. Stir in lhalf a pint of Milk, and pour into the flour, and mix. This miXiure should be just like that for a sandwich. Add a little sugar to the rhubarb; pour the mixture over, and bake in a good Oven for one hour. If a gas oven is used, it is best 25 minutes on the grid, the rest of the time on the plate with gas half-turned down: This is a very delicious recipe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Mutton Curry.</head>
        <p>Mutton Curry.</p>
        <p>Few people like cold mutton, but it may, bo made into an appetising. dish for tea: Cut cold roast or boiled mutton into dice; put.the hones over the fire with a little water and let them simmer for an' hour. Chop and fry one small onion in butter until it is a light brown; put in the meat and stir it about for a minute or two; add a teaspoonful of flour and a quarter of a teaspoonful of curry7powdel, and stir all well together; then: add about a teacupful of the broth made from the bones and half a teacupful of strained, stewed tomatoes. Let it simmer for about ten minutes; then pour it into the centre of a border of nicely steamed rice and serve at . , once. , Valk</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Mussels Cause Typhoid.</p>
        <p>"Here in Mancheste;," Dr. Niv:en said,. we we still suffer too much from typhoid fever. It is largely conveyed into Manchester. by means of mussels. I have no hesitation whatever in say- ing that. These Mussels come from places on the coast of Wales and the coast of Ireland chiefly. When we investigate cases of typhoid fever we find that a large proportion are to be traced to mussels. I should not be sorry to see the, mussel industry entirely obliterated. The price which we pay, for in my opinion, greater than its value. But if it 4to continue would strongly urge that no. mussels shogld be consumed in it raw state." Dirt and Bread.</p>
        <p>The .Board of Health of Erie; in Pennsylvania, has made what in this country would be called a by-law to the following effect "No bakeshop product shall be sold or offered for sale unless the same shall be closely' wrapped in a clean paper covering, which must be impervious to any pollution whatsoever from dust; flies, or any vermin, nor from the hands of any person or persons engaged in its sale, the wrapping to,. be done- at the shop or plant where the product is made, The fine for infringing this enactment may be from £1 to 120, or, in default of paying the fine, imprisonment for. a maximum term of thirty days. It is contended by the Erie Board of Health that not only does the wrapping • of bread not affect the loaf adversely, but it ensures that it shall be kept moist for a much longer period than when it is-exposed to the air. . About this season we shall have hard and dusty -roads; these tire the little ones more than most people realise, and after a two or three mile trudge home from school, as is often the case in the country, the. little feet should be bathed in warm 'water and fresh, cool socks-or stockings put on.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>Nagllk</head>
        <p>Nagllk</p>
        <p>NUM. k Mak NOM 'II:2:k &lt;AIM osas, Volk Ntassa !ask</p>
        <p>" Quite sure, sir. She keeps wyiting to him, •at • his lodgings, and -thinks be has thriwn her over. We are doing all we can tc keep the truth from her, poor girl." " It's cruel enough as it is," i murmured. It is bitterly cruel," she sobbed. " Still," I suggested sympathetically, "would it not, after all, be better that the girl should know the truth rather than ima- gine that her lover has deserted her ?" " Ah, that's what I think sometimes," the old lady answered bitterly. " But then there's the disgrace; we could not bear that she should know that." I tried gently to argue that out of con- sideration for the girl, to whim they owed it, a confession of the true state of the case could scarcely make matters -worse, wiel might soften the, bitterness she must reel. But. Mrs. Johnsm was firm in refusing to follow my suggestion. It was not for herself but .for her husband that she rejected ' the course; he would never bring himself to con- fess even c infidentially the disgrace and grief which was killing him. • " Yes, killing him as sure as we stand here," she insisted. " Luke won't cat ; •he thinks of our poor boy there on prison fare, and won't touch anything .4ut a piece of dry bread and a glass of water. And it's killing me, too,_ to see it."</p>
        <p>is I -felt in duty bound, I did all I con::a to comfort this poor couple. IC was true ettlugh that their misery. waa sapping the life from them. Beyond the mental .ailing, they were starving themselves in their bitter determination not to touch the smallest lux- ury in the way of food which would be denied the prisoner. The old man would not walk on the Heath; the contrast between its breezy expanse and the exercising yard being too toignant to. bear. He would sit for hours to- gether brooding, listless, nursing his aching mis..,ry, denying himself even the pleasure of a newspaper.. " I'll never look at one again," he said. " I've read the news once too. often." So things went on in their dull wretched- ness whica defied all p)wers of conso7ation, till one day the situation suddenly changed. I had come in late in the afternoon and soon became aware of an. abnormal stir in the. house. From the usually silent r)onis came th 3 sound of Voices, raised, it seemed to me, in tones ol distress azel entreAty. What might be going on was no business of mine; the old couple s natural grief was above all things-to be respected, to, wondering wheth- er a crisis in their trouVe had come, I rose and shut my door which had stood ajar. I had scarcely settled down to my book when there v.-as a sharp, impatient knock at the (Lor:•rs. Johnson came in with a troubled, excited face.</p>
        <p>" Oh, sir," she said tearfully, " Miss -Chal- men, our son's young lady, is here, and we don t know what to.. say to hpr. She insists on knowing what hug. happened, but we cant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>VOW 'OM VIM Na6o., vask vim Noon, NUM</head>
        <p>VOW 'OM VIM Na6o., vask vim Noon, NUM</p>
        <p>Johnson on the stairs her eyes were red. Time went on, sentence had been passed, and the silent misery of the o7d people had became so obvious that the sight oi their lonely strug- gle was more than 1 could bear. I knew that their grief mttst be all the more terrible from the fact that their pride seemed ti reject all sympathy and comfort from outside. Every day their faces seemed to get more drawn. and haggard. I had hoped that when the suspense of the trial was over they would have felt a certain relief, but when the worst Was kn.lwn there was no sign or rms. us course I was supposed to guess nothing of what had befallen them, yet as I was aware of the story it seemed horribly uncharitable and heartless to allow delicacy to prevent my offering a word, of comfort- to them in their bitter sorrow.. Still I hesitated, respecting their natural pride, until one day a chance remark of mine to the old lady seemed to knock away the last barrier of fortitude; she fairly br,-.lre down and sobb-cd. " I fear you have some trouble," I said gently, " about your son ?" For a while e..,he, could not answer. I spoke to her as tactfully and sympathetically as I could, begging her to let me know her grief and assuring her that she might safely con- t.&amp; in me. So at length she brought herself to admit the sad truth, but never his moral guilt, in- sisting, mother-like, upon his having been led away and made the scapegoat of others. It's just breaking- our hearts, sir," she sobbed. "lie 14-il;i the light of our eyes, and to think o 2 him now, my darling handsome oc see the life going cut of him, day by day."- . It's kill- ing boy, in prison, he that was •so active and loved to get about and see everything--oh, year, faatifhder, : a sir, it'll be his death, teo. Ile can never Ofli bear it for twelve long months, every one like, a -killritung h I gone. g h an I to'd her everything I cmid think of to cheer her, drawing on my imagination to make light of the rigours of gaol life. For the prisoner's health I had little' apprehen- sion; he was nit the sort of spark that is easily snuffed out, although I could not say So to the mother to whom he had brought s-trrow. There is the disgrace!" she moaned. _</p>
        <p>I cbjected, " who, besides our- selves, knows of it ? The first name was given differently 'in the papers. Sure:y your son is Cecil, not Charles ? " Ile has both names, sir. He was called Cecil after a gentleman in whose service we were, when we were first married and wh.) stood godfather to our boy. But in business he called himself Charles and kept Cecil for what he called the social side of his life. That's why his young lady don't knAv any- thing of what's happened to him 4" I started. Here was another branch of the trouble. " Are you sure of that ?"</p>
      </div>
