      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>ST. HELENS NON-UNIONIST MINERS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS NON-UNIONIST MINERS.</p>
        <p>At the Monthly meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Miners' Federation in the Caxton Hall, Salford, on Saturday, a deputation, in- eluding Mr. T. Creenall (president) and Mr. T. Ashton (secretary) was appointed to meet the general manager of one of the colleries nem St. Helens, with a view to obtaining facilities for dealing with men employed there who are not Members of the miners' organisaticm, and the Executive were empowered to take a strike ballot of the Men in the event of no arrange- ment being Made. Between 3,000 and 4,000 men are employed at the colliery.</p>
        <p>After a week's search the body of Richlird Gorton, a gardener, of .Aughton, Ormskirk, was found in a pit in a field at Meeling, near Liver- pool. The clothing and a razor were found close at hand. There was a superficial wound on the throat.</p>
      </div>
