        <p>telt her. It would be sucn a xinancss y)u would come mid Ba 7 a word." I rose at once, _g.ad to be of service. `-` She ought to kn w the truth," I said. " You must let .me tell her."</p>
        <p>6ne sank- uoNv' m covering her e. bing bitterly. The tinexpect-ed ei truth seemed to surprise the old I their dull misery. " Cecil was always generous," ther. " I'm sure he thought the 1 too good for you, my dear." "Au, that he would, ' Johns()) " All the same you can't blan my dear," Mrs. Johnson contin' though you might well have Won a man in his position got all from.''</p>
        <p>-• The o'.d lady's lip quivered, but she made no answer, just holding the door open for me. As I went into the sitting-room ,Johnson was sitting with bowed head, while before him stood a girl, flushed and tearful. She was stylishly dressed and decidedly pretty; be- yond mere looks there was "something in her face which told me she was a good sort; too good, I inwardly commented, for the man with whom she was in love. Mrs. Johnson in- troduced me. briefly. '• This gentleman will tell ym," she said. " We can't." At the words Johnson quickly raised his head. In his eyes was a pathetic look of fear. " No, no," he protested, but his wife laid her hand on his shoulder.</p>
        <p>"I might, but 1 was a though" the girl cried self-reproachfully. "The end was bound to Cc; Johnson said with the set look °' signation. " And now there it is. over. Poor Cecil, my poor b y. Miss Chalmers had dried her c,:e turned to her. " But it is not all over, Mrs. ~,he declared. " Not all over he'</p>
        <p>" Yes, Luk'?," she said gently, " it is for the bast," and he subsided again into his downcast attitude.</p>
        <p>The girl was staring at me in wonder and appreh(noion. Tier look was so pitiful that for a m nnent I had not the nerve to speak the words which must crash the trembling hope that remained. ' You know what has happened to my—to Mr. Cecil Johnson ?'' she demanded fearful. ly, and then as gmtly as I cou:d I tAd her the truth.</p>
        <p>and me." o ( The mother shook her head. must be," she insisted. "You calla° convicted felon, and Cecil, whatcir4 be, would be too honmrable to s 4 pect it." I'm sure of it, Mrs. Johnson, replied. " But there won't be for that. I shan't wait for him 1" " What ?" Mr. Johnson was r° his apathy. You would marry he—when be is free, a disgraced 111,°, lost his good Maine, his p ,sition, " He lost them for me," the gic• simply. " And I shall never give II; Jolinson tried to speak, but t found no utterance. His wife lils and cried.</p>
        <p>_ As I spoke, her face which had been flushed went deadly pain, then flushed again, her breath came hard, but she repressed her ex- citement and heard rne with scarce an inter- ruption to the end. I was puzzled as to what her grave silence indicated. As I finished the sad story Sirs. Johnson broke in with a pas- sionate excuse. "He was led away by others, my dear," she exclaimed. " He was always a good boy, and would never have--</p>
        <p>" I've got a little money of MY', Chalmers said quietly. "We 001' get along till he finds another I owe him that at any rate." • Thank God he'll have a Johnson exclaimed, between 3 thankfulness. "lie will indeed," I Intirulti turned to leave them. From that day Edith Chalnn: stantly with the afflicted couP` them and helping them to cal months of venally,. They ps6s- and one day Cecil Johnson C° changed sou. And a week after early morning I was presen,t when he and the faithful r Ile is now doing well in a own, and hi offence has Oblivion.</p>
        <p>The girl spoke, cutting short the protest. " Yes, Mrs. Johnson," she said with such quiet laitternosS that I concluded my sbry had effectually killed her love. " Cecil has been led away, led. on to do this, and shall 1' tell you by whom ? By me." Johnson raised his head quickly, but it WAS his wife who spike. " By you, my dear I" she questioned, at a loss. " Yes," the girl insisted. impetuously, " by ace. If Cecil, roor tellow, has taken what was not his, it is I who have wronged him by bo- ing a partner in his extravagance. If he able he stole for me; all the pleasures he gav2 rao, the theatres, the river-trips, the presents, the dinners, and a hundred other things, oh, I might have known it was wrong to take them, horribly wrong to keep him up in such expensive treats. He did it for me, every bit for me. How could he stop it when he knew how happy it all made me. Oh, if I had on!y known, only guessed what it must have meant. But I was a fool, and he is suffering for it, while I. miserable worthlesis thing—"•</p>
