      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>BAKER ONLY PROMINENT.</head>
        <p>BAKER ONLY PROMINENT.</p>
        <p>Baker was the only man in the threequarter line who showed any real knowledge of the finer arts of the game, and had' lie had anyone to back 'him up he might have altered the whole tone of the game. He has plenty of speed and with a good centre would make more use of it. In my opinion he is worthy of a place with the senior thirteen. Steele did not field the ball so accu- rately as he might have done, but made no mis- take in his tackling. The new players—Tabener and Nicholls—did not show that form with the juniors which it was anticipated they vc, ould, but it must be admitted they were opposed to a very smart pair in the Recs. right wing. At half, A. .Brown at times maintained his reputation with an exhibition of cleverness in dodging and feinting, but Dainteth did not play up to his usual form. At full-back 3. Brown showed all his old resource up to his injury, but Harmer, who deputised on his retirement, was not a great success in that position. The for- wards could not claim any advantage over the opposing sextet. Taken all round, the Recs. were too smart for our local lads, and in the threequarters showed the 'greatest superiority, and their passing in the last 20 minutes indicated a reserve of attack which, could they have put on the finishing touches, spelt heavy scoring.</p>
        <p>SCHOOLBOYS' CUP FINAL. The final in the South-West Lancashire School- boys' Cup Competition will be played on the Warrington ground next Saturday week, April 25. Kick-off 3.30. The finalists are Warrington and.'St. Helens.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKSHOPS COMPETITION. The Draw for the Second Round. April 11—Rylands' Wet Shop v. Greening's; Fletcher's M.H. v. Whitecross No. 1. April- 14—Waring's Tannery v. Firth Co.; Houghton's c;. Rylands' Netting. April 15—Welsby's Boot Factory v. L. and N.-W. or Monks Hall; Woolston Farmers v. C.L.C. April 16—Warrington Butchers v. P. and K. •(Bewsey); Richmond's Apprentices v. CrOsfield's (Cheshire). April 17—Crosfield's Shipping v. Orford Tan- nery ; Longford Wire Co. v. Crosfield's No. 1. April 20—Richmond's (Academy-street) or Al- mond's v. P. and K. Blackwatch; Fennel-street v. Warrington Tube Co. or Fletcher's xo. 1, April 21—Johnson's Skip Works v. Harding's; Whitecross West v. Richmond's (Horseferry). April 22—Rylands' Apprentices v. Forshaw's; Randall Bros. or St. Helens Cable v. Richmond's Foundry.</p>
        <p>RESULT OF MATCHES. Crosfield's No. 1, 13; P. and K. Wheel Shop, 0. Fletcher's MIL, 3; Rylands' Dalton Bank; 0. Owing to the shortness of time two matches of 40 minutes each are to be played each night in the competition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>RYLANDS' RECS. v. SALFORD "A."</head>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECS. v. SALFORD "A."</p>
        <p>- This return fixture was played on the Gorsey- lane ground.' Rylands' were short of Stretford and Mason. Rylands' at once commenced to press, Wild being tackled in possession. From the ensuing pack Brandwood scored for 'brands', the 'goal kick failing. The Salford forwards, ably led by Buckler, went into their opponents ding-dong, Whittaker having to be attended to on the line. Clifford, who was playing a grand game for Salford, opened the game out, but Gardner was pushed into touch. Knott scored from a forward rush. Bevan failed at goal. ilalf-time : Rylands', one try; Salford, one try. Bevan commenced the second half for Salford, and good play took the ball into the Wiremen't- quarters, where a series of scrummages were fought. Horrocks relieving. From a mistake on the part of one of Rylands' forwards, Salford scored. Again the goal kick failed. For ob- struction on the part of Salford, Warren kicked a goal. From a beautiful passing movement be- tween Gallop, Griffiths and Laws, the last player scored, Morris failing at goal. Salford again attacked, and Laws, in attempting to save, was injured and had to be carried off the field, Brand- wood taking his place. Nice play on the part of Clifford ended up with a pass to Hampson. and that player scored far out. Result: Sal- ford, three tries (nine points); Rylands', one goal two tries (eight points). Warren, Gallop, and Griffiths played well for the home team.</p>
        <p>. • Team for To-day. -Rylands' Recs. " A " meet Stittou at Gorsey- lane to-day with Potter (capt.); Griffiths, Hor- rocks (F.), llorrocks (A.), Morris; Gallop, Riley; Peers, Brandwood, Welsby, Slater, Broadhurst, Cooper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD DRAW.</p>
        <p>Latchford had a visit from Sale and the result was a 4-4 draw, which is a fair representation of the play. The home side were early on the attack, and from a centre by Knight the first goal was scored by Colton with a- good shot, and a few minutes after this Knight made a bril- liant run on the left touch, ran into the circle, and netted. Sale then had a spell of attacking, and Barnes and Mee put in some strong shots, but Brulkl kept them out. Barnes, however, Scored before the interval. After the change of ends, Sale pressed almost continuously for about ten minutes, and forced several corners. Robson equalised the scores and Mee bit the cross-bar with a splendid drive from one of the corner hits. -The same player got a third goal for Sale, and Barnes gave them the lead, but before time Minards and Pearson got through for Latchford. Jessop, liillkirk, Mee, Bishop and Robson were 'the' best a the Sale eleven, and Brill'', Wilkin- son and Albinson were prominent in defence for Latehfor&amp; The Latchford forwards combined "and shot better than ,they have done since "Christmas. Minards played a, strong game in the'centre. Dale and Pearson on he right wipg, and Knight "and Colton on the left, Were quite good, and, with a forward line such as luSt week, Latchford should be able to get goals. A holiday match will be played at 'Matlock this afternoon.' The annual meeting will be jie.,ld at Atkinson's Cafe on Saturday, April 18th, at '6.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>irtrb AND DELAGE ePiRs• p°4Eias, 'nudge, 8.5. A., Sunbeam, and Bradbury Motor Cycles. Agent:—W. KNIGHT, Stockton Heath Garage, °Dhorte 63. 109, LONDON ROAD.</p>
      </div>
