      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>POWDERS TO CURE DRINK HABIT.</head>
        <p>POWDERS TO CURE DRINK HABIT.</p>
        <p>' Remarkable Story at Runcorn.. - At Runcorn on Monday, Louisa Johnson, 26, Trinity-street, was granted a separation ordei against her husband, Alexander Johnson, on the ground of his persistent cruelty. It was stated that he had struck her several times, 'that the chief .cause of the trouble was drink, and that , the wife had sent for powders to try to cure her husband of taking drink. The Husband Where did 3..0u get the 1,071-&amp;-rs froniF—l• sent away for them. 7 The husband: .There were aboat i 3 of them in the packet, but they have, not cured me of drink. The Wife No. I don't think nythin4 v ill ever cure you. I have spent nearly Xl3O to try to cure you of the drink. The wife was granted 103. per w..ek, nod is to have the custody of the children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH'S MATCH FOR CHARITY.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH'S MATCH FOR CHARITY.</p>
        <p>The signs of the times in the local football world are that the close season is near at hand, and probably following the Easter holidays the fixtures will be few that remain to be fulfilled. Some of the clubs have completed their League while have eu pal already ef rdiyentdelrymfioni: ieiarndleaasree tor); ground and closed their sia:rrington and District League the fixtures,aa account ce termin- ated litheir t s of both divisions are decio the season. ct 11 lilftennihptolillotednf oas.whry , Great Sankey and Warrington Excelsior heading their respective divisions, and last week there were no fixtures in the First Division, while there were only two in the Second Division. The Orford St. John's could do no better than play a drawn game against the Bank Quay Recs., although they missed 'scoring from a penalty just before time, and thus they had to be satis- fied with a division of points. Emmanuel Old 13eYs entertained Fairfield in Victoria Park in their return League and after a keen and even display in half, ultimately ran out easy winners at et'lni re e Mission, and the tiefitrresed,t athe close of play. In the Minor Medal Competition, Longford United, after a keen and well contested game gained the verdict b thus they now take part in the semi-final tie against St. Barnabas' to-day on the Orford St. John's ground at Long- lane.with</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH CHARITY MATCH. The committee and members of the Stockton Heath Club are to be complimented on their efforts which are being made to help the funds of the Stockton Heath Nursing Association by arranging a friendly fixture with the Orford St. John's Club for Easter Monday morning. The kick off is to take place at 10.30, when Mr. J. H. Clark, of Orappenhall-road, has promised to set the ball rolling. The objects of this Associa- tion are well known, and last year it is estimated that close upon 1,000 cases were attended to in the village. Last year a similar match realised the sum of .03 10s., and it is fully expected that this amount will be easily passed at this match on Easter Monday morning,</p>
        <p>By " Linesman .7,</p>
        <p>works team now redoubled their efforts, and they quickly regained the lead, whilst H. Ditchfield shot over the bar from a penalty kick. The in- terval arrived with the score in favour of the works team by two goals to one. In the second portion the game was contested at a fast pace, and Bond was called upon to clear his lines. End-to-end play followed, the backs on both sides tackling well, but Green- ing's were the more effective, and registered a third goal.. The home players failed ttv add to their score and the result was: Greening's, three goals; Great Sankey, one goal.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD WORKS COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Forge v. Richmond's Lads. These teams met in the above competition on Saturday to replay their drawn game, and after a hard and strenuous struggle the Forge repre- sentatives won by one goal to none.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Second Division.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Second Division.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Old Boys V. Fairfield United. In Victoria Park. Flatton winning the toss for the Old Boys, Gregory set the ball rolling, and the game was keenly contested. The United got to close quarters and their forwards missed several good opportunities. The home defence now had some relief, their half-backs, improving in their play, but the visitors' forwards com- bined well, and Gregory, seizing an opening, scored the first goal for the United. This re- verse made the Old Boys exert themselves, and they invaded the United's territory. Rowlands both kicked and tackled very well and was well supported by Gregory. Still the homesters could net be driven back, and Brooke equalised. Both teams now played hard to gain the lead, but the home team preyed the smarter lot, and Isher- wood scored the second goal for the Old Boys, and they retained their lead at the interval. On resuming the Boys advanced on the visit- ors' goal, and following exciting play, Gregory cleared by kicking into touch. The home team now appeared to have got the measure of their opponents, and play was confined chiefly in the visitors' half. Wainwright got the third goal for the Boys. The United now woke up, made a strong effort to reduce the arrears, and made a rush on the home goal, where good chances were misled by wild shooting. Continuing to have the best of play, Short made a fine individual run along the line, and centred right in front of goal, where a splendid chance was mulled. The home backs cleared and Millington broke away, and scored a fourth goal. The visitors' chances Of scoring seemed hopeless and even play fol- lowed. When the final "thistle blew the result was: Emmanuel Old Boys, four goals; Fairfield United, one goal.</p>
        <p>Orford St, John's v. Bank Quay .Recs. .. These teams met in their return League fix- ture at Long-lane ground, under splendid condi- tions. The home team won the toss, and a very' fast pace was set. The sides being equally matched, the game was keenly contested, and both goals were visited in turn, and each side missed dton red what t appearedthey p onl to 0 liet easy chances. in of this half for the visitors. The second portion of the play was just as keenly contested, both teams fighting hard to gain the points in order to top the other in the League chart. Tickle was successful in equalis- ing the score with a beautiful shot. Both now being level, fought in ding-dong style, with the homesters showing the more finished style. As the end was drawing near they set up a warm attack on the Recs.' goal and gained a penalty for foul charging. To the dismay of their sup- porters, this was not improved upon, and when time arrived the result was: Orford St. John's, one goal; Bank Quay Bees., one goal.</p>
        <p>MINOR MEDAL COMPETITION. . Longford United v. St. George's Mission. On the ground behind the Horse and Jockey Inn, when the following players turned out :—• Longford United: Barber; Holden and Mosley; Lea, Finch and Darbyshire; Finch, Darbyshire, Sherratt, Holloway-and. Ifelsby. St, George,6 Mission:aßnc.dbeZre'd retln; Thompson,ilYandVal E an cia; GEM hien, dirionds, T. Hands, S. Hands and Brown. Referee, H. II owell. Sherratt kicked off for the home team; and Helsby, receiving a long pass, overran the ball, Livesay relieving with a timely clearance and setting his forwards going. They outwitted the home defence, Brown finishing with a weak shot, which was got away. The game was transferred to the home end, and Holden and Mosley de- fendedof finely, while the Brothers Hands sent in rth Loseveral good shots. Then Sherratt received the ball in his own half, beat several opponents, 11 and placed to Helshy, who was in a good Longford.: This raelisedirtsoheres h, ad a opened soon marked iita beautiful centre, from which Brown equalised. Half-time arrived with the scores one goal each. On restarting Hands opened out the game with long passes, and the visitors' forwards set up a warm attack on the 'home goal. A penalty was given against the home team, from which S. Rande shot wide of the mark. Even play in mid-field followed, and the opposing half-backs defended well, until Holloway . sent in a shot which Roberts made- a poor attempt to save, and the ball went over the line and a goal was counted to the United.. The Mission now tried ;mad to equalise, but failed to get on equal terms, and when tbe final whistle went the re- sult was: • Longford United, two goals; St. George's Mission, one goal. Roberts. apart from his mistake at the time the second goal was scored, did well, as also did Valancia. Livesay and T. Hands for the Mission, and the United were best served by Barber, Holden, Mosley, Eitel; and Sherratt.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY FIXTURES. Great Sankey v. Greening's.</p>
        <p>A friendly fixture between the Itb-ove teams was played on the Penketh Recreation Ground. The home club ouly fielded- a scratch team, being -short of Po-alter, J. Ditehfield, Bond and Gardner, the two latter players assisting the works team, in winch Bond gave a very . good exhibition between the posts. After the opening 'exchanges Greening's were awarded. a free kick', from which .they :opened, their account. :The homesters played bard to get on equal terms', which they did by means of a free kick just out- side the penalty area, 11. Ditchfield scoring. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>Naylor's v. Union Tanning Co.</head>
        <p>Naylor's v. Union Tanning Co.</p>
        <p>The above teams met last Saturday, and after a good first half the Woodworkers were leading by two goals to nil. During the second half Naylor's had the most of the game, and added further goals to the score, the result being: Nay- lor's, six goals; Union Tanning Co., nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>Crosfield's No. 1 v. Wilderspool. Brewery.</head>
        <p>Crosfield's No. 1 v. Wilderspool. Brewery.</p>
        <p>The meeting of these teams proved a one-side;., affair, and Crosfield's ran out easy winners by six goals to none.</p>
        <p>HONOUR FOR GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>_ The success which has attended the efforts of the players and working commiltee of the Great Sankey Football Club with respect to the honours they have gained upon the playing field, when last season they won the Widnes Alliance League and this season they head the Warrington and District League; lias- given them a reputation of which they ought to be justly proud. As a result of their successes be- coming widely known, Mr. B; Clarke, the lion. secretary, has received an invitation from the Denbigh Town Club to play a friendly fixture on their ground to-day, the visitors having been granted a satisfactory guarantee for travelling expenses. The Denbigh Town Club are mem- bers of the North Wales League. and occupy a very good position, and are holders of several trophies. The Great Sankey team will lack the services of roulter, as he is going to his home for the holidays. - The team will probably be as follows :—Bona ; Glover and Stephens; H. Ditchffeld-, Gardner, and Payne; Darlington, Illidge, J. Ditchfield, Banner and Houghton. The team are travel- ling by train, leaving Bank Quay 9.5- Mr. Clarke, the .secretary, has made all arrange. ments in his usual satisfactory manner.</p>
        <p>MINOR MEDAL COMPETITION. Draw for the Semi-Final. At a committee meeting held at the Lion Hotel, there was only a small agenda of busi- ness to decide, as up to the present all the games in the competition have been satisfactorily dealt with. The chief item on the agenda was the draw for the semi-final ties and the selection of grounds to be played upon, with the following result:— Longford United v. S. Barnabas', at Long- lane; referee, J. Smith. Orford St. John's v. Emmanuel Old Boys, probably at Stockton Heath; referee, S. Breeze.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION TEAMS. Elmwood-avenue team against Warrington C.L.C. will be: Phoenix; Clayton and Hall; Hazeldine, Phoenix and Dutton; Phoenix, Hunt, Gleave, Ellam and Hughes. Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's meet Stockton Heath, at Stockton Heath., with: Shakeshaft ; Evans and Peters; Hankey, Evans and Parn. Woodcock, Ford, Richards, Starkey and Howells. Emmanuel Institute meet Paddington Albion at Victoria Park, with: Knight; S. Hughes and A. Hughes; Broadhurst, Banner and Ayres: Walter Eaton, Oakes, Roberts; T. Hughes and W. Eaton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>FACE ITCHED AND BURIED.</head>
        <p>FACE ITCHED AND BURIED.</p>
        <p>- ---- - - --- "Five years I had suffered intense pain from little red spots on the right side of the face. It Station-road Collumpton, started at first with iust one solitary spot until Devon, Eng.— at last it spread until it nearly covered one side on my face. It itched and burned so much that 1 I would have given anything to get rid of it. When I scratched it the more inflamed it be- came. Occasionally these spots would break and discharge matter which only made it worse and caused it to spread more. • "For two weeks only I Used the enticura Soap and Ointment three times arday. I bathed the affected parts with warm water and then washed them with Cuticura Soap and then applied the Cuticura Ointment whicli gave immediate relief, taking all the burning and itching feeling away. The Cuticura soap and Ointment cured me." (Signed) Miss Penner, February 13, 1913. Cuti- rse:Pl le. Newbery and Sons. n, of each With 32_p. book ' cura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists cpl,o- estr y fv,r- leie e.r e .AdAdss sample Charterhouse-square, London. or Potter Drug and Chem. Corp., Boston, U.S.A.</p>
        <p>NARCOTIC BULLETS. • Drugged bullets are to be introduced to Polar bears by hunters, Whose object is not to kill, but capture, so that the victims may be taken" to the Panama-Pacific Exhibition, to live happily for ever while children feed them with buns. The animals to be hunted by this method (says the Standard ") are the great Kadiak bears of Alaska, and it is claimed that any beast receiv- ing the slightest wound from a drugged bullet can be caught without danger. An expedition Hun peculiarly armed has left for the Empire of Ice. Mr. Alexander F. Humphrey, a New York gunmaker, is the inventor of the narcotic) bullet. liadiak Island lies in the North Pacific Ocean,. east of the Alaska peninsula, and its bears are said to be peculiarly fat:miens. When the bear is struck he may be fierce for a few moments, but gradually he goes to sleep—and wakes a powerless prisoner, muzzled, and con- signed in quite an undignified fashion to Panama in a crate.</p>
      </div>
