      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>Items Dealt With by Class Members.</head>
        <p>Items Dealt With by Class Members.</p>
        <p>At the Sankey Bridges Men's Class on Sunday morning Councillor W. 11. J. Jenkins gave a lecturette entitled "A Working Man's Holiday." In his own inimitable way Mr. Jenkins told how the *members of a Fishing Club, by sacrificing themselves, enabled a fellow-member who war., " down on his luck" to gratify the wish of his sick child by getting some fish out of a stream where the child had often sat by his side and , watched him, when times were brighter. In the 'afternoon the usual fortnightly meet- ing of the Current Events Class was, in the un- avoidable absence of Mr. W. J. Rowland, ad- ' dressed by Mr. W. A. C. Landon, on " Citizen- , The speaker treated the subject undei ' the three headings: (1) Historical, (2) Philoso- phical, and (3) Practical. The conception of citizenship in the ancient and modern world was very different. In the former it was considered a privilege; in the latter a duty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>BEWSEY MEN'S.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY MEN'S.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edwin Wright gave a lecturette on here- dity on Sunday morning to a large attendance. He pointed out that in recent years biological science had done much to bring the question of heredity to the front, and to dispel many of the mistaken ideas about the transmission of many diseases.</p>
        <p>• LONGFORD MEN'S. Since 'entering upon their new premises in West-street. this School is making splendid pro- gress. On Sunday morning Mr. Frank Jenkins, of Stockton Heath School, gave a lecturette on "Memory," basing his remarks upon his early life in Buckinghamshire. On Monday evening a meeting was held under the chairmanship of Mrs. J. A. Ransome, who was supported by Mrs. Simpson and several members of Bewsey Women's School. The Secretary gave a brief outline of the Adnit School movement, especially referring to women's schools in the county. After Mesdames Ransame and Simpson had addressed the meet- ing, several questions were asked by women present regarding the working details of the school.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Simpson moved a resolution that a Women's School be started in Longford on Sun- day, the 12th of April. This was seconded by Mrs. J. A. Bradley. Several present promised to me members of the new school. Mr. Brad- ley was elected secretary pro. tern. The first meeting of the school will be on the 12th of April at 3 p.m.</p>
        <p>become</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>CYCLING CLUB.</head>
        <p>CYCLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Adult School Cycle Club, with the present membership of about 30, had their opening run on Saturday. There was a good muster, including many new members Although the weather was threatening a very enjoyable circular 16-mile run in Cheshire ,stimulated the appetites of the members, as was evidenced at the headquarters, Academy-street, where a fine spread awaited their return. After tea the annual business'ineeting was held. The following officials were re-elected. President, Mr. J. A. Ransome; secretary and treasurer, Mr. B. A. Webb; captain, Mr. A. Walker; sub-cap- tains, Messrs. A. Cook and T. Church.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT AMATEUR BOWLING ASSOCIATION. Fixtures for 1914. The following fixtures-have been arranged (suhject to confirmation) for the present season:— ' Championship Coropetiiiorr: First round, 18th May; second round, 30th May; third round, 6th June; semi-final, 18th July; final, 22nd August. Consolation Competition: First round, 6th June; second round, 13th June; semi-final, 18th : July; final, 29th August. Handicap: June 13th, June 20th, June 27th, July 11th. Individual Merit: July 25th, August let, August Bth, August 15th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>WIDNES SOAPWORKS' FATALITY.</head>
        <p>WIDNES SOAPWORKS' FATALITY.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at Widnes on Morfay on James Cawdor-street, Runcorn, who met Bwriotwhna(24sll). 3ocling death at Messrs. Gos.sage's Soapvk orks. lie - was employed in the boxmaking department, and got entangled in w the shafting. When the body was extricated it was badly mutilated. A foreman, John Dodd, said he could not understand how the accident happened. He had been in the box department for nineteen years, and this was the first acci- dent. ' The Government Inspector said- the machinery wo4 properly fenced. He was of opinion that rizawy young men, desirous of becoming .better , acquainted with the machinery, placed them- ;74ielves in danger. It was intended, outside any legal obligation, to ,put up an extra guard. A verdict of " Accidental death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
