      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>EXCELLENT HIPPODROME FARE.</head>
        <p>EXCELLENT HIPPODROME FARE.</p>
        <p>The attractions at the Hippodrorae'this week are so great that all lovers of a delightful vaude- ville show are more than highly pleased. Rinaldo pays his first visit, and it is to be hoped it is not his last. He is a clever violinist and literally makes the instrument speak. With. his somewhat eccentric number he convulses the audience with laughter. There is nothing orthodox in the style adopted by Will Gardner. He gives his impressions of how favourite music hall artistes would sing ragtime and gives 'extracts" from Shakespeare's plays in rag- time " to show how much more pleasant they would be sung in rag-time melody." His "show" is clean, witty and highly diverting. The burlesque "Dick Whittington," presented by the Whittakers, is excruciatingly funny, and the antics of "the cat" cause much laughter. The Miller Sutcliffes indulge in Highland sports, to the delight of Trio sing and dance well; Jennie Reeve is a dashing comedietnhnee,aaunddienTciei. T The vigloieranCtloayTmroitoe, Dutch acrobats, gymnasts, and teeth per- appreciated. tf oeurgs present a neat turn, which is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>HIPPODROME ATTRACTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE HIPPODROME ATTRACTIONS.</p>
        <p>—z-- A most excellent bill is posted for the Hippo- drome for holiday week, when the leading turn will be provided by Reginald Davies and Corn-1 pany in a sketch entitled "Navy Blue." Gra- ham, the human marionette, and his star artistes' revue should please, as should The Gillettes in their great success, " The Crazy Postman." Other good turns will be Vere and Neldon, in burlesque; The Dennisons, in novel songs and dances; Baker and Balfe, xylophon- ists and eccentric dancers; Monty Walker, comedian, and the Hippodrome Perfection Pic- tures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>A fine holiday Programme will be presented at the New Picture House next week, when on Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday " The Secret of the Black Mask" will be shown, and for the remainder of the. week "War of the Lillipu- tians" will receive chief attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>At this house, "Detective Finn," "A Muddy Romance," and "Adross our Alley" are amongst the pictures to be shown on Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday, and "Cross v. Crescent," "His Majesty the Baby," and "Jerry's Ma-in-law" are three of the attractions for the rest of the week...!</p>
        <p>Preachers for To-Morrow. Cairo-street Chapel—Morning and evening, Rev. Stanley A. Mellor, Legh-street Primitive Methodist—Morning and evening, Rev. A. J. Chessum. Bewsey-road • Wesleyan—Morning, Rev- H. Middlewood; evening, Rev. W. A. Beckett. Bold-street Wesleyan—Morning, Rev. E. Mor- gan; evening, Rev. H. Williamson. Christadelphian Meeting Room—Evening, Bible address by Mr. William Roberts, Birken- head.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational—Morning and even- ing, _Rev. T. T. James,</p>
        <p>Golborne-street Baptist—Morning and evening, Rev. IT. Ogden. . St. John's Presbyterian—Morning and even- ing, Rev. William Reid. Latchford Baptist—Morning and evening, Rev. J. S. Chedburn.</p>
      </div>
