      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>COMPULSORY SERVICE.</head>
        <p>COMPULSORY SERVICE.</p>
        <p>National Service Leaguers and the Need for National Effort.</p>
        <p>CAPTAIN GROSFIELD'S CHALLENGE.</p>
        <p>The men:triers of the Warrington Division of the National Service League 'dined on Monday evening in the dining room at Messrs. Cros- field's works. The function, an annual one, was largely attended, and speeches in favour of the scheme for national military service were deliv- ered. Capt. Crosfield threw out a challenge to Mr. Barry, of the Australian Anti-Military Association, who recently spoke at a pease meet- ing in Warrington, and offered to give £5O to the Warrington Works Bowling Association if Mr. Barry could prove one of the three chief points he had brought forward against national service was correct. Captain Crosfield presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>DEPUTATION TO THE PREMIER.</head>
        <p>DEPUTATION TO THE PREMIER.</p>
        <p>• Mr. -J. Eitviziz, in proposing -the—i;s—t- Of the President of the League, Lord Roberts, said he had the pleasure of meeting him a few weeks ago on a deputation to the Premier regarding the League's proposals. The composition of that deputation was of the broadest possible charac- ter-.--(hear, hear)—for amongst the twenty or , thirty representatives he found Army and Navy men, civil servants, employers and employed, and men who followed no trade at •allt The political character was quite as broad, for in- cluded in the number were men holding the views of Conservatives, Liberals, Labour and Socialist. They had an excellent address from Lord-Roberts on the necessity of their proposals, for the safety of the Empire demanded them, and there was another address calling attention to the fact that that country of the Empire could never be safe in its present condition. Other excellent speeches were also given by Sir Evelyn Woods, representative of the Admiralty, and Sir James Crichton-Browne. _ _ _</p>
        <p>In reply, the Premier, ibough indefinite in his statement, was very fair, and went as far as was possible for any Premier to go on any great question such as that. One of his statements was that he wished the defence of this coun- try was placed on a broader basis than at pros- sent, and that it should be spread over a broader area.</p>
        <p>Voluntary Effort Failed. He (Mr. Elwell) took that to mean that vol- untary defence had practically failed, because there was only a certain section of the commu- nity who did their duty in preparing for de- fence. No doubt others were willing, but they were placed in such a position that they were unable to do their share, because if they did, probably when they returned from their train- ing they would have to search for other employ- ment. Another statement by Mr. Asquith was the advantage derived from military training, but he qualified himself to a certain extent by saying that military training was not the only means of developing the individual. Another Point where Mr. Asquith was very good was at the conclusion of his speech, when he remarked that the deputation were not in any way discouraged because they did not get everything they wanted. A deputation of so representative a character should not fail to have a great influ- ence upon the whole of the community, and he trusted that when they went forth they would not be discouraged, but would do what they could. to be</p>
        <p>Although those are not the exact words of the Premier, said Mr. Elwell, I believe they were implied. It was as good a statement as we could expect from a man in the position of Mr. Asquith—(hear, hear)—and every member of the deputation was very well satisfied with the re- plies. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>"THE WARRINGTON DIVISION."</head>
        <p>"THE WARRINGTON DIVISION."</p>
        <p>Mr. H. TIIETON, a collier from Ashton-inMakerfield, in proposing the toast of the War- rington Division, said that if they did not look the question of home defence in the face and bring it to a head within a very short time, he was confident, they would land themselves into serious trouble. It was quite a non-political and non-party question—(hear, hear)—and it was the duty of the members of the League to see that their Bill was put before the people of the coun- try. He had no need to labour the question of the utter failure of the voluntary system. When they got men who had been the champions of the voluntary system turning round and telling them that the present system was inadequate, he thought it was sufficient for them. Lord Haldane himself had said that if they were to rely on the ,present Territorial Force for the defence of the country they were relying on very little. If they only obtained a part of the League's scheme—that part which stated that employers should not be allowed to penalise their workmen who went for training in camp— their League was well worth supporting. (Hear,• hear.)</p>
        <p>He was only an ordinary-ftommon Lancashire pitman, and when a catastrophe happened in the pit or on the railway one of the first things that was done was to hold-an inquiry into bow the accident happened, and how to prevent a recurrence in future. That was a proper course, but it would be too late for the country to make an inquiry after the catastrophe had happened. It was their duty to make the inquiry before- hand. They had men going about the country preaching the gospel of salvation for the world to -come, but they, the members of the N.S.L., had a gospel of salvation for the earth they knew they were upon, and they should preach the .gospel of salvation of the people while they were here. Their kith and kin in the colonies had seen the necessity for a good army; and it behoved the mother country to wake up to the. same fact. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>"AMAZING AND MOST ENCOURAGING." Capt. Citosristn voiced the regret of the League at the death of the local P Henry Ly on resident, Mr. James , one of the oldest inhabitants of the district, who became president of the local branch at its inception in 1903. During the past year the League held a series of meetings throughout the country, conducted by their splendid President, Field Marshal Earl Roberts. The earnestness and enthusiasm displayed on all sides were amazing and most encouraging: They held a great meeting in the Free Trade Hall, Manchester: on October 22nd, 1912. Four years prior to that date they held a meeting in the same building, at which Lord Roberts was also present, and they had the greatest difficulty in getting people in the hall, but at the meeting in 1912 they had the greatest difficulty in pre- venting the doors being smashed in. (Applause.) They had an overflow meeting, which itself over- flowed. The resolution put by himself was car- ried with only one dissentient, whilst at the overflow meeting , there were only three who voted against the League's proposals. The change was phenomenal. It could not be ascribed to the presence of Lord Roberts; be- cause he was also present at the meeting four years pTevious. They took it that there was a distinct change of opinion, that People were be- ginning to understand the subject much better, and that the value of the spadework which had been done in the district was beginning to show itself. During the two years prior to the latter meeting about GO meetings had been held in and around Manchester, and at these the helper's from Warrington (lid a good work. They had heard about the deputation which had inter- viewed Mr. Asquith. He thought it was almost a record to get an hour of his most Valuable time and he was of opinion that the Premier could not Possibly go further than he did, for he practically said, " YOji are doing good work, go on with it, -said prepre the country." (Ap- plause.)</p>
        <p>Defence of the Territorials. There' was one part of Mr. Turton's remarks which he did not quite agree with, and it Was when he touched upon the Territorials, and which might give a false impression. Until they had persuaded a very great. majority of the people of the country that it was right, proper, necessary and advantageous that they should have a citizen army somewhat on the lines they had in Australia and New Zealand, they must support their Territorials. Let them be clear. ;They knew the Territorial Force was not up to the mark, and really could not be, because of the limiting nature of. the conditions under which they served, namely, the voluntary system. Even then they were far and away better than no force at all. (Hear, hear.) Let them remember that until they had the country ready for their scheme, it was their duty as British men to back up the Territorials to the best of their ability, for if they did not do so, he thought they would lay themselves open to the .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>RELIGIOUS e-SOC/AL</head>
        <p>RELIGIOUS e-SOC/AL</p>
        <p>PARISH CHURCH OLD VESTRY.</p>
        <p>The restoration of the old vestry at the Parish Church has been completed, and the Rector, writing in the Parish Magazine, says: "The result is most satisfactory. The old vestry so called was never a part of the original fabric The eastern part was built as a porch to pro- tect the entrance into the chancel from the weather, and the western part was added to make a connection under cover into the nave. The restoration has been carried out in harmony with the new choir vestry on the north side, and reflects the greatest credit on the builder and masons. We have to thank the churchwardens and officers, too, as we do ;nost sincerely, fot their interest and help in furthering the scheme, which gives, we believe, the greatest satisfaction to everyone."</p>
        <p>the extent that it enabled men to gain a truer perspective of life, and thus, bring about a yearning after the higher things which were of benefit to themselves and their fellow-men.</p>
        <p>Amongst those present in th'e evening-were the Mayor and Mayoress (Councillor and Mrs. P. Peacock).</p>
        <p>The singing throughout the day was well sus- tained by the children and choir, who were ably assisted by an orchestra, Mr. P. Atkinson act- ing as conductor, while Mr. T. Tanner officiated at the organ.</p>
        <p>The collections for the day Aiounted to over £46.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>WORKING MEN'S MISSION. Bank Quay Anniversary Services.</head>
        <p>WORKING MEN'S MISSION. Bank Quay Anniversary Services.</p>
        <p>What was termed as the most successful anni- versary ever held in association with the WorlK- ing Men's Mission, Bank Quay, was that which took place on Sunday at the Parr Forty-three years have passed away since the first anniversary service, which was held in a building serving the pprposes of chapel and school, and 'Which stood on the ground now occupied by Crosfield's engineering works. Since that time great strides have been made, and to-day the number of scholars on the books is 947. It is worthy of note that the first super- intendent of the school was Mr. John Barlow. The preacher was the Rev. J. W. Lowe, of Sheffield. At the morning service, held in the Mission Chapel, the reverend gentleman took his text from St. Matthew a. 42, and spoke on the interest and appreciation which the Savioui had in the children.. His love for them was manifested in the fact that they were the nearest approach to the inhabitants of the kingdom from which He came in order to redeem man- kind. The child, said the preacher, was the embodiment of all that was pure and noble, and no matter how small the work the teacher per- formed in the endeavour to maintain these beautiful traits, it was blessed in an abundant manner. It was a noble service to be engaged In, and one worthy of the best efforts which the teacher could bring to bear upon it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>"KEEPING OF PROMISES."</head>
        <p>"KEEPING OF PROMISES."</p>
        <p>- - In the afternoon the service was held in the Parr Hall, the scholars Walking in procession from the school, and a large Congregation was presided over by Mrs. John CrosAhld, of Colwyn Bay, who came over to Warrington, although not in the best of health, to undertake this position. The speaker was Miss Jessie Reid Crosbie, of the Liverpool Primary Sunday School Union, who gave a very earnest and able address on "The Keeping of Promises." Speak- ing principally to the children, Miss Crosbie riveted their attention by the manner in which she illustrated her topic with short stories, thus showing to the adult portion of her audience the methods adopted by the primary schools in better enabling the children to grasp the mean- ing of the lessons taught in the Sunday schoOl. In connection with the Sunday school Union Scripture examination it had been decided to present a book to any of the Mission scholars who were successful in gaining prizes, and at an interval in the afternoon's proceedings Mr. T. Dale, senior superintendent; handed the awards to the following: Edward Tilling Gordon Rigby, Mabel Millington, and Frank Lowe. At the evening service the hall was crowded to its utmost capacity. The Rev. J. W. Lowe based his sermon on a text from Colossians iii. 2. He showed how the motive power of the best life of to-day was centred in Christ. There was a deep yearning in the souls of all men for God, yet, strange to say, their affections were not centred on the noblest things. Too much, to-day, was centred on self, whereas when men were brought into contact with Christ they were taught that they lived to love. More sacredness was needed in life and labour. This was brought about through true religion, which was not alto- gether a saving of souls and attending at public worship, but the transforming of character to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>STRETTON LADY'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>A STRETTON LADY'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>Popular Worker in Runcorn Union.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place on Wednesday at Strat- ton Church of the late Mrs. C. T. Whitley, of Wall's Pit House, Stretton, widow of the late Mr. Charles T. Whitley, architect and surveyor. The deceased, who had been ailing for about two months, was 62 years of age, was a member of the Ladies' Visiting Committee of the Run- corn Union, and at a meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians on Tuesday a vote of sym- pathy was passed to the relatives Mrs. ley took a great interest in all the parochial undertakings and was highly respected. The principal mourners at the funeral were': Dr. Bower (son-in-law) and Mrs. Bower, Miss Whitley (daughter), Miss Kendall, Mr. Hart, Miss Hart, Mr. and Mrs. Hugh Sadler, Miss Vera Hart, tlie Misses Sharpley and Billingsley, Miss Minta and Master T. Bower.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>COMPULSORY SERVICE—continued.</head>
        <p>COMPULSORY SERVICE—continued.</p>
        <p>charge of disloyalty. He entirely disagreed with those who said that the members of the Terri- torial Force should not criticise, for it was those inside the Force who best knew what the limit- ing conditions were, and if they did not say any- thing the man in the street would think all was I right, and he would not worry. Hear, hear.) I At last, after working for so many years, they had got the opposition into the field, and they were -holding anti-conscription meetings up and down the country. Their opponents referred to the League's propcals as " conscription." Mr. John Barry _had been to Warrington.</p>
        <p>A £5O Challenge.</p>
        <p>He had challenged Mr. Barry on three essen- tial points, and if that gentleman could prove even one of them correct he would give £5O to the Warrington Works Bowling Association. He made that challenge because if one had an op- ponent who was at all slippery, and who did not ! meet him fairly and squarely, he wanted him to come out; he therefore made that challenge so that Mr. Barry could meet him fairly. He did not say that the Australian compulsory service was perfect, and no doubt they had got to amend many points. Be believed that there was a cer- tain feeling springing up amongst a certain sec' - lion of Australians that the force should not be employed to put down strikes. They in the N.S.L. made it one of the cardinal points in their programme that their citizen ariny should not be used to quell strikes. (Hear, hear.) The opposition which they were now meeting was of the greatest encouragement, because it showed that their opponents were getting alarmed. Above all, if they could get, in the in- dustrial towns, men to work for the League as they did in Warrington, he was perfectly sure that it would not be long before the country had a Force on a really sound basis, and a really national footing. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>'ARTISTES AND PRIZE-WINNERS."</head>
        <p>'ARTISTES AND PRIZE-WINNERS."</p>
        <p>..... ----• Mr. B. S. TOWNROE proposed the health of the artistes and Shooting Committee, which was warmly received. 46. The following artistes provided a really good programme: Messrs. E. Walker, Sephton, W. J. Hopkins and C. H. Clarke (pianist), together with Mr. H. M. Ashton, who gave many humor- ous recitations.</p>
        <p>_ The prizes were presented by Capt. Crosfield to the following : Open Competition (possible score 70).—1, F. C. Bennion, 67; 2, J. Sankey, 66; 3, E. Pollard, 6.5; 4, E. Ball, 63; 5, R. W. D. Cook, 60; 6, A. Shep- herd, GO.</p>
        <p>Old Soldiers.-1, J. W. Plinston, 67; 2, T. 12. Ashworth, 61; 3, C. Shepley, 60. Mr. Plinston having won the cup for the third time in suc- cession, retains it. Civilians.-1, Hutclienson, 67; 2, S. James, 65; 3, C. P. Day, 62.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>BOLD-STREET MEN'S BIBLE CLASS.</head>
        <p>BOLD-STREET MEN'S BIBLE CLASS.</p>
        <p>Visit of the Mayor.</p>
        <p>Sunday was a special day in.the history of the Bold-street Men's Bible Class, the speaker on that occasion being the Mayor. The meeting, which was very largely attended, and held in the Church Room, included, amongst other pro- minent members of the church, Messrs. George Weaver, William Cooper and W. Isaac, trustees; also Mr. Edwin Plinston.</p>
        <p>The President (Mr. J. C. Holland) briefly sketched the history of the class. Its inception, he said, dated back to the days when Warring- ton Methodism was centred at Buttermarket- street. Many godly men had presided over their meetings, and succeeded in establishing an organisation which had proved to be a valuable training ground for the lay workers of the Church. He (Mr.+llolland) had followed one of Bold-street's most esteemed workers, and was pleased to pay his tribute of praise to the untiring zeal of the late Mr. W. C. Nicholson The numbers at that time had fallen off consid- erably; but despite the inevitable loss of mem- bers by reason of removals, the class to-day was in a vigorous and flourishing condition. The visit of the Mayor was gratifying for many reasons, and on behalf of the meeting he ex- tended to his Worship a very cordial welcome. The Mayor, who was very enthusiastically received, liaid his chief difficulty had been in the choice of a suitable subject, and pressure of engagements had prevented him bringing a Biblical subject: He had, however, prepared a paper on " The progress in transport in Eng_ land," and he hoped it would prove acceptable to the class. He referred to the state of the land in pre-Roman days and went on to illus.,l trate the great work which the Romans did in the question of road-making. The various stages of progress in road and river traffic were detailed and reference was made to the different legisla- tion which was undertaken in connection with these means of transit. The rise of canals and the wonderful invention of macadam gave a great impetus to trade generally, until, with the perfection of the steam engine and the con- struction of railways, England appeared to have reached the zenith of progression in the matter of locomotion. The many anomalies which existed in the fixing of freightage were very ably pointed out, and caused his Worship to remark that Parliament might be occupying its time better by regulating these tolls, and thus giving English trade a greater chance of expanding. What the possibilities of electricity and flying were he had not time to discuss, but doubtless these would be as far-reaching as the revolution which the railways had produced in travelling and transport. Mr, Stanley C. Jones (treasurer of the class) thanked the Mayor. The occasion was unique, in that this was the first time that the Chief Magistrate of the Borough had addressed their afternoon meeting. They were thankful that I Warrington had a Mayor who was ready to assist every agency that was at work to better the town generally'. Mr. William Isaac, in supporting, said that to his mind Councillor Peacock was one of the most energetic Mayors Warrington ever had. Every organisation which aimed at social better- ment received his active support regardless of creed or party.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Williamson also expressed his appreciation of the Mayor's visit, and referred to the vastness of the subject, both in regard to its social and commercial aspects, ansl also in reference to its religious side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>THAT DEPUTATION.</head>
        <p>THAT DEPUTATION.</p>
        <p>The Education Committee's London Trip Discussed in the Council.</p>
        <p>MORE MEMBERS SUGGESTED.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday the minutes of the Education Committee, in which it was proposed to send a deputation to interview the Board of Education, came under discussion. This was the subject which occa- sioned the recent scenes in the committee.</p>
        <p>- Councillor ARCHER, thought the Chairman of the Education Committee (Councillor Roberts) should withdraw the resolution so that the depu- tation might be strengthened by the inclusion of two members from the Council selected by the General Purposes Committee. Councillor ROBERTS thought the Education Committee would have no objection to the depu- tation being strengthened. He was certain the committee would agree to such a course. Councillor ARCHER said that promise satisfied him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>DELAYED, NOT WITHDRAWN.</head>
        <p>DELAYED, NOT WITHDRAWN.</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGE(: Is the motion about the deputation being withdrawn?</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Not withdrawn, only delayed to strengthen the deputation.. , Councillor GOUGH : I can't see why Councillor Pemberton has been struck off the deputation. The 31Avos, : The Education Committee is re- sponsible for that. • Councillor GOUGH : I move that the resolution be referred back, as a financial question is in- volved. The MAroa. : It is not a financial question. I think the promige Councillor Roberts has given ought to satisfy you. I shall rule it is not a financial question.</p>
        <p>Councillor Govou : But I can move this back. The MAYOR: No ! Councillor PEMBERTON : It involves the spend- ing of money.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: I rule you cannot deal with it. You should be satisfied with the promige of ,Councillor Roberts.</p>
        <p>"PUTTING THE SCREW ON." Councillor Go: I don't think we ought to be satisfied,</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: You can discuss it generally, but you cannot move it back. Councillor GOUGH It appears you are putting the screw on. (" Chair.") The MAYOR: I have given my decision, and I won't alter it for anyone. Councillor GOUGH : I suppose on the advice of the Town Clerk? The MAYOR: It is on my own responsibility. Councillor PEMBERTON thought the matter was of great importance to the ratepayers. Some- thing like .{116,000 more was to be spent than in 1904-5, or about is. 3d. in the X. The Council therefore should have the opportunity of ap- pointing two members on the deputation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>"LIKE PIE CRUSTS."</head>
        <p>"LIKE PIE CRUSTS."</p>
        <p>The Alma That has been promised. Councillor PEMBERTON : There are a great many things promised that are not carried out. I have been long enough in public life to know that such promises are like pie crusts—made to be broken. (Laughter.) Councillor ROBERTS: I will ask the Sites and Buildings Committee to defer any action in the matter until the General Purposes Committee have met. I hope my word is worth being taken. I think the Education Committee will acquiesce with any reasonable request put before them. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>• , The matter then ended and the minutes were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <p>LEGH STREET PRIM METHODISTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>Veteran Sundiy School intendent Honoure</head>
        <p>Veteran Sundiy School intendent Honoure</p>
        <p>Yesterday was a great day in ti the Legh-street Primitive Methoi School, for the photograph of Mr. sell, who has been the superinter school since 1868, was unveiled, main in the schoolroom. On eithe: photograph were diplomas beloni Bissell, one from the Primitive Me day School Union for 40 years' Berl other from the Primitive Metho School 30 years ago. .</p>
        <p>Old associations were renewed at scholars had come from different from as far as South Staffordshire, t veiling ceremony, which was perfo Rev. J. B. Buglass, no,w of. Man who was pastor of the church abet ago. Both he and the Rev. A. (pastor), who presided, spoke of done by Mr. Bissell and his regul to the work of the schools. Mr. Bissell, who is 72 years of ag up his attendance at the schools, are 230 scholars, as hon. superinten is succeeded in the office of super, Mr. B. T. Parkes, an energetic set The prizes for regular attendan conduct were distributed by Mr. J Neston, an old scholar.</p>
