        <p>PADGATE'S NEW VICAR. Rev. H. F. S. Collier, Southport, Accepts the Living. —7— The Rector of Warrington informs us that the Rev. ,Herman F. S. Collier, late organising secretary of the Waifs' and Strays' Society, and present Vicar of Banks, Southport, has accepted the benefice of Padgate, in succession to the late R. G. W. Dickinson, who was the Vicar at Padgate from 1908. The Rev. H. P. S. Collier was educated at Pembroke College, Cambridge, and obtained high degrees and 'University honours. He was ordained in 1905; and was curate at St. Ann's Church, Nottingham, in 1906-7.</p>
        <p>example of commercial success and progress that we could not do a more questionable or foolish thing than to ask him to take charge of our water undertaking, or of any other undertaking that required financial acumen and commercial knowledge. Councillor PEMBERTON : To a point of explana- tion I.was referring to the charge for a.thousand gallons, which is a rate. I did not mention any- thing at all about Mr. Gray's salary. Councillor ROBERTS: The explanation given that we charge a water rate per thousand gal- lons—well, it is a piece of piffle. The resolution was adopted unanimously.</p>
